WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:18.670
KAISER: Welcome family and
friends of the MIT graduating

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:23.245
Class of 2004.

00:00:23.245 --> 00:00:27.130
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:32.009
I am speaking to you up here
at the head of the commencement

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.060
stand.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.080
Can you all see me?

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.180
I'll wave.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.530
Can you see me?

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.880
Great.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.260
Hello.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.250
I think our picture is also
on the big television screen

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.420
over there, the jumbotron.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:54.300
My name is Jay Kaiser, and I'm
an emeritus professor at MIT.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.890
And this is what
emeritus professors do.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.010
We entertain you
until your graduates

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.820
come into this beautiful court.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:08.880
I'm joined by my colleague
Warren Seamans, who,

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.210
for 32 years, was
associated with MIT

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:19.190
and known, most of all, as
director of the MIT Museum.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:23.540
There's no one at MIT who
knows more about MIT lore

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:26.500
and history than Warren.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.710
We will be visiting with
you for the next hour or so

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.150
as we await the arrival
of the guests of honor--

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.000
your children.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.210
They are currently gathering
in the Johnson Athletic Center

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.460
on the west side of the
campus, and they should

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.965
be joining us at about 9:50.

00:01:49.616 --> 00:01:50.990
If you take a look
at the screen,

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.510
you can see them over in
the Johnson Athletic Center

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:58.170
now through the
magic of television.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.920
It is now 8:18.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.275
Please synchronize your watches.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.960
During this time, we will also
have some video pieces to show.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.330
So make sure that you have
a good view of the screen.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.460
And we'll share with you
a variety of perspectives

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:20.810
of the MIT experience
that the students had

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.260
when they were here.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.000
This is a gorgeous day.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.350
Rarely has there been
a day when I could say,

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:33.350
look up in the sky, and
you will not see a cloud.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:34.640
It's amazing.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.190
We have really been blessed
with this beautiful day,

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:41.980
and it's a beautiful
day for us and for you.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.085
We've been dodging rain.

00:02:43.085 --> 00:02:44.460
It rained yesterday.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:45.540
It rained the day before.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.930
But no rain today, and that
makes this commencement

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:53.190
setting a special one.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:58.080
The court has a rich
and interesting history.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.570
And as I said,
there are few people

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:02.960
who know more about that
history than my colleague Warren

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.440
Seamans, who will tell you
a little bit about the place

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:07.910
you're now sitting in.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.530
Warren?

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:11.690
SEAMANS: Welcome,
welcome, welcome.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:15.680
It is a great pleasure for me
to be here again this year.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.880
I think it's important
to know a little bit

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.170
about this holy center of MIT.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:29.450
88 years ago, in 1916,
this was dedicated

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:35.690
when this new campus was built
and dedicated June of 1916.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.810
Since that time, it has served
as really the Institute's

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:41.150
public living room--

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.640
everything from
graduation commencement

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.990
ceremonies through the
inauguration of new presidents

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:51.260
to a lot of social
activities, some of which

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.950
you will see on our
video screens today.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:04:02.870
Over the years, it has
served as a meeting place.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:05.330
Traditionally, over the
history of the place,

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:10.110
the first actual meeting of
the freshmen class coming in

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.010
was held here in--

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.730
it was known as the Great Court.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:17.339
In 1974, it was
renamed Killian Court

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:20.610
after James Rhyne
Killian, who had just

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:25.000
stepped down as chairman
of the MIT Corporation.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:33.300
This has served in many other
ways as a center for MIT life.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:39.200
In 1930, June 6 of
1930, Karl Compton

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.360
was inaugurated
president in this locale.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.620
At that day, in
comparison to today,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:53.230
it was 106 here in
the Killian Court.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.680
It's also interesting to note
that the ashes of at least

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.010
two MIT presidents have
been sprinkled or spread

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.050
in to the Great Court.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:06.754
So it is truly a
significant center of MIT.

00:05:06.754 --> 00:05:08.170
I thought it would
be interesting,

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:11.800
since we are here, to take
a look at the object that's

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.350
directly behind us on
the speaker's podium.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.870
That's the great seal of MIT.

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:18.940
You will notice
from looking at it

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:24.190
that it has a lot of
significance to the group

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.976
meeting today.

00:05:26.976 --> 00:05:31.980
1861 is the day of the actual
signing of the MIT charter.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:33.975
That would be 134 years--

00:05:33.975 --> 00:05:36.080
33 years ago--

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:47.470
143 years ago, and this has been
always part of the MIT story.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.650
Over the years, they've tried
to upgrade it, to change it,

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:55.660
but it always comes back to
use of the original MIT seal.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.060
"Mens et Manus,"
"Mind and Hand,"

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:02.500
are opposed to the
Science and [AUDIO OUT]

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.690
artist and the scientist.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.510
And Science and Arts are
repeated on the chapters--

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:13.070
on the spines of the books that
are on the mantle of learning.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:15.860
So you'll get an idea
of this, and you'll

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.320
see this used in a
lot of other places.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:21.170
With that, I'll
turn back to Jay.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:31.830
KAISER: If you look
behind you, you'll

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:35.820
see a glorious cityscape.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:38.490
It's one of the most beautiful,
I think, in the United States.

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:41.850
And so be sure you take
a moment at some time

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:45.100
to look at the cities--

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:48.920
the view of the city from
the Killian Court here.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:51.960
In the beginning when
MIT was designed,

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.540
I believe this court actually
went all the way down

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.840
to the river, and you could
actually dock at the end there.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.236
Now, of course, Memorial
Drive is a major thoroughfare.

00:07:02.236 --> 00:07:06.430
Well, as Warren said, the
ashes of two presidents

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:08.550
have been scattered here.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:12.330
So I'd like to ask you to
be careful where you walk.

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.850
And while you're
doing that, we're

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:19.550
going to roll our first tape.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:23.720
This court provides
many activities--

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.790
a place for many
activities at MIT.

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:30.410
And occasionally it's
used as a flight test site

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:36.650
for our robotic helicopter
developed by students at MIT.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:40.480
If you'll take a look
at the jumbotron,

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:42.790
we'll show you video of
what that looked like.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:46.262
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:54.360
- This project is to demonstrate
what automatic [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.835
maneuvering for
an aerial vehicle.

00:07:57.835 --> 00:08:03.430
- In the Killian Court, we
have done a flight in so-called

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:06.596
control augmentation system,
where the pilot gives very

00:08:06.596 --> 00:08:07.970
high-level commands,
for example,

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:14.080
go forward or go sideways
or turn, go slightly higher,

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:15.052
slightly lower.

00:08:15.052 --> 00:08:16.510
The pilot doesn't
have to take care

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.100
of stabilization of the system.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.260
That's done by automatic
control system.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:21.850
- The vehicle we
did not work on.

00:08:21.850 --> 00:08:26.500
We purchased a vehicle directly
from a miniature aircraft.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:27.970
- Nice.

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:30.380
- We picked this machine
because it's very cheap.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:34.929
It's mass manufactured,
and we could outfit it

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:37.390
with our own electronics
very quickly.

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:39.490
- So this is our ground station.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:43.152
It's a very old laptop,
about three years old.

00:08:43.152 --> 00:08:45.360
It's around [INAUDIBLE] time
[? for ?] rating system.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:51.490
And it gets the data from the
helicopter about the sensors

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:53.740
and the state--

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:56.695
attitude, velocities,
altitude, and everything else.

00:08:56.695 --> 00:09:00.900
And the ground station operator
monitors that all of sensors

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:01.814
are doing fine.

00:09:01.814 --> 00:09:03.730
And then we take all
this data, we analyze it.

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:08.770
We record the trajectories
of acrobatic maneuvers

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:13.060
that Roger has performed, and
we designed the controllers

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:14.960
that follows this trajectory.

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:19.600
- So what happens is we use
filtering technologies in order

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:25.030
to synthesize this information
into a complete and accurate

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:29.430
estimate of where the helicopter
is and what's it's doing.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:33.130
- Now, previously on
a small rotorcraft,

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:38.980
and larger rotorcraft as
well, when they were flown

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:41.470
autonomously, have been
flying very non-aggressive

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:43.810
trajectories.

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:49.550
And what we have contributed
is autonomous aggressive

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:50.500
maneuvering.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:54.730
So we were able to design
the control laws that

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:59.170
allow us to perform an aerobatic
maneuver completely hands-off.

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:04.030
- The applications could
include news gathering,

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:10.560
so gathering video footage
of hard-to-explore areas,

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.390
top of mountains, top of
volcanoes, fire situations,

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:22.670
any environment which is
very [? computated, ?] very

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:28.152
contrived, and they require
[INAUDIBLE] machines

00:10:28.152 --> 00:10:29.885
to operate.

00:10:29.885 --> 00:10:32.360
- You can also use it for
a number of [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:36.375
applications, for example,
[INAUDIBLE] and [? pursuit. ?]

00:10:36.375 --> 00:10:38.590
- There's an aesthetic
component to our research,

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:43.450
which is to make such machines
as graceful as possible.

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:45.880
What we would want is that
this machine eventually

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:49.370
behaves and flies as well,
if not better, than a bird.

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:50.867
- Altitude, altitude.

00:10:50.867 --> 00:10:54.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:13:23.561 --> 00:13:27.054
[END PLAYBACK]

00:13:31.046 --> 00:13:33.650
KAISER: Now, that
tape that you just saw

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:38.255
was the work of Eric
Feron, and his office

00:13:38.255 --> 00:13:43.000
is in the new MIT Stata Center.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.410
Hi.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:47.290
And I met him recently.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:49.390
That's one of the great
things about the center.

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:52.425
It's very easy for people
to meet one another.

00:13:52.425 --> 00:13:54.550
And one of the things that
the tape didn't show you

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:58.480
was that Eric has this
ability to write backwards.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:00.290
He can write backwards
with either hand.

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:03.370
Now, you might say, what
good is that ability?

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:06.190
Well, his office
has a glass door.

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:08.740
And if you write
backwards on the inside,

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:13.240
you can read it right
side up on the other side.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:14.800
And so that's what he does.

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:22.910
He wrote his name and his office
backwards in the Stata Center.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:25.130
The students being
honored today have

00:14:25.130 --> 00:14:29.000
been at MIT during a period
of unprecedented growth

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.520
in our student life
and learning programs,

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:36.020
resulting in the construction
of new dorms, new classrooms,

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:38.510
and a beautiful new
athletic center.

00:14:38.510 --> 00:14:42.740
For people like Warren and me,
who have been at MIT for, well,

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:46.190
30, 40 years, MIT
is really taking

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:48.560
on a completely new
look, and we hope you'll

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:51.830
get a chance to enjoy that.

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:54.800
The new dormitories, the
new athletic center, even

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.640
the Stata Center itself
has just opened up.

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:02.690
It all gives-- it's just MIT
doesn't look the same anymore.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:06.380
We recently produced a series
of short video retrospectives

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:09.830
where students talk
about the MIT experience

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:13.040
outside of their classrooms
and laboratories.

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:18.050
And if you'll watch the
humongous-tron behind us,

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:20.090
you'll catch a glimpse--

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:22.890
you may catch a glimpse
of one of your children.

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:23.600
So here we go.

00:15:28.055 --> 00:15:29.045
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:15:29.045 --> 00:15:32.510
[MUSIC - "THE ENGINEERS'
 DRINKING SONG"]

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:40.595
(SINGING) We are, we
are, we are, we are,

00:15:40.595 --> 00:15:41.915
we are the engineers.

00:15:41.915 --> 00:15:44.179
[INAUDIBLE] We can, we
can, we can, we can.

00:15:44.179 --> 00:15:45.470
- There's clubs for everything.

00:15:45.470 --> 00:15:47.690
And people are doing it, and
MIT gives them money for it.

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:49.960
And there's so much to do
here, which I did not think.

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:51.950
I thought everybody would just
be holed away in their lab

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:52.449
somewhere.

00:15:52.449 --> 00:15:55.749
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.760
- They let you have a whole
diverse group of opportunities

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:10.250
for you to kind of
follow, and you can easily

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:12.090
transition from one to another.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:14.600
- The expectations of today's
students are different.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:17.085
They expect more of
their universities

00:16:17.085 --> 00:16:18.830
than I think the
generation of students

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:20.820
maybe 20 or 30 years ago.

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:24.500
They expect their universities
to provide a range of services

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:28.750
and opportunities, which many
universities didn't provide

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:30.320
30 years ago when I came here.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:33.785
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:54.650
- I think it helps
keep them sharp.

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:56.130
I think it contributes
to the mental health.

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:57.560
It's a great way
to blow off steam

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:00.131
from the intense
classroom experience

00:17:00.131 --> 00:17:01.880
and then the sheer
intensity of the place.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:03.921
That hasn't changed either,
in fact, if anything,

00:17:03.921 --> 00:17:05.160
it's more intense.

00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:06.950
So I think it's just
a key to leading

00:17:06.950 --> 00:17:09.440
a very well-balanced life.

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:13.490
And gee, they have a lot
of interests nowadays too.

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:14.986
- A lot of students
who come to MIT

00:17:14.986 --> 00:17:16.819
were very involved in
things in high school.

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:19.619
I held several leadership
positions in tons of clubs,

00:17:19.619 --> 00:17:22.130
and I wanted to continue
that while I was here.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:23.599
Because for me,
learning isn't just

00:17:23.599 --> 00:17:26.396
about doing the curricular
aspect of things,

00:17:26.396 --> 00:17:28.520
like just learning biology
or learning about cells.

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:30.620
It's about learning
how to be a leader,

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:32.575
how to be a better
person through activities

00:17:32.575 --> 00:17:33.200
that you're in.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:35.510
And you gain a lot of skills
from working with people

00:17:35.510 --> 00:17:37.180
and learning from
people as well.

00:17:37.180 --> 00:17:41.344
- Mr. Worthing and I are
engaged to be married.

00:17:41.344 --> 00:17:42.860
- Pardon me.

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:45.230
- Because MIT did
have all of it,

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:49.040
I definitely thought that
it was the place for me.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:51.890
- We've come to understand
that the full experience

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:54.750
for the student really needs to
have this sense of community.

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:58.250
And a community means what
happens in residential groups?

00:17:58.250 --> 00:18:02.210
What happens in sports with
intramural and formal sports

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:04.250
events and collegiate
sports events?

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:05.960
What happens in music groups?

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:09.380
What happens day to day as
students work with each other

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:10.595
and with the faculty?

00:18:10.595 --> 00:18:13.220
- There are only so many things
you can learn in the classroom.

00:18:13.220 --> 00:18:16.700
And I've been here for
three and a half years now,

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:19.460
and definitely the
most I've gotten out

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:21.391
of my college experience
has not been things

00:18:21.391 --> 00:18:22.640
I've learned in the classroom.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:24.320
It's definitely been
the opportunities

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:25.580
I've had outside of it.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.371
- These are going to
be the future leaders,

00:18:27.371 --> 00:18:32.090
someone who is able to do
the work at MIT and who are

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:38.270
top-level athletes,
musicians, drama stars.

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:43.040
Whatever their passion is,
we want to find the best.

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.810
And it's not mutually
exclusive with the academics.

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:49.818
(SINGING) Don't
you lose your head.

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:55.330
- It's been something that's
given me a lot personally,

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:57.700
and I feel something that's
going to give a lot back

00:18:57.700 --> 00:18:59.350
to the community
at MIT and the rest

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:00.850
of the world in the future.

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:02.590
- I think the
current MIT student

00:19:02.590 --> 00:19:05.410
comes out and sees a much
wider range of opportunities.

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:06.970
They will lead companies.

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:08.620
They will lead groups
within companies.

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:11.520
They will go to government,
to nonprofit organizations.

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:14.890
And all the skills they acquire,
not just in the classroom,

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:18.712
but in the full community
life of being an MIT student,

00:19:18.712 --> 00:19:20.170
will be a tremendous
value to them.

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:22.045
(SINGING) Yes, we
are, yes we are.

00:19:22.045 --> 00:19:23.981
We are the engine--

00:19:23.981 --> 00:19:26.903
we are, we are, we are,
we are the engineers.

00:19:26.903 --> 00:19:27.864
[END PLAYBACK]

00:19:27.864 --> 00:19:28.364
KAISER: Yep.

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:35.100
All right, this is the
part of the program where

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:37.530
we'd like to meet some of you.

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:39.630
And we thought it
might be fun to talk

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:44.630
to those of you who have come
from the farthest distance.

00:19:44.630 --> 00:19:47.260
So if you think you've
come the longest

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:50.260
distance for this
graduation, come up to us.

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:52.499
We're standing right
over here by the screen.

00:19:52.499 --> 00:19:53.290
You can all see me.

00:19:53.290 --> 00:19:56.180
Come up, and we'll talk to
you about where you're from.

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:58.720
So those of you who have
come the longest distance.

00:19:58.720 --> 00:20:02.230
Now, remember, longest
distance is not London.

00:20:02.230 --> 00:20:05.550
That's just, I mean, London,
just around the corner.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:07.210
And not Hoboken.

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:09.906
Hoboken just seems far.

00:20:09.906 --> 00:20:10.980
Ah, here's somebody.

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:11.985
Good, hi.

00:20:11.985 --> 00:20:14.032
And let's have some others.

00:20:14.032 --> 00:20:16.240
If you think you've come
the longest distance, please

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:17.440
come here.

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:20.440
In the meantime,
let's check in and see

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:24.640
how the students are doing over
in the Johnson Athletic Center.

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:32.710
Hi, can you hear me over there?

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:38.976
You can hear me?

00:20:38.976 --> 00:20:42.555
Yes, can we work
on the sound there,

00:20:42.555 --> 00:20:45.060
because I can't quite hear you.

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:47.519
JK: Mike, they can't hear me.

00:20:47.519 --> 00:20:48.560
You want me to go louder?

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:49.976
KAISER: Yeah, that's good.

00:20:49.976 --> 00:20:52.336
[INAUDIBLE]

00:20:52.336 --> 00:20:53.170
JK: All righty.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:54.170
KAISER: That's good.

00:20:54.170 --> 00:20:55.130
What's your name?

00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:57.114
JK: JK.

00:20:57.114 --> 00:20:58.106
KAISER: JK?

00:20:58.106 --> 00:20:59.420
JK: Yep.

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:00.586
KAISER: That's my initials--

00:21:00.586 --> 00:21:01.578
Jay Kaiser.

00:21:01.578 --> 00:21:02.570
JK: [LAUGHS]

00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:05.560
KAISER: What do you know,
Where are you from, JK?

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:08.445
JK: I'm from India, but I
grew up in the Middle East.

00:21:08.445 --> 00:21:10.160
KAISER: And where
did you grow up?

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:12.305
JK: In Muscat, the
Sultanate of Oman.

00:21:12.305 --> 00:21:16.030
KAISER: So how many
languages do you speak?

00:21:16.030 --> 00:21:19.324
JK: Fluently, or languages
I assume I speak?

00:21:19.324 --> 00:21:21.930
KAISER: Languages in which you
could conduct a conversation.

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:22.672
JK: Four.

00:21:22.672 --> 00:21:24.000
KAISER: And what are they?

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:27.290
JK: English, Hindi,
Tamil, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:28.046
KAISER: Amazing.

00:21:28.046 --> 00:21:28.754
That's wonderful.

00:21:28.754 --> 00:21:30.218
And what did you major in?

00:21:33.146 --> 00:21:34.122
What was your major?

00:21:34.122 --> 00:21:35.586
JK: Aero/astro.

00:21:35.586 --> 00:21:38.520
KAISER: And do you have a job?

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:40.120
JK: Yep, I'm on staff at MIT.

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:43.480
KAISER: Wow.

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:45.400
Do you have a place to live?

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:48.540
JK: Yep, 1010 Mass Ave. My folks
are in the audience right now.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:49.915
KAISER: Oh, where
are your folks?

00:21:49.915 --> 00:21:50.865
Raise your hand.

00:21:50.865 --> 00:21:52.630
Are your folks here?

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:53.288
JK: Yep.

00:21:53.288 --> 00:21:54.960
KAISER: Where are they?

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:56.276
JK: I got--

00:21:56.276 --> 00:21:58.184
KAISER: Boy, you've
got a lot of folks.

00:21:58.184 --> 00:21:59.646
JK: [LAUGHS]

00:21:59.646 --> 00:22:00.604
KAISER: Where are they?

00:22:00.604 --> 00:22:03.024
Oh, there they are.

00:22:03.024 --> 00:22:04.960
JK, they're waiting for you.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:05.944
JK: I know, I know.

00:22:05.944 --> 00:22:08.360
Hang on, let me introduce you
to some people who are here.

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:10.380
KAISER: Terrific, please.

00:22:10.380 --> 00:22:13.290
PRETORIUS: Hi, I'm [? Jakob ?]
Pretorius from South Africa.

00:22:13.290 --> 00:22:15.000
JK: Where are you
from in South Africa?

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:15.360
PRETORIUS: [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:16.370
JK: South Africa?

00:22:16.370 --> 00:22:17.428
PRETORIUS: Johannesburg.

00:22:17.428 --> 00:22:18.844
KAISER: I've been
to Johannesburg.

00:22:18.844 --> 00:22:20.114
JK: He's been to Johannesburg.

00:22:20.114 --> 00:22:21.030
PRETORIUS: Oh, he has?

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:21.571
KAISER: Yeah.

00:22:21.571 --> 00:22:23.934
Where do you-- let's
see, jacaranda.

00:22:23.934 --> 00:22:27.322
That's right, jacaranda
trees grow in [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:27.322 --> 00:22:28.940
PRETORIUS: Yeah, very nice.

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:31.820
But Pretoria is actually the
city for jacaranda trees.

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:34.000
KAISER: Yeah, I've been to
Pretoria too, nice town.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:35.880
JK: He's been to
Pretoria as well.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:37.760
KAISER: And your name
is, what, [? Jakob? ?]

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:38.700
JK: [? Jakob? ?]

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:39.510
PRETORIUS: Yes.

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:42.067
KAISER: And are
your parents here?

00:22:42.067 --> 00:22:43.900
PRETORIUS: They should
be there, and my wife

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:45.470
should be on stage too.

00:22:45.470 --> 00:22:47.430
KAISER: You're married?

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:48.275
JK: You're married?

00:22:48.275 --> 00:22:48.900
PRETORIUS: Yes.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:50.174
KAISER: Good for you.

00:22:50.174 --> 00:22:51.174
You look like you're 16.

00:22:51.174 --> 00:22:54.070
PRETORIUS: I'm not prepared to
disclose that information right

00:22:54.070 --> 00:22:54.630
now, thanks.

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:00.502
KAISER: I don't blame you.

00:23:00.502 --> 00:23:02.210
All right, it's nice
talking to you guys.

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:03.934
We'll come back to
you in a few minutes.

00:23:03.934 --> 00:23:05.850
But we're going to roll
another tape over here

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:07.040
to entertain your parents.

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:08.240
JK: All right, cheers.

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:09.110
KAISER: Take care.

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:09.740
See you, JK.

00:23:20.106 --> 00:23:22.340
When I got a [INAUDIBLE],,
I'll come to you.

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:26.600
When the Class of
2004 were sophomores,

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:31.190
a good number of them took MIT's
renowned engineering course

00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:33.430
2.007.

00:23:33.430 --> 00:23:37.420
This is the much emulated
course where students design,

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:40.270
engineer, and build
a machine that

00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:43.540
accomplishes a specific task.

00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:47.950
All this hard work culminates
in an end-of-semester contest,

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:52.120
which is the big event at MIT.

00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:56.110
At this contest, students
compete head to head

00:23:56.110 --> 00:24:00.930
in MIT's most exciting
spectator sport.

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:03.810
We offer you a look at
some of the participants

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:06.000
and their clever machines.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:08.580
You might recognize
your graduate

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:14.200
as they appeared when they were
hard at work as sophomores.

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:17.132
2.007.

00:24:17.132 --> 00:24:19.552
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:24:19.552 --> 00:24:22.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:30.405
- 3, 2, 1, go!

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:35.890
- It's time again for the
granddaddy of all the student

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:37.570
engineering contests--

00:24:37.570 --> 00:24:40.840
the annual battle of machines
built by sophomores at MIT.

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:46.330
- And now comes the fun
part-- calling my mom.

00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:49.655
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:57.880
- This year's contest,
hatched six months earlier,

00:24:57.880 --> 00:25:03.680
involves a teeter-totter beam
and a swinging 8-pound ball.

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:05.500
The challenge is
to build a machine

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:07.600
that starts out
sitting on the beam

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:09.820
and that after
exactly 45 seconds

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:11.950
has managed to tilt
its side of the beam

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:16.790
down working against an opponent
trying to do the same thing.

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:19.960
There's a 10-pound weight limit
for each machine and a box

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:21.340
of parts to make it from.

00:25:21.340 --> 00:25:24.380
- There are a bunch of
structural materials

00:25:24.380 --> 00:25:25.570
that they can--

00:25:25.570 --> 00:25:28.240
- Each of the 100-plus
students in the contest,

00:25:28.240 --> 00:25:31.100
which is actually a course
in mechanical engineering,

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:34.780
gets an identical kit of stuff,
including several electric

00:25:34.780 --> 00:25:38.200
motors from home power tools,
as well as things that seem like

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:39.400
mechanical leftovers.

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:41.230
What would you do with this?

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:43.164
- Nobody in the class
knows what that is.

00:25:43.164 --> 00:25:44.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:45.790
- We had a--

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:47.779
- So nobody-- you don't
know what this is?

00:25:47.779 --> 00:25:49.570
- No one knows how
they're going to use it.

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:52.111
- Usually, people just do this--
pop it in and take the motor

00:25:52.111 --> 00:25:54.580
and do something with the
motor and then toss that back

00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:56.160
in there.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:57.160
What it is good for is--

00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:58.704
- It is a mistake
to toss that away?

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:05.760
After several weeks of
brainstorming and designing

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:08.150
their machines on
paper and computer,

00:26:08.150 --> 00:26:11.250
the class sets to work
manufacturing them.

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:15.480
After 14 weeks of design,
construction, and testing,

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:17.580
the machines faced
one last hurdle.

00:26:17.580 --> 00:26:19.714
- Fit it in the box
however you can get it in.

00:26:19.714 --> 00:26:20.640
- Yeah.

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:23.190
- To qualify for the
contest, every machine

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:26.236
must fit within the
box its parts came in.

00:26:26.236 --> 00:26:27.230
- Did that go straight?

00:26:27.230 --> 00:26:29.785
- Yeah, there.

00:26:29.785 --> 00:26:30.285
- All right.

00:26:30.285 --> 00:26:32.660
Put the lid on, please.

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:34.560
- Woo-hoo.

00:26:34.560 --> 00:26:38.240
- Before an expectant crowd
at MIT's ice hockey arena--

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:40.380
- 2, 1, go!

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:43.590
- --Nick Martin's machine,
flawless in tests,

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:46.006
doesn't give him
his money's worth.

00:26:46.006 --> 00:26:47.838
[INAUDIBLE]

00:26:47.838 --> 00:26:49.472
- It fell over.

00:26:49.472 --> 00:26:50.430
That was of depressing.

00:26:50.430 --> 00:26:51.580
I'd never seen it before.

00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:53.160
And I tested it, ran it.

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:57.720
But I guess that's what
engineering is all about.

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:00.690
- The first semifinal,
and now it's

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:03.360
Will Lark's turn to face
Will DelHagen's jack.

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:10.850
Almost before the car has
even jumped off the beam,

00:27:10.850 --> 00:27:14.030
the jack is hoisting it up.

00:27:14.030 --> 00:27:16.960
All Will DelHagen has to
do is stand by and watch

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:20.450
as Will Lark's car desperately
tries to hook around the jack

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:21.200
and yank it away.

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:25.128
[CHEERING]

00:27:25.128 --> 00:27:30.530
- 4, 3, 2, 1.

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:32.700
- For Will Lark, it's
the end of a great run.

00:27:35.555 --> 00:27:37.430
- That's my only way I
could've gotten it was

00:27:37.430 --> 00:27:40.208
was trying to mess him up, so.

00:27:40.208 --> 00:27:43.050
- 3, 2, go.

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:45.630
- The second semifinal.

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:47.970
It's Alex [? Jacobson's ?]
mobile jack

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:50.700
against a simple extender
that has quietly made it

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:52.710
through round after around.

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:55.020
For a moment, it looks like
Alex has gotten trapped

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:57.210
by the corner, but then--

00:27:59.805 --> 00:28:03.674
so for the finals, it's mobile
jack against mobile jack.

00:28:03.674 --> 00:28:05.340
- There was a lot of
things I didn't get

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:06.631
done that I wanted to get done.

00:28:06.631 --> 00:28:09.070
But everything I needed,
I guess, I finished, so.

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:12.150
- We tracked Alex down
behind the scenes,

00:28:12.150 --> 00:28:16.470
where it turned out he'd
been conspiring with Will.

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:18.500
Good friends who have
been comparing notes ever

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:20.820
since the class
began, Will and Alex

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:23.328
now plan a final collaboration.

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:30.930
Carefully choreographed,
the two robots

00:28:30.930 --> 00:28:34.050
jack the whole apparatus
an inch off the ground

00:28:34.050 --> 00:28:35.790
with the beam dead level.

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:37.380
- Doghouse.

00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:38.740
- The result is a tie.

00:28:38.740 --> 00:28:40.186
- Double win?

00:28:40.186 --> 00:28:41.150
- Double win.

00:28:41.150 --> 00:28:42.120
Double win.

00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:45.252
- Or, in MIT speak,
a double win.

00:28:45.252 --> 00:28:46.670
- Double win.

00:28:46.670 --> 00:28:49.069
- This has never
ever happened before.

00:28:49.069 --> 00:28:50.610
And we're so happy
it did, because we

00:28:50.610 --> 00:28:52.408
like doing things different.

00:28:52.408 --> 00:28:53.216
- Double win.

00:28:53.216 --> 00:28:53.850
[END PLAYBACK]

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:59.090
SEAMANS: Scientific American--
the clip you've just seen

00:28:59.090 --> 00:29:04.680
is credited to Scientific
American Frontiers.

00:29:04.680 --> 00:29:11.340
The 2.007 contest remains MIT's
most widely attended spectator

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:12.390
event.

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:14.970
But a lack of
spectators does not

00:29:14.970 --> 00:29:19.500
prevent MIT students from
participating in athletics.

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:23.460
In fact, it is not
widely known, but MIT

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:29.610
fields more NCAA athletic
teams than any other school

00:29:29.610 --> 00:29:32.580
in the country, with
the possible exception

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:36.770
of that other
school up the river.

00:29:36.770 --> 00:29:41.520
As this video
illustrates, MIT students

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:43.620
don't sit in the stands.

00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:44.580
They participate.

00:29:48.516 --> 00:29:49.500
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:52.944
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:16.770
- MIT sponsors a uniquely
expansive athletic department

00:30:16.770 --> 00:30:20.070
which features 25
intercollegiate sports, 40 club

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:23.070
sport offerings, and 42
intercollegiate athletic

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:23.570
programs.

00:30:30.310 --> 00:30:34.390
- Athletics are part of this
notion of building a community

00:30:34.390 --> 00:30:37.870
and building a complete
individual's education.

00:30:37.870 --> 00:30:40.360
- I certainly feel more
well rounded because of it.

00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:44.650
I feel that I've had the chance
to excel in different aspects

00:30:44.650 --> 00:30:45.610
of my life.

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:48.730
- MIT has the largest number
of academic All-Americans

00:30:48.730 --> 00:30:52.930
in the country, with 114 since
the program began in 1980.

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:55.000
I think it showcases
that our kids are

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:57.400
doing exceptionally
well in the classroom

00:30:57.400 --> 00:30:58.664
as well as on the field.

00:30:58.664 --> 00:31:00.080
- At the beginning
of each season,

00:31:00.080 --> 00:31:03.470
the football coach says,
gentlemen, the first priority

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:05.404
for you is going to
be your academics.

00:31:05.404 --> 00:31:07.070
Second priority is
going to be football.

00:31:07.070 --> 00:31:08.840
And if your list
doesn't look like this,

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:10.298
then you shouldn't
be in this room.

00:31:10.298 --> 00:31:12.050
- Nearly 60% of
our undergraduates

00:31:12.050 --> 00:31:14.869
compete in organized sports
at some level on campus.

00:31:14.869 --> 00:31:16.910
- It's a good thing because
I met a lot of people

00:31:16.910 --> 00:31:18.368
that I wouldn't
have met otherwise,

00:31:18.368 --> 00:31:19.986
who I would see afterwards.

00:31:19.986 --> 00:31:21.610
And I'm like, oh,
you're on my IM team.

00:31:21.610 --> 00:31:22.440
So how's it going?

00:31:22.440 --> 00:31:23.570
How's your research?

00:31:23.570 --> 00:31:25.109
- They don't exist
for themselves.

00:31:25.109 --> 00:31:27.650
They don't exist for the alumni
to make the alumni feel good.

00:31:27.650 --> 00:31:29.160
We have them
because they're part

00:31:29.160 --> 00:31:30.410
of the educational experience.

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:35.360
- [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:37.340
Out and into the water, down.

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:40.805
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:31:51.710 --> 00:31:54.260
- The crew boat
itself, a 70-foot boat,

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:56.400
is a micromanaged
system in and of itself.

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:58.280
You got the administrator
in your coxswain.

00:31:58.280 --> 00:31:59.780
And then you've got
your powerhouse.

00:31:59.780 --> 00:32:02.930
You've got your stroke, who is
your leader of your powerhouse.

00:32:02.930 --> 00:32:04.860
And then you've got
your 4 and 5 seats

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:06.960
who are the peak of your boat.

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:08.960
And then you've got your
bow, who kind of guides

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:11.043
you along in the little
directions left and right.

00:32:11.043 --> 00:32:14.360
And so together you work as
a team to the common goal.

00:32:14.360 --> 00:32:19.790
- They learn incredible amounts
about leadership, teamwork,

00:32:19.790 --> 00:32:23.330
sportsmanship, and
courage, the willingness

00:32:23.330 --> 00:32:27.560
to take risks and push
their abilities beyond what

00:32:27.560 --> 00:32:29.070
they think they can achieve.

00:32:29.070 --> 00:32:32.540
- I have learned to
push myself during races

00:32:32.540 --> 00:32:36.560
and during practices so
hard and commit myself

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:39.740
to a certain goal
unequivocally and in a way

00:32:39.740 --> 00:32:41.364
that I never had before.

00:32:41.364 --> 00:32:44.752
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:32:51.050 --> 00:32:54.520
- The MIT I knew in the '60s
and early '70s had a proper

00:32:54.520 --> 00:32:57.790
emphasis on athletics and a
tremendous diversity of sports

00:32:57.790 --> 00:32:59.890
and really emphasized
participation.

00:32:59.890 --> 00:33:01.304
That part hasn't changed.

00:33:01.304 --> 00:33:03.220
But what's happened is
there is even a greater

00:33:03.220 --> 00:33:04.630
variety of sports today.

00:33:04.630 --> 00:33:07.180
- If MIT doesn't have
something, be it a club

00:33:07.180 --> 00:33:09.729
or an organization or a sport,
if two people want to do it,

00:33:09.729 --> 00:33:11.770
they'll let you organize
it, and you can have it.

00:33:11.770 --> 00:33:15.201
It's up to the individual,
but MIT will embrace that.

00:33:15.201 --> 00:33:17.200
- It always has been in
the past and always will

00:33:17.200 --> 00:33:18.560
be about opportunity for all.

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:20.560
- One of the things that
I think is particularly

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:23.350
striking about the
athletic program at MIT

00:33:23.350 --> 00:33:27.010
is the emphasis on
participation in sports

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:28.600
rather than spectator sports.

00:33:28.600 --> 00:33:30.700
You don't see the
whole campus coming out

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:33.070
to watch the football game
on a Saturday afternoon

00:33:33.070 --> 00:33:35.260
like you might find on
some college campuses.

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:37.150
But you do see an
incredible number

00:33:37.150 --> 00:33:39.220
of students that are engaged.

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:42.010
Currently, about
20% of the students

00:33:42.010 --> 00:33:43.990
are involved in a varsity sport.

00:33:43.990 --> 00:33:48.610
About 80% of the students are
involved in intramural sports.

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:51.430
And 100% of the
undergraduates are

00:33:51.430 --> 00:33:53.620
involved in the physical
education program.

00:33:53.620 --> 00:33:56.660
- So it really lets students
pursue their interests,

00:33:56.660 --> 00:33:59.616
whether they pursue them as
an extracurricular activity

00:33:59.616 --> 00:34:01.990
through their school previously
or whether it's something

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:04.340
brand-new.

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:07.480
- We've been able to make
major investments in facilities

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:10.389
and support services
that have enhanced

00:34:10.389 --> 00:34:11.770
an already vital program.

00:34:11.770 --> 00:34:14.614
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:34:20.489 --> 00:34:23.030
The biggest single change has
been the opening of the Zesiger

00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:23.530
Center.

00:34:23.530 --> 00:34:26.683
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:34:33.389 --> 00:34:34.350
- The Z Center.

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:35.840
That thing's just
beautiful, man.

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:38.170
- It just seemed like the
MIT campus became healthier

00:34:38.170 --> 00:34:40.179
because people were
more willing to work out

00:34:40.179 --> 00:34:44.110
because there were more
facilities and they were nicer.

00:34:44.110 --> 00:34:47.139
- The new facilities
have been made possible

00:34:47.139 --> 00:34:50.710
because there was a
vision on the part of MIT,

00:34:50.710 --> 00:34:53.290
but then the
facilities could not

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:56.440
have been created without the
financial support of the alums,

00:34:56.440 --> 00:34:59.500
who have come forth
to say, yes, we

00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:02.140
recognize that that is
an important priority,

00:35:02.140 --> 00:35:04.109
and we want to be
able to support it.

00:35:04.109 --> 00:35:05.650
- You'll be in there
lifting weights,

00:35:05.650 --> 00:35:06.610
and your professor
will be in there

00:35:06.610 --> 00:35:08.050
lifting weights
also or something,

00:35:08.050 --> 00:35:09.400
and you'll say hi to him.

00:35:09.400 --> 00:35:11.740
And just little things like
that, seeing people outside

00:35:11.740 --> 00:35:15.150
of the classroom and interacting
not in an academic way,

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:17.375
I think, really helps
build the community.

00:35:20.050 --> 00:35:21.990
- It's not just
about the classroom

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:24.120
or just about the
laboratory, but it's

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:26.040
about growing as a person.

00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:29.310
And part of that is
working with other students

00:35:29.310 --> 00:35:32.100
or participating in sports
or other sort of events,

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:35.180
the arts, all of which
will carry forward

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:36.180
into their future lives.

00:35:36.180 --> 00:35:39.652
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:35:51.556 --> 00:35:54.050
[END PLAYBACK]

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:58.550
KAISER: If you count Europe
and Asia as a single continent,

00:35:58.550 --> 00:36:00.500
there are six
continents in the world,

00:36:00.500 --> 00:36:03.360
and we have three of
them represented here.

00:36:03.360 --> 00:36:07.590
We have the man who has come
the farthest, from Perth,

00:36:07.590 --> 00:36:09.950
Australia, is Tan.

00:36:09.950 --> 00:36:13.210
Raise your hand, Tan.

00:36:13.210 --> 00:36:16.430
He spent, what, 25
hours on the plane.

00:36:16.430 --> 00:36:20.620
TAN: 25 hours flying [INAUDIBLE]
from Perth to Boston.

00:36:20.620 --> 00:36:21.670
KAISER: And you have--

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:23.500
how many children
are graduating?

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:26.230
TAN: I got one child
who's graduating now, yep,

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:29.590
and she's in the Sloan
School of Management.

00:36:29.590 --> 00:36:31.420
KAISER: Sloan School
of Management.

00:36:31.420 --> 00:36:36.460
And the next farthest
comes from Bombay, India.

00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:40.310
Her name is Amita Parekh.

00:36:40.310 --> 00:36:40.810
PAREKH: Hi.

00:36:40.810 --> 00:36:42.500
KAISER: [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:43.440
PAREKH: One.

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:46.420
He's graduating from the
Sloan School of Management,

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:48.890
and I'm very proud.

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:53.300
KAISER: Two comments,
one graduate school.

00:36:53.300 --> 00:37:00.788
Our next comes from Buenos
Aires, Argentina, Hugo Batales.

00:37:00.788 --> 00:37:02.234
And your child?

00:37:02.234 --> 00:37:05.700
BATALES: My child is
Alexander Batales.

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:07.280
He's [INAUDIBLE] in economics.

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:08.209
KAISER: In economics.

00:37:08.209 --> 00:37:09.000
BATALES: Economics.

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:11.630
KAISER: And is he going to
go back to Buenos Aires?

00:37:11.630 --> 00:37:14.610
BATALES: No, I think he
thinks to continue study

00:37:14.610 --> 00:37:15.810
in the school.

00:37:15.810 --> 00:37:17.190
KAISER: Very good.

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:23.050
And then finally, from Beirut,
Lebanon, Michelle and Patricia

00:37:23.050 --> 00:37:24.060
Nabti.

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:24.930
NABTI: Hello.

00:37:24.930 --> 00:37:27.780
Yes, our daughter
is Jumana Nabti

00:37:27.780 --> 00:37:30.630
and she's getting two masters,
one in urban planning,

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:32.722
one in transportation.

00:37:32.722 --> 00:37:34.090
KAISER: Wow, that's wonderful.

00:37:34.090 --> 00:37:36.546
So tell me, Tan, since
you've come the farthest,

00:37:36.546 --> 00:37:38.920
is this your first time you've
been to the United States?

00:37:38.920 --> 00:37:42.598
TAN: No, I've been here a few
times, and I like America.

00:37:45.410 --> 00:37:47.040
KAISER: Well, that's a relief.

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:50.360
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:50.360 --> 00:37:51.500
It's good to hear that.

00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:54.005
I like Australia.

00:37:54.005 --> 00:37:56.210
And Hugo, is this
your first time?

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:58.050
BATALES: No, I came many times.

00:37:58.050 --> 00:38:02.405
Actually, I was married
with an American woman,

00:38:02.405 --> 00:38:06.310
and we got three kids, two
born in Buenos Aries and one

00:38:06.310 --> 00:38:07.985
born in the States.

00:38:07.985 --> 00:38:09.850
KAISER: And what do you do?

00:38:09.850 --> 00:38:12.040
BATALES: I have an industry.

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:15.040
I make fire extinguishers
in Argentina.

00:38:15.040 --> 00:38:17.100
KAISER: You make
fire extinguishers.

00:38:17.100 --> 00:38:19.165
Would you give the
people here a deal?

00:38:19.165 --> 00:38:21.290
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:21.290 --> 00:38:23.750
Hugo says, if there's anybody
out there needs a fire

00:38:23.750 --> 00:38:26.400
extinguisher, see him.

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:28.370
Tan, what do you do?

00:38:28.370 --> 00:38:29.900
TAN: I'm a businessman.

00:38:29.900 --> 00:38:31.429
I make cakes.

00:38:31.429 --> 00:38:32.345
KAISER: You make what?

00:38:32.345 --> 00:38:33.692
TAN: Cheesecakes.

00:38:33.692 --> 00:38:35.540
AUDIENCE: Mm, cheesecake.

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:40.925
KAISER: All right, now
Tan is a friend of mine.

00:38:40.925 --> 00:38:43.835
NABTI: He is my friend too.

00:38:43.835 --> 00:38:44.960
KAISER: And what about you?

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:45.860
What do you do?

00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:50.830
PAREKH: I work in a bank
in India, a private bank.

00:38:50.830 --> 00:38:52.510
KAISER: I just
changed allegiances.

00:38:55.470 --> 00:38:58.221
Michelle and Patricia,
what do you do?

00:38:58.221 --> 00:38:58.720
NABTI: Yes.

00:38:58.720 --> 00:39:01.060
Well, I established
a volunteer center

00:39:01.060 --> 00:39:03.000
for the country of Lebanon.

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:05.866
So the last six years
that's what I've been doing.

00:39:05.866 --> 00:39:06.820
KAISER: And you?

00:39:06.820 --> 00:39:09.520
NABTI: I spent a career
at the Hoover Institution

00:39:09.520 --> 00:39:13.300
on War, Revolution, and
Peace at Stanford University

00:39:13.300 --> 00:39:16.280
as Middle East area specialist.

00:39:16.280 --> 00:39:18.895
KAISER: So your area of
specialty is Middle East.

00:39:18.895 --> 00:39:20.030
NABTI: Modern Middle East.

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:21.050
KAISER: So it's your fault.

00:39:21.050 --> 00:39:21.550
NABTI: Yes.

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:26.420
KAISER: All right.

00:39:26.420 --> 00:39:29.690
So what we got to do here is
break out a little cheesecake

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:34.550
and break out a little fire
extinguisher and have fun.

00:39:34.550 --> 00:39:35.810
Thank you very much, folks.

00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:36.934
It's really been marvelous.

00:39:36.934 --> 00:39:38.327
AUDIENCE: Oh, wait.

00:39:38.327 --> 00:39:39.410
Here comes a proud mother.

00:39:39.410 --> 00:39:41.018
KAISER: OK, we've got this--

00:39:41.018 --> 00:39:43.320
we've got to [INAUDIBLE]
people [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:43.320 --> 00:39:43.820
All right.

00:39:43.820 --> 00:39:46.624
Now, I want-- oh, we
have somebody else here?

00:39:46.624 --> 00:39:47.831
Oh, where are you from?

00:39:47.831 --> 00:39:49.205
PARENT: I am coming
from Chennai.

00:39:49.205 --> 00:39:50.870
PARENT: India.

00:39:50.870 --> 00:39:53.404
And I'm mother of
JK, who just talk.

00:39:53.404 --> 00:39:54.320
KAISER: Oh, very good.

00:39:54.320 --> 00:39:57.601
Well, is Chennai
farther than Bombay?

00:39:57.601 --> 00:39:58.100
PARENT: Yes.

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:05.250
KAISER: JK, your
parents are here.

00:40:05.250 --> 00:40:08.216
They want to know
what you're up to.

00:40:08.216 --> 00:40:09.340
Thank you very much, folks.

00:40:09.340 --> 00:40:10.850
It's really been wonderful.

00:40:10.850 --> 00:40:11.740
Thank you very much.

00:40:11.740 --> 00:40:13.536
Let's give them a big hand.

00:40:13.536 --> 00:40:16.404
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:20.930
He's got two.

00:40:20.930 --> 00:40:22.610
Hang on just a second.

00:40:22.610 --> 00:40:27.110
Now then, I would like to
invite all those families who

00:40:27.110 --> 00:40:30.930
have the most MIT
degrees back to back.

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:34.550
In other words, if you have
a child and you went to MIT

00:40:34.550 --> 00:40:38.180
and your grandfather went to
MIT and your great-grandfather,

00:40:38.180 --> 00:40:39.950
come on up.

00:40:39.950 --> 00:40:44.840
In the meantime, Killian
Court is not only

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:48.620
the setting for MIT's grand
commencement ceremony,

00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:52.620
but it's a favorite setting
for relaxation and recreation.

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:54.800
This is something very special.

00:40:54.800 --> 00:41:00.030
Killian Court has also
become a preferred hunting

00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:04.860
ground for MIT's resident
red-tailed hawks.

00:41:04.860 --> 00:41:07.680
Apparently, a number of
nesting pairs of hawks

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:11.040
have decided, as many of
you did four years ago,

00:41:11.040 --> 00:41:15.970
that MIT is a great environment
for maturation and learning.

00:41:15.970 --> 00:41:19.650
We recently had the pleasure,
due to their choice of nest

00:41:19.650 --> 00:41:24.850
location, to witness up close a
four-week process in which two

00:41:24.850 --> 00:41:28.050
down-covered chicks grew
into fledglings that

00:41:28.050 --> 00:41:30.430
left the nest just last week.

00:41:30.430 --> 00:41:32.830
We offer this
retrospective as we

00:41:32.830 --> 00:41:36.520
wait for your prospective
fledglings to arrive.

00:41:36.520 --> 00:41:39.862
Take a look at the
humongous-tron.

00:41:39.862 --> 00:41:40.770
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:41:40.770 --> 00:41:43.860
- These baby chicks were
hatched on April 9 in a tree

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:47.070
just across the street
from the Student Center.

00:41:47.070 --> 00:41:50.430
During the week of April 19,
MIT's in-house video production

00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:54.330
department set up a video camera
and shared these images 24/7

00:41:54.330 --> 00:41:58.890
throughout the Institute
via MIT's cable TV system.

00:41:58.890 --> 00:42:01.620
The following week, we began
sharing the development

00:42:01.620 --> 00:42:07.050
of these chicks with the world
via a 12-hour per day webcast.

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:10.800
We were amazed at how
quickly they grew.

00:42:10.800 --> 00:42:12.690
Many viewers wrote
to tell us that they

00:42:12.690 --> 00:42:16.680
had detected visible
growth within a single day.

00:42:16.680 --> 00:42:19.800
Not unlike the graduates we
honor today when they were just

00:42:19.800 --> 00:42:24.000
babies, the single mission of
these youngsters was to be fed

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:26.170
and to sleep.

00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:28.920
The parent hawks constantly
doted on the chicks,

00:42:28.920 --> 00:42:34.150
delivering a nonstop assortment
of Cambridge-area prey.

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:36.400
Their first tentative
steps resulted

00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:38.860
in the inevitable
stumble and header.

00:42:38.860 --> 00:42:41.260
The parent hawks continued
to fortify the nest

00:42:41.260 --> 00:42:43.490
as the babies grew.

00:42:43.490 --> 00:42:45.680
These chicks also
experience some

00:42:45.680 --> 00:42:48.020
of the same character-building
New England weather

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:51.800
that your children have been
exposed to these past years.

00:42:51.800 --> 00:42:53.954
At different times,
the outstretched wings

00:42:53.954 --> 00:42:55.370
of the mother
protected the babies

00:42:55.370 --> 00:43:00.250
from a 2-inch dusting of snow
or from several downpours.

00:43:00.250 --> 00:43:05.220
And through it all, the chicks
ate and grew and ate some more.

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:08.340
During their sophomore period,
the down gave way in places

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:10.230
to feathers, and
the now juveniles

00:43:10.230 --> 00:43:12.270
became more adventurous.

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:14.510
More aggressive attempts
at negotiating the nest

00:43:14.510 --> 00:43:16.336
earned similar results--

00:43:16.336 --> 00:43:18.170
a face plant.

00:43:18.170 --> 00:43:21.750
But as the footing became
surer and the balance improved,

00:43:21.750 --> 00:43:23.970
it was time to
stretch those wings

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:27.100
and exercise those muscles.

00:43:27.100 --> 00:43:29.600
Now, as juniors, these
students were learning

00:43:29.600 --> 00:43:31.460
as much as they could handle.

00:43:31.460 --> 00:43:33.590
Mother explained that
the food they ate

00:43:33.590 --> 00:43:35.870
didn't just grow on trees.

00:43:35.870 --> 00:43:38.490
Lessons were provided in
the value of the hunt.

00:43:38.490 --> 00:43:40.880
And as the last bits of
down were preened away

00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:44.540
and feathers continued to grow,
the youngsters enthusiastically

00:43:44.540 --> 00:43:48.410
tested their abilities
at defying gravity.

00:43:48.410 --> 00:43:51.050
As is usually the case,
one of the siblings

00:43:51.050 --> 00:43:54.035
was far more the
risk-taker, the trailblazer.

00:43:54.035 --> 00:43:56.770
Though only separated by
perhaps a day at birth,

00:43:56.770 --> 00:43:58.850
it was clear that
one of the siblings

00:43:58.850 --> 00:44:02.930
was far more anxious to
explore life outside the nest.

00:44:02.930 --> 00:44:06.500
Finally, graduation
week had arrived.

00:44:06.500 --> 00:44:09.590
And just a week ago Tuesday,
the more precocious sibling,

00:44:09.590 --> 00:44:14.270
who we named Kitty Hawk,
jumped from the nest

00:44:14.270 --> 00:44:16.220
and glided safely
to the other side

00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:19.430
of Mass Ave., landing on
a bicycle rack in front

00:44:19.430 --> 00:44:21.710
of the Student Center.

00:44:21.710 --> 00:44:24.710
MIT Campus Police, aided
by a number of onlookers,

00:44:24.710 --> 00:44:26.810
secured the area,
while the fledgling

00:44:26.810 --> 00:44:29.210
practiced take-offs
and landings by flying

00:44:29.210 --> 00:44:32.300
from handlebar to handlebar.

00:44:32.300 --> 00:44:34.160
Over the next few
hours, Kitty Hawk

00:44:34.160 --> 00:44:37.100
made its way, seemingly
a branch at a time,

00:44:37.100 --> 00:44:39.920
up a nearby evergreen
to an elevation where

00:44:39.920 --> 00:44:42.890
it gained enough confidence to
attempt a return flight back

00:44:42.890 --> 00:44:45.863
to the nest, encouraged
by cries from its mother.

00:44:45.863 --> 00:44:49.244
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:44:52.142 --> 00:44:54.280
Kitty Hawk provided
encouragement

00:44:54.280 --> 00:44:56.320
to the less daring
sibling, Mitzi,

00:44:56.320 --> 00:44:58.240
also known as Chicken Hawk.

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:00.250
But Mitzi wasn't quite ready.

00:45:00.250 --> 00:45:03.400
Days passed, and Mitzi, although
the spirit seemed willing,

00:45:03.400 --> 00:45:06.730
couldn't bring himself to
venture beyond the nest.

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:09.520
We speculated that yet
another torrential downpour

00:45:09.520 --> 00:45:11.260
might provide the
necessary incentive,

00:45:11.260 --> 00:45:13.150
but Mitzi toughed it out.

00:45:13.150 --> 00:45:15.940
Food continued to be
provided, and he now

00:45:15.940 --> 00:45:18.250
had a room all to himself.

00:45:18.250 --> 00:45:20.470
Why leave?

00:45:20.470 --> 00:45:22.090
Parental and sibling
encouragement

00:45:22.090 --> 00:45:25.495
continued, to where last
Friday Mitzi jumped,

00:45:25.495 --> 00:45:27.880
flew to the highest
point in the tree,

00:45:27.880 --> 00:45:31.090
and looked like he was ready.

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:33.250
A gust of wind and
a flurry of wings,

00:45:33.250 --> 00:45:35.511
and the less than gracious
hawk stumbled downward--

00:45:35.511 --> 00:45:38.948
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:45:42.880 --> 00:45:46.000
--luckily landing
in the nest below.

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:48.670
Failure breeds success,
and Mitzi undoubtedly

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:50.860
learned from that
failed attempt.

00:45:50.860 --> 00:45:54.490
The very next morning,
Saturday, 5:00 AM, Mitzi

00:45:54.490 --> 00:45:56.290
took his maiden flight.

00:45:56.290 --> 00:45:58.165
Perhaps he was waiting
for a smaller audience

00:45:58.165 --> 00:46:00.310
or for the sun to finally shine.

00:46:00.310 --> 00:46:02.140
But later that day,
he and Kitty Hawk

00:46:02.140 --> 00:46:04.900
were spotted together on the
highest ledge of the Student

00:46:04.900 --> 00:46:06.940
Center and have
since been spotted

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:10.630
on ledges and windowsills
throughout the Institute.

00:46:10.630 --> 00:46:12.550
You may even see them
flying about today.

00:46:17.420 --> 00:46:19.730
This is indeed a place
where the young learn

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:23.840
while they mature
and test their limits

00:46:23.840 --> 00:46:26.510
and learn from their
mistakes and finally

00:46:26.510 --> 00:46:29.930
leave, with confidence and a
sense of great accomplishment

00:46:29.930 --> 00:46:32.660
to stretch their wings and
explore new horizons where

00:46:32.660 --> 00:46:36.130
possibilities are boundless.

00:46:36.130 --> 00:46:38.080
We wish them the very best.

00:46:38.080 --> 00:46:41.461
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:46:41.461 --> 00:46:44.842
[END PLAYBACK]

