00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:44.540
Is everyone ready?

00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:46.123
KAISER: Everybody,
look at the screen.

00:49:46.123 --> 00:49:48.400
LEE: We're kind of
limbered up here.

00:49:48.400 --> 00:49:49.960
All right, you
give us the count.

00:49:49.960 --> 00:49:51.020
KAISER: A 1.

00:49:51.020 --> 00:49:51.795
LEE: 1.

00:49:51.795 --> 00:49:52.295
KAISER: 2.

00:49:52.295 --> 00:49:53.011
LEE: 2.

00:49:53.011 --> 00:49:53.510
KAISER: 3.

00:49:53.510 --> 00:49:53.976
LEE: 3.

00:49:53.976 --> 00:49:54.475
Hey.

00:49:54.475 --> 00:49:56.772
ALL: Yay.

00:49:56.772 --> 00:49:59.435
LEE: Love you.

00:49:59.435 --> 00:50:00.310
KAISER: Great, Kenny.

00:50:00.310 --> 00:50:01.815
That's the MIT wave.

00:50:01.815 --> 00:50:02.440
LEE: Thank you.

00:50:07.519 --> 00:50:09.310
KAISER: Wow, that looks
like they're having

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:11.230
a very good time over there.

00:50:11.230 --> 00:50:15.260
I wonder if they'll
ever come over here.

00:50:15.260 --> 00:50:20.210
MIT has just recently
celebrated the completion

00:50:20.210 --> 00:50:24.350
of the long-awaited
Ray and Maria Stata

00:50:24.350 --> 00:50:28.850
Center, a Frank
Gehry-designed building that

00:50:28.850 --> 00:50:31.700
is the home to the Computer
Science and Artificial

00:50:31.700 --> 00:50:36.980
Intelligence Laboratories, the
lab for Information and Design

00:50:36.980 --> 00:50:42.110
Systems, and the Departments
of Linguistics and Philosophy.

00:50:42.110 --> 00:50:47.180
I'm a member of the Department
of Linguistics and Philosophy,

00:50:47.180 --> 00:50:52.070
and I have an office
in the Stata Building.

00:50:52.070 --> 00:50:54.480
If you haven't seen
it, you must go see it.

00:50:54.480 --> 00:50:59.420
It's on Vassar Street, and it's
that very odd-shaped-looking

00:50:59.420 --> 00:51:00.370
building.

00:51:00.370 --> 00:51:03.500
None of the lines are
straight up and down.

00:51:03.500 --> 00:51:05.660
They're all sort of at angles.

00:51:05.660 --> 00:51:10.250
In fact, the worse
fear of the architect

00:51:10.250 --> 00:51:14.030
is that there will
be an earthquake

00:51:14.030 --> 00:51:17.630
and straighten the building.

00:51:17.630 --> 00:51:20.620
So we're going to see
a video tape that's

00:51:20.620 --> 00:51:23.950
going to compress
into a few moments

00:51:23.950 --> 00:51:28.971
the building of this
remarkable edifice.

00:51:33.215 --> 00:51:33.881
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:51:33.881 --> 00:51:37.318
[MUSIC - "TAKE FIVE"]

00:51:41.246 --> 00:51:44.690
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:44.690 --> 00:51:48.800
- I'm sure that the legacy of
the great accomplishments that

00:51:48.800 --> 00:51:51.710
were born in Building 20 that
you've all heard about here

00:51:51.710 --> 00:51:56.930
today are going to be preserved
and extended and enhanced

00:51:56.930 --> 00:52:00.650
in this new space that will
replace the time-honored

00:52:00.650 --> 00:52:01.730
building.

00:52:01.730 --> 00:52:04.410
- We are thrilled that we
will be able to move there,

00:52:04.410 --> 00:52:08.010
and our generations of students
and researchers to come

00:52:08.010 --> 00:52:10.280
will be able to
continue to innovate

00:52:10.280 --> 00:52:12.380
and help us lead better lives.

00:52:12.380 --> 00:52:14.960
- This piece of land in
this part of the campus

00:52:14.960 --> 00:52:17.840
will mark the information
science, cognitive science,

00:52:17.840 --> 00:52:21.860
and biology parts that will
drive much of our economy

00:52:21.860 --> 00:52:25.160
and much of our knowledge
in this next century.

00:52:25.160 --> 00:52:28.160
- We have the best faculty
and the best students.

00:52:28.160 --> 00:52:31.190
And soon they will have
the group of buildings

00:52:31.190 --> 00:52:33.704
of the quality they deserve.

00:52:33.704 --> 00:52:37.148
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:09.390
- This is also a building
that pushes the edge of design

00:53:09.390 --> 00:53:11.160
and construction technology.

00:53:11.160 --> 00:53:13.980
It's a building that
was built entirely

00:53:13.980 --> 00:53:17.347
from a three-dimensional
digital model.

00:53:17.347 --> 00:53:20.756
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:53:30.577 --> 00:53:32.910
- One of the things that we
really wanted from the start

00:53:32.910 --> 00:53:35.780
was room to think, and
this literally gives us

00:53:35.780 --> 00:53:36.780
plenty of room to think.

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:37.710
There's light.

00:53:37.710 --> 00:53:38.339
There's air.

00:53:38.339 --> 00:53:39.380
- It's like an ecosystem.

00:53:39.380 --> 00:53:40.880
It has everything we need.

00:53:40.880 --> 00:53:41.730
- I love it.

00:53:41.730 --> 00:53:43.830
It's like living in a diorama.

00:53:43.830 --> 00:53:47.050
- It's made me use
my imagination,

00:53:47.050 --> 00:53:49.320
and I think that's
what it's all about.

00:53:49.320 --> 00:53:52.320
- The transition from the inside
world to the outside world

00:53:52.320 --> 00:53:54.150
is gradual, and
there seems to be

00:53:54.150 --> 00:53:57.540
a union between the
inside and the outside.

00:53:57.540 --> 00:53:59.080
- Just amazing
space, amazing space.

00:53:59.080 --> 00:54:01.330
You see, we have the two
floors here, the first floor,

00:54:01.330 --> 00:54:02.510
the second floor.

00:54:02.510 --> 00:54:04.190
Each slab is organized that way.

00:54:04.190 --> 00:54:06.480
- The space is really original.

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:09.560
And I really like the fact
that a lot of the neighborhood

00:54:09.560 --> 00:54:13.200
organized around open
spaces that really encourage

00:54:13.200 --> 00:54:15.570
collaboration and
informal meeting,

00:54:15.570 --> 00:54:17.730
just bumping into each other.

00:54:17.730 --> 00:54:20.100
- I thought it was
rather traditional.

00:54:20.100 --> 00:54:22.440
- I love the fact that I
don't have to go outside

00:54:22.440 --> 00:54:24.205
to get to the main campus.

00:54:24.205 --> 00:54:26.580
- I was really excited to see
how beautiful the space is.

00:54:26.580 --> 00:54:28.350
It's really just
these lofted ceilings

00:54:28.350 --> 00:54:29.880
and a lot of community areas.

00:54:29.880 --> 00:54:33.870
- There was always a community,
but it was a community

00:54:33.870 --> 00:54:35.970
in spite of the architecture.

00:54:35.970 --> 00:54:38.940
Here we have a community
abetted by the architecture.

00:54:38.940 --> 00:54:42.510
- What I have is a view
not of Boston or Harvard,

00:54:42.510 --> 00:54:45.760
but a view looking
right into the building.

00:54:45.760 --> 00:54:48.180
So I get to see
Frank Gehry every day

00:54:48.180 --> 00:54:51.590
as I look out of my
window, and I like that.

00:54:56.180 --> 00:54:58.070
- Stata is a building
that's bubbling over

00:54:58.070 --> 00:55:02.150
with the joy of invention, the
pleasure of space and light,

00:55:02.150 --> 00:55:04.550
the satisfaction of
making something well,

00:55:04.550 --> 00:55:06.560
and the enjoyment
of being members

00:55:06.560 --> 00:55:12.440
of a vital, argumentative,
bursting with ideas community.

00:55:12.440 --> 00:55:15.800
This is a building that
combines superb technology

00:55:15.800 --> 00:55:17.619
with humanity and fun.

00:55:17.619 --> 00:55:21.112
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:55:30.593 --> 00:55:34.090
[END PLAYBACK]

00:55:34.090 --> 00:55:38.110
KAISER: We are about to
interview the MIT generations.

00:55:38.110 --> 00:55:41.410
But before we do, I'd like
to make a brief announcement.

00:55:41.410 --> 00:55:45.310
The MIT News Office
has produced a brochure

00:55:45.310 --> 00:55:49.780
that outlines the
accomplishments of Chuck Vest's

00:55:49.780 --> 00:55:53.360
administration
and career at MIT.

00:55:53.360 --> 00:55:57.040
That brochure is available
at the tent behind you,

00:55:57.040 --> 00:55:59.870
and you can pick it
up any time you like.

00:55:59.870 --> 00:56:01.420
It's a very
interesting brochure,

00:56:01.420 --> 00:56:03.760
and it picks up every one
of the important points

00:56:03.760 --> 00:56:06.110
of Chuck's career,
which, as you know,

00:56:06.110 --> 00:56:09.190
will come to an end as
president with this commencement

00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:12.140
after having been president
for 13 and 1/2 years.

00:56:12.140 --> 00:56:16.870
So the MIT News Office has made
that available to all of you.

00:56:16.870 --> 00:56:20.504
Now we have behind me the
generational MIT group.

00:56:20.504 --> 00:56:22.420
So we're going to first
start with you, ma'am.

00:56:22.420 --> 00:56:23.800
What is your name?

00:56:23.800 --> 00:56:25.860
[? LAPIN: ?] Marian [? Lapin. ?]

00:56:25.860 --> 00:56:27.910
KAISER: And tell us
how many generations?

00:56:27.910 --> 00:56:31.630
[? LAPIN: ?] We have three
generations in my family--

00:56:31.630 --> 00:56:38.920
my husband class of 1935,
my son class of '64,

00:56:38.920 --> 00:56:46.540
and now my grandson, who's
getting his master's in 2004.

00:56:46.540 --> 00:56:47.950
KAISER: That's unbelievable.

00:56:51.130 --> 00:56:53.170
Your husband was class of '35?

00:56:53.170 --> 00:56:55.822
[? LAPIN: ?] Yes, he was an
electrical engineer, and he

00:56:55.822 --> 00:56:59.260
worked for RCA for many years.

00:56:59.260 --> 00:57:00.890
KAISER: That was
the year I was born.

00:57:03.410 --> 00:57:05.764
May I ask how old you are?

00:57:05.764 --> 00:57:08.824
[? LAPIN: ?] Yes, 91.

00:57:08.824 --> 00:57:09.782
KAISER: How about that?

00:57:12.740 --> 00:57:15.830
May I have this dance?

00:57:15.830 --> 00:57:17.929
[? LAPIN: ?] I'd
rather play tennis.

00:57:17.929 --> 00:57:19.220
KAISER: Do you play tennis now?

00:57:19.220 --> 00:57:23.360
[? LAPIN: ?] Yes, I do, I
do, several times a week,

00:57:23.360 --> 00:57:26.114
indoors in the winter,
outdoors in the summer.

00:57:26.114 --> 00:57:27.530
KAISER: Is that
what you attribute

00:57:27.530 --> 00:57:28.447
your good health and--

00:57:28.447 --> 00:57:29.155
[? LAPIN: ?] Yes.

00:57:29.155 --> 00:57:30.050
KAISER: --to tennis?

00:57:30.050 --> 00:57:31.250
[? LAPIN: ?] Yes.

00:57:31.250 --> 00:57:33.920
KAISER: All right,
everybody, take up tennis.

00:57:33.920 --> 00:57:36.230
And this man here is her son.

00:57:36.230 --> 00:57:38.340
[? LAPIN: ?] But not the
one that went to MIT.

00:57:38.340 --> 00:57:39.680
KAISER: Yes, he went to Penn.

00:57:39.680 --> 00:57:42.110
That's Okay, Penn's
good, Penn's good.

00:57:42.110 --> 00:57:43.190
And you, ma'am?

00:57:43.190 --> 00:57:45.440
KELLER: Hi, I'm Margie
Keller, and I'm here

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:48.380
with my husband Rob Keller.

00:57:48.380 --> 00:57:53.480
Our daughter Karen Keller
is graduating in 2004.

00:57:53.480 --> 00:57:58.330
I graduated in 1968 in physics.

00:57:58.330 --> 00:58:01.870
My husband graduated in 1970--

00:58:01.870 --> 00:58:02.570
KELLER: '70.

00:58:02.570 --> 00:58:04.700
KELLER: --a PhD in physics.

00:58:04.700 --> 00:58:09.290
And my daughter's
grandfather has a master's

00:58:09.290 --> 00:58:13.800
in chemistry in 1948.

00:58:13.800 --> 00:58:15.286
KAISER: How about that?

00:58:15.286 --> 00:58:17.230
And you, ma'am?

00:58:17.230 --> 00:58:20.500
[? SHEREN: ?] Elizabeth
[? Sheren. ?] My father-in-law

00:58:20.500 --> 00:58:26.050
is class of 1930, Sloan School,
and I come back for my 30th

00:58:26.050 --> 00:58:29.140
reunion, also from
the Sloan School.

00:58:29.140 --> 00:58:32.560
And my son is graduating
today in Course 8.

00:58:36.240 --> 00:58:39.060
KAISER: All right,
folks, that's it.

00:58:39.060 --> 00:58:40.920
If I counted correctly,
there's something

00:58:40.920 --> 00:58:45.270
like 12 generations of
MIT graduates represented

00:58:45.270 --> 00:58:47.010
in this line.

00:58:47.010 --> 00:58:48.270
That's really amazing.

00:58:48.270 --> 00:58:50.550
Thank you so much,
and especially to you.

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:52.050
You are wonderful.

00:58:52.050 --> 00:58:55.320
[? LAPIN: ?] I want to know
what happened to the tar hut.

00:58:55.320 --> 00:58:58.950
I remember coming on
a fancy day in May,

00:58:58.950 --> 00:59:02.100
and young men were trying
to climb this hut that

00:59:02.100 --> 00:59:04.085
was covered with black tar.

00:59:04.085 --> 00:59:05.460
KAISER: Oh, I know
what happened.

00:59:05.460 --> 00:59:06.390
Do you know what happened?

00:59:06.390 --> 00:59:07.560
Warren will tell
you what happened.

00:59:07.560 --> 00:59:09.740
SEAMANS: [INAUDIBLE] rush,
is what that was, yeah.

00:59:09.740 --> 00:59:12.334
[? LAPIN: ?] It was funny.

00:59:12.334 --> 00:59:14.250
KAISER: Didn't somebody
get hurt or something?

00:59:14.250 --> 00:59:16.547
SEAMANS: No, tar
is carcinogenic.

00:59:16.547 --> 00:59:17.880
KAISER: Oh, tar is carcinogenic.

00:59:17.880 --> 00:59:19.110
It was bad for your health.

00:59:19.110 --> 00:59:22.760
[? LAPIN: ?] The paddles
came out the top of the roof.

00:59:22.760 --> 00:59:25.572
The paddles came out
the top of the roof.

00:59:25.572 --> 00:59:26.780
KAISER: Thank you, old folks.

00:59:35.460 --> 00:59:37.950
SEAMANS: Okay, I think we're--

00:59:37.950 --> 00:59:42.540
KAISER: MIT's motto, as Warren
told you, is "Mons et Manus"--

00:59:42.540 --> 00:59:48.720
"Mens et Manus," which
stands for "Mind and Hand."

00:59:48.720 --> 00:59:52.770
At MIT, students learn
by doing, as illustrated

00:59:52.770 --> 00:59:57.300
in these next three easy pieces.

00:59:57.300 --> 00:59:59.700
Look at the "hugeatron."

01:00:03.018 --> 01:00:03.684
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:00:03.684 --> 01:00:07.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:00:13.146 --> 01:00:16.632
- 3, 2, 1, go!

01:00:16.632 --> 01:00:20.118
[CHEERING]

01:00:25.098 --> 01:00:27.140
- Yeah!

01:00:27.140 --> 01:00:29.540
- More and more, our students
are looking for their MIT

01:00:29.540 --> 01:00:34.070
experience to be the foundation
of a career and life that

01:00:34.070 --> 01:00:35.321
has many dimensions to it.

01:00:35.321 --> 01:00:37.320
And I think we're seeing
more of those students,

01:00:37.320 --> 01:00:39.111
and I think we're
serving them much better,

01:00:39.111 --> 01:00:41.900
as we quite consciously
try to expand

01:00:41.900 --> 01:00:44.090
the range of activities
that go beyond the classroom

01:00:44.090 --> 01:00:45.620
and laboratory.

01:00:45.620 --> 01:00:47.794
- In the end, when
you see your project

01:00:47.794 --> 01:00:50.210
helping people rather than
just going up and getting dusty

01:00:50.210 --> 01:00:53.540
on a shelf somewhere, it really
inspires you and shows you

01:00:53.540 --> 01:00:55.540
what you're really
doing with your life

01:00:55.540 --> 01:00:57.140
or why you really want to learn
the engineering that you're

01:00:57.140 --> 01:00:57.960
learning.

01:00:57.960 --> 01:00:59.480
- It's a great market.

01:00:59.480 --> 01:01:02.540
It's waiting to change, and
we believe we can change it.

01:01:02.540 --> 01:01:03.740
Thank you.

01:01:03.740 --> 01:01:05.600
- One of the things
that the Institute does

01:01:05.600 --> 01:01:09.290
is it fosters, like, individual
initiative and creates

01:01:09.290 --> 01:01:13.179
opportunities for
that to happen.

01:01:13.179 --> 01:01:14.220
- Good evening, everyone.

01:01:14.220 --> 01:01:16.389
Welcome to our last Senate
meeting of the term.

01:01:16.389 --> 01:01:17.930
- Through the MIT
Washington program,

01:01:17.930 --> 01:01:22.160
I spent the summer on the Senate
floor working on legislation.

01:01:22.160 --> 01:01:24.790
And I brought that
experience back here to MIT

01:01:24.790 --> 01:01:26.660
to be the MIT
[INAUDIBLE] speaker.

01:01:26.660 --> 01:01:30.350
- Extracurricular
activities are really

01:01:30.350 --> 01:01:32.600
important in that they're
the way that students

01:01:32.600 --> 01:01:37.120
get to apply all the theory that
they learn in the classroom.

01:01:37.120 --> 01:01:42.000
- [INAUDIBLE] steps on the
[INAUDIBLE],, leans forward,

01:01:42.000 --> 01:01:44.060
and lowers himself
to the garden.

01:01:44.060 --> 01:01:47.900
- We're not simply educating
the mind of the student,

01:01:47.900 --> 01:01:49.940
but we're trying to
educate the student

01:01:49.940 --> 01:01:52.610
to be able to actually
accomplish things.

01:01:52.610 --> 01:01:54.290
- --and stand himself back up.

01:01:54.290 --> 01:01:56.000
- For the first time
since I came to MIT,

01:01:56.000 --> 01:01:59.600
I really feel like I have a
purpose beyond my four or five

01:01:59.600 --> 01:02:01.820
years here or beyond
the next problem set.

01:02:01.820 --> 01:02:07.280
- They learn incredible amounts
about leadership, teamwork,

01:02:07.280 --> 01:02:10.820
sportsmanship, and
courage, the willingness

01:02:10.820 --> 01:02:15.020
to take risks, and push
their abilities beyond what

01:02:15.020 --> 01:02:16.430
they think they can achieve.

01:02:16.430 --> 01:02:18.116
- Just being able
to deal with lots

01:02:18.116 --> 01:02:19.490
of different types
of situations.

01:02:19.490 --> 01:02:21.770
There's one type of
competence that you have

01:02:21.770 --> 01:02:23.390
and being able to
sit at a lab bench

01:02:23.390 --> 01:02:25.010
and solve a scientific problem.

01:02:25.010 --> 01:02:27.230
It's a different skill
set to be able to interact

01:02:27.230 --> 01:02:30.895
with different kinds of people
to accomplish some task.

01:02:30.895 --> 01:02:33.020
- Participating in these
extracurricular activities

01:02:33.020 --> 01:02:35.570
has given me the
confidence to lead

01:02:35.570 --> 01:02:37.250
an organization
and the experience

01:02:37.250 --> 01:02:39.380
in working with other
people that I really

01:02:39.380 --> 01:02:42.620
believe that I can walk
into any kind of a situation

01:02:42.620 --> 01:02:46.520
and have had some kind of
experience that has prepared me

01:02:46.520 --> 01:02:48.470
for anything that might arise.

01:02:48.470 --> 01:02:51.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:02:59.832 --> 01:03:04.000
- Robosnail is being developed
in the Hatsopoulos Microfluids

01:03:04.000 --> 01:03:05.440
Lab here at MIT.

01:03:05.440 --> 01:03:08.245
We're interested
in the fluids lab

01:03:08.245 --> 01:03:10.960
at studying fluids
at very small scales.

01:03:10.960 --> 01:03:14.170
Microfluidics is becoming
increasingly important today,

01:03:14.170 --> 01:03:18.340
mostly because of medical
and biological applications.

01:03:18.340 --> 01:03:20.860
People are learning
to build these labs

01:03:20.860 --> 01:03:24.070
on a chip, in which you have
fluids that flow through very

01:03:24.070 --> 01:03:26.290
small channels, maybe
chemical reactions

01:03:26.290 --> 01:03:28.529
take place in those channels.

01:03:28.529 --> 01:03:30.070
And the first thing
you need to know,

01:03:30.070 --> 01:03:32.260
if you're really going to
design these chips effectively,

01:03:32.260 --> 01:03:34.009
is you have to understand
the fluid motion

01:03:34.009 --> 01:03:35.050
at those small scales.

01:03:35.050 --> 01:03:39.400
A real snail moves by a series
of stretching and compression

01:03:39.400 --> 01:03:39.970
waves.

01:03:39.970 --> 01:03:41.440
Basically, the
only way the snail

01:03:41.440 --> 01:03:45.010
interacts with the substrate is
through a thin layer of fluid.

01:03:45.010 --> 01:03:47.590
The slime is actually
incredibly interesting.

01:03:47.590 --> 01:03:49.990
It's not a Newtonian
fluid, which

01:03:49.990 --> 01:03:52.030
means that the
characteristics of the fluid

01:03:52.030 --> 01:03:54.410
change depending on
how you stress it.

01:03:54.410 --> 01:03:57.160
And when you go to
very, very small scales,

01:03:57.160 --> 01:03:59.290
suddenly you have a
completely different set

01:03:59.290 --> 01:04:00.910
of forces that become important.

01:04:00.910 --> 01:04:03.220
And you have to learn to
either beat those forces

01:04:03.220 --> 01:04:04.800
or use them to your advantage.

01:04:04.800 --> 01:04:06.460
Robosnail is answering
the question of,

01:04:06.460 --> 01:04:09.010
how can we use these high
pressures in thin films

01:04:09.010 --> 01:04:10.960
to actually get something
to propel itself?

01:04:10.960 --> 01:04:11.806
- From back here?

01:04:11.806 --> 01:04:12.305
- Okay.

01:04:12.305 --> 01:04:12.980
- But the--

01:04:12.980 --> 01:04:16.360
- The current version of
Robosnail is a prototype.

01:04:16.360 --> 01:04:18.490
We built him mostly
as a proof of concept

01:04:18.490 --> 01:04:20.650
to show that you really
can generate forward

01:04:20.650 --> 01:04:23.120
momentum using the
pressures that build up

01:04:23.120 --> 01:04:24.340
in these lubricating layers.

01:04:24.340 --> 01:04:25.130
- Upside down?

01:04:25.130 --> 01:04:25.630
- Yeah.

01:04:25.630 --> 01:04:25.810
- Okay.

01:04:25.810 --> 01:04:27.590
- I haven't really gone
through it with Brian yet

01:04:27.590 --> 01:04:28.298
to know what he--

01:04:28.298 --> 01:04:31.630
- Robosnail was really
initiated and designed

01:04:31.630 --> 01:04:33.900
by a graduate
student, Brian Chan.

01:04:33.900 --> 01:04:35.650
Brian is one of the
most creative students

01:04:35.650 --> 01:04:37.390
I've ever come across.

01:04:37.390 --> 01:04:39.700
The guy can build
anything mechanical.

01:04:39.700 --> 01:04:42.400
You give him-- you tell
him, I need something that

01:04:42.400 --> 01:04:44.290
walks on water, he'll build it.

01:04:44.290 --> 01:04:46.220
- Last year I had to take--

01:04:46.220 --> 01:04:49.330
I was taking a
fluids course, and we

01:04:49.330 --> 01:04:51.270
had to do a course project.

01:04:51.270 --> 01:04:56.430
And I saw some web pages on
water snails and how they move,

01:04:56.430 --> 01:05:00.910
and I decided to
make a robosnail.

01:05:00.910 --> 01:05:03.310
I just went to California
and took a look

01:05:03.310 --> 01:05:08.200
at the banana slugs, which
are these huge slugs.

01:05:08.200 --> 01:05:10.610
They grow to like 7 or 8 inches.

01:05:10.610 --> 01:05:13.970
And since they're so big, you
could see what's going on.

01:05:13.970 --> 01:05:18.010
And if you look closely at the
bottom, you could see that--

01:05:18.010 --> 01:05:20.230
you could see that they
also have these compression

01:05:20.230 --> 01:05:23.600
waves, which is actually how I
got the idea for this machine.

01:05:23.600 --> 01:05:25.600
- We are extremely good
at designing things

01:05:25.600 --> 01:05:26.890
that move on flat surfaces.

01:05:26.890 --> 01:05:28.660
If we build a
road, we can design

01:05:28.660 --> 01:05:30.090
a vehicle that goes on it.

01:05:30.090 --> 01:05:32.849
But snails can climb
up a vertical wall,

01:05:32.849 --> 01:05:34.390
and that's something
that we have not

01:05:34.390 --> 01:05:35.621
been able to design yet.

01:05:35.621 --> 01:05:37.120
And that's something
where, I think,

01:05:37.120 --> 01:05:39.700
we can learn a lot from looking
at these natural systems.

01:05:39.700 --> 01:05:42.235
- I noticed that a
lot of times people,

01:05:42.235 --> 01:05:45.130
they think of bugs or snails,
and they just get grossed out,

01:05:45.130 --> 01:05:46.400
and they don't really care.

01:05:46.400 --> 01:05:49.870
But if we take the time
to actually observe

01:05:49.870 --> 01:05:52.702
these creatures,
we can learn a lot.

01:05:52.702 --> 01:05:54.160
- It's taking a
biological creature

01:05:54.160 --> 01:05:56.380
that, after all this
time, still exists,

01:05:56.380 --> 01:05:59.080
and so his motion must
have some advantage to him,

01:05:59.080 --> 01:06:01.810
or it would have fallen out
of the evolutionary chain.

01:06:01.810 --> 01:06:04.180
And we're looking at
it, saying, okay, well,

01:06:04.180 --> 01:06:06.760
if it has applications
in real life,

01:06:06.760 --> 01:06:08.325
then maybe there's
a way to model

01:06:08.325 --> 01:06:10.033
it to have applications
in something else

01:06:10.033 --> 01:06:12.088
that we want to do later on.

01:06:12.088 --> 01:06:14.110
[END PLAYBACK]

01:06:14.110 --> 01:06:14.860
KAISER: All right.

01:06:14.860 --> 01:06:18.050
I just looked at my watch, and
my watch says that it is 9:23.

01:06:20.720 --> 01:06:23.240
The guests of honor are
beginning to process,

01:06:23.240 --> 01:06:26.890
and they will be here
in about seven minutes.

01:06:26.890 --> 01:06:28.620
You could take a look
up on the screen,

01:06:28.620 --> 01:06:31.370
and you'll see them
getting ready to come here

01:06:31.370 --> 01:06:33.020
to Killian Court.

01:06:33.020 --> 01:06:41.060
This is a good time for me to
outline some of the protocols

01:06:41.060 --> 01:06:43.250
when they come.

01:06:43.250 --> 01:06:46.220
First of all, we
know that each of you

01:06:46.220 --> 01:06:50.210
is going to be very anxious
to get the best possible view

01:06:50.210 --> 01:06:53.600
or photograph of your graduate.

01:06:53.600 --> 01:06:57.770
But I urge you to be
considerate of the fellow guests

01:06:57.770 --> 01:06:59.060
around you.

01:06:59.060 --> 01:07:05.270
Please do not stand on the
chairs to take pictures.

01:07:05.270 --> 01:07:07.610
We've had people
in the past do that

01:07:07.610 --> 01:07:10.160
and they've actually
hurt themselves,

01:07:10.160 --> 01:07:12.350
and we don't really want
that to happen to you.

01:07:12.350 --> 01:07:16.760
So whatever you do,
don't step on the chairs.

01:07:16.760 --> 01:07:19.760
And also, remember when
you're taking pictures

01:07:19.760 --> 01:07:22.070
to be mindful of the
people around you

01:07:22.070 --> 01:07:25.640
so that you don't block
their view as well.

01:07:25.640 --> 01:07:28.010
So you might take a moment
now to introduce yourself

01:07:28.010 --> 01:07:30.590
to those people around
you if you don't know them

01:07:30.590 --> 01:07:33.440
so that you can work this
out among yourselves.

01:07:37.910 --> 01:07:40.940
We would also like to
request that you now

01:07:40.940 --> 01:07:46.190
take the time to put your cell
phones in a non-audible mode.

01:07:50.950 --> 01:07:54.490
In the unlikely-- and we
hope it doesn't occur--

01:07:54.490 --> 01:07:58.550
in the unlikely event that
you'll need medical attention,

01:07:58.550 --> 01:08:03.377
the medical tent is located
at the rear of Killian Court.

01:08:08.250 --> 01:08:14.720
The words to the national
anthem and the MIT school song

01:08:14.720 --> 01:08:19.020
are printed in the commencement
program, in addition to

01:08:19.020 --> 01:08:22.890
some helpful information
regarding logistics.

01:08:22.890 --> 01:08:25.620
So you might want to
take a look at that.

01:08:25.620 --> 01:08:27.060
There will be some
people who will

01:08:27.060 --> 01:08:30.644
be wearing red jackets
in the procession, a very

01:08:30.644 --> 01:08:31.810
interesting group of people.

01:08:31.810 --> 01:08:36.899
And Warren Seamans will
tell you about them.

01:08:36.899 --> 01:08:38.670
SEAMANS: The red
jackets, which you

01:08:38.670 --> 01:08:42.090
will see I am wearing one
of those red jackets--

01:08:42.090 --> 01:08:43.830
I'll tell you why
I am in a minute--

01:08:43.830 --> 01:08:46.590
but these mark
the 50-year class.

01:08:46.590 --> 01:08:49.050
You can only wear
the red jacket,

01:08:49.050 --> 01:08:54.660
the cardinal red jacket, when
you reach the 50-year class.

01:08:54.660 --> 01:08:58.529
And so as part of the
procession coming in today,

01:08:58.529 --> 01:09:04.490
there will be members
of the class of '50--

01:09:04.490 --> 01:09:04.990
KAISER: 4.

01:09:04.990 --> 01:09:07.740
SEAMANS: --'54,
coming in as a group,

01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:11.819
and they will be
seated as a group.

01:09:11.819 --> 01:09:15.210
You can become an
honorary member of a class

01:09:15.210 --> 01:09:17.720
and thereby wear a red jacket.

01:09:17.720 --> 01:09:21.819
I very proudly wear my
class of 1935 jacket

01:09:21.819 --> 01:09:23.700
because I'm an honorary
member of that class.

01:09:23.700 --> 01:09:27.670
KAISER: [INAUDIBLE]

01:09:27.670 --> 01:09:29.930
SEAMANS: The class
coming in today

01:09:29.930 --> 01:09:33.994
includes [? Alec ?] Dreyfus
and our former president

01:09:33.994 --> 01:09:36.490
and chairman of the
Corporation, Paul Gray.

01:09:39.279 --> 01:09:41.080
KAISER: Thank you
very much, Warren.

01:09:41.080 --> 01:09:42.580
I'm going to say
goodbye to you all.

01:09:42.580 --> 01:09:45.644
For Warren and myself, it's
been great visiting with you.

01:09:45.644 --> 01:09:47.560
And now we're going to
introduce one last tape

01:09:47.560 --> 01:09:52.450
and then say good-bye
for Commencement 2004.

01:09:52.450 --> 01:09:55.540
MIT has been visited by
many interesting folks

01:09:55.540 --> 01:09:57.820
over these past years.

01:09:57.820 --> 01:10:00.760
Here is a sampling
and a compilation

01:10:00.760 --> 01:10:03.750
we call "Voices at MIT."

01:10:06.752 --> 01:10:07.418
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:10:07.418 --> 01:10:10.262
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:10:12.160 --> 01:10:15.500
- MIT is admired
around the world

01:10:15.500 --> 01:10:20.660
as a crucible of creative
thought, a force for progress,

01:10:20.660 --> 01:10:25.260
a place where dreams of
generations become reality.

01:10:25.260 --> 01:10:31.080
MIT has done much to make
this the American century.

01:10:31.080 --> 01:10:36.120
- The Boston area boasts of
several excellent institutions

01:10:36.120 --> 01:10:42.780
of higher learning, but
there is only one MIT.

01:10:42.780 --> 01:10:44.970
- MIT has shown a
standard of excellence

01:10:44.970 --> 01:10:49.320
in education and research
that sets a benchmark

01:10:49.320 --> 01:10:52.290
for universities everywhere.

01:10:52.290 --> 01:10:56.260
- [SPEAKING CHINESE]

01:10:56.260 --> 01:11:00.055
- Right now, China is very
short of talented managers.

01:11:00.055 --> 01:11:01.570
What we would like
to see more than

01:11:01.570 --> 01:11:04.150
anything else is
to see MIT helping

01:11:04.150 --> 01:11:07.510
us to train these high-level
managerial persons.

01:11:07.510 --> 01:11:13.544
- I promise I will pay
what you are paid here.

01:11:13.544 --> 01:11:15.480
[LAUGHTER]

01:11:15.480 --> 01:11:18.384
[APPLAUSE]

01:11:18.384 --> 01:11:19.352
Thank you.

01:11:22.270 --> 01:11:24.500
- I personally,
and Microsoft, have

01:11:24.500 --> 01:11:27.800
been an incredible beneficiary
of the kind of work

01:11:27.800 --> 01:11:31.640
that goes on here and in
other places like this.

01:11:31.640 --> 01:11:35.090
Here at MIT, over
50 years ago there

01:11:35.090 --> 01:11:38.780
was a vision laid
down by Vannevar Bush

01:11:38.780 --> 01:11:45.720
that we're probably about, oh,
20% of the way to achieving.

01:11:45.720 --> 01:11:48.810
- I can't tell you how much
I have personally benefited

01:11:48.810 --> 01:11:50.640
from this institution.

01:11:50.640 --> 01:11:53.160
As part of this
wonderful campaign,

01:11:53.160 --> 01:11:56.500
I would like to put $100
million on the table.

01:11:56.500 --> 01:12:01.350
[APPLAUSE]

01:12:01.350 --> 01:12:04.810
- If one is unfortunate
enough to be disabled,

01:12:04.810 --> 01:12:08.446
this is the age
in which to be so.

01:12:08.446 --> 01:12:13.950
The disabled have a lot
to thank technology for.

01:12:13.950 --> 01:12:16.670
I probably wouldn't
have survived,

01:12:16.670 --> 01:12:18.570
and I certainly
wouldn't have been

01:12:18.570 --> 01:12:21.010
able to write a
best-selling book

01:12:21.010 --> 01:12:25.140
if I had been born any earlier.

01:12:25.140 --> 01:12:28.140
- Managing innovation
and figuring out

01:12:28.140 --> 01:12:32.780
how to actually turn
good ideas into products

01:12:32.780 --> 01:12:36.060
is a really interesting
thing that you probably don't

01:12:36.060 --> 01:12:38.200
learn about in universities.

01:12:38.200 --> 01:12:41.380
It's not about just technology.

01:12:41.380 --> 01:12:43.020
It's about the way
people are treated.

01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:47.010
It's about how you
motivate people.

01:12:47.010 --> 01:12:50.490
- What was that inspiration
which led suddenly to the world

01:12:50.490 --> 01:12:51.746
wide web?

01:12:51.746 --> 01:12:54.370
For me, I wanted the thing to be
a project management tool that

01:12:54.370 --> 01:12:57.090
allows all to work together, not
management from the top down,

01:12:57.090 --> 01:12:59.610
but management in there
working together way.

01:12:59.610 --> 01:13:01.890
So I then said, it
should be intercreative,

01:13:01.890 --> 01:13:05.430
universal space for
people to communicate

01:13:05.430 --> 01:13:07.950
through sharing knowledge.

01:13:07.950 --> 01:13:13.110
- We are at the dawn of
a true innovation age.

01:13:13.110 --> 01:13:17.490
It's estimated that the entire
store of human knowledge

01:13:17.490 --> 01:13:20.610
now doubles every five years.

01:13:20.610 --> 01:13:25.260
- I would like to thank MIT for
this incredible honor, which

01:13:25.260 --> 01:13:28.460
is, I have to say, the first
academic honor I've ever

01:13:28.460 --> 01:13:29.930
received.

01:13:29.930 --> 01:13:32.090
So it's nice to
start at the top.

01:13:32.090 --> 01:13:35.780
This is such a great
institution dedicated

01:13:35.780 --> 01:13:40.840
to the search for the new and
to the openness of thought

01:13:40.840 --> 01:13:43.040
that that search requires.

01:13:43.040 --> 01:13:47.150
- This case is the
great test of where

01:13:47.150 --> 01:13:51.290
we stand on the issue of
freedom and solidarity

01:13:51.290 --> 01:13:54.750
and the future of
our own culture.

01:13:54.750 --> 01:13:57.680
- For in the end, I believe
the real challenge of history

01:13:57.680 --> 01:13:59.720
is to resist the
tendency so prevalent

01:13:59.720 --> 01:14:02.330
today to label, to
stereotype, to expose,

01:14:02.330 --> 01:14:05.150
to denigrate, and instead
to bring common sense

01:14:05.150 --> 01:14:08.450
and empathy to our subjects
so that the past can truly

01:14:08.450 --> 01:14:10.970
come alive, if only
for a few moments,

01:14:10.970 --> 01:14:15.360
in all of its beauty, glory,
sadness, and complexity.

01:14:15.360 --> 01:14:17.360
- Young people
like you are always

01:14:17.360 --> 01:14:19.940
the vanguard of any
social movement,

01:14:19.940 --> 01:14:23.660
setting an energetic tone
of courage and commitment.

01:14:23.660 --> 01:14:27.320
And now we need
you to, once again,

01:14:27.320 --> 01:14:32.780
lead us to a higher
and more noble destiny.

01:14:32.780 --> 01:14:37.070
- 21st-century America
belongs to you.

01:14:37.070 --> 01:14:38.830
Take good care of it.

01:14:38.830 --> 01:14:40.300
Thank you and God bless you.

01:14:40.300 --> 01:14:43.779
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:14:48.252 --> 01:14:50.531
[END PLAYBACK]

01:14:50.531 --> 01:14:51.780
KAISER: Hello again, everyone.

01:14:51.780 --> 01:14:53.860
I'm now talking to
Larry Isaacson who

01:14:53.860 --> 01:14:56.890
is the director of Mass Brass.

01:14:56.890 --> 01:14:59.320
The Mass Brass is
the ensemble who

01:14:59.320 --> 01:15:03.430
will be providing the music for
the commencement this morning.

01:15:03.430 --> 01:15:10.480
And Larry is currently assistant
director of the music division

01:15:10.480 --> 01:15:11.980
at the Boston Conservatory.

01:15:11.980 --> 01:15:16.060
He was actually on the faculty
of MIT, on the staff of MIT,

01:15:16.060 --> 01:15:17.370
from 19--

01:15:17.370 --> 01:15:17.955
what, 1990?

01:15:17.955 --> 01:15:20.517
ISAACSON: 1990 to 2000.

01:15:20.517 --> 01:15:22.600
KAISER: And now you're at
the Boston Conservatory.

01:15:22.600 --> 01:15:23.710
And what are your duties there?

01:15:23.710 --> 01:15:25.450
ISAACSON: Well, actually,
I produce their concerts.

01:15:25.450 --> 01:15:26.866
There are about
30 concerts a year

01:15:26.866 --> 01:15:29.410
that we do, both orchestra,
wind ensemble, musical theater,

01:15:29.410 --> 01:15:32.585
dance, opera, and I
oversee all of those.

01:15:32.585 --> 01:15:34.960
KAISER: I wonder if you could
tell the folks a little bit

01:15:34.960 --> 01:15:36.376
about the music
that they're going

01:15:36.376 --> 01:15:39.640
to be hearing as the
graduates process

01:15:39.640 --> 01:15:40.724
into Killian Court, Larry.

01:15:40.724 --> 01:15:41.348
ISAACSON: Sure.

01:15:41.348 --> 01:15:43.474
We put together a really
good combination of music,

01:15:43.474 --> 01:15:44.890
things that you
would be familiar,

01:15:44.890 --> 01:15:47.620
things like Copland and
Anderson and Gershwin,

01:15:47.620 --> 01:15:49.900
and also music that's been
written either for the MIT

01:15:49.900 --> 01:15:52.580
Brass Ensemble or for this
graduation in particular.

01:15:52.580 --> 01:15:55.775
We had Kevin [? Kaska ?] write
a piece a few years ago for us,

01:15:55.775 --> 01:15:57.400
and we actually will
do that as well as

01:15:57.400 --> 01:16:00.160
part of the processional for
the students, in addition

01:16:00.160 --> 01:16:01.310
to Pomp and Circumstance.

01:16:01.310 --> 01:16:03.434
So it's a lot of familiar,
a lot of older things,

01:16:03.434 --> 01:16:05.850
and a lot of new things that
we sort of introduce as well.

01:16:05.850 --> 01:16:07.150
Should be very enjoyable.

01:16:07.150 --> 01:16:09.441
KAISER: I understand that
there's something of your own

01:16:09.441 --> 01:16:11.320
in this program as well.

01:16:11.320 --> 01:16:13.780
ISAACSON: Yes, the fanfare
when the color guard comes in.

01:16:13.780 --> 01:16:14.946
It's actually John Williams.

01:16:14.946 --> 01:16:15.760
I didn't write it.

01:16:15.760 --> 01:16:17.660
I arranged it for this grouping.

01:16:17.660 --> 01:16:20.200
It's his Olympic Fanfare,
one of his Olympic Fanfares,

01:16:20.200 --> 01:16:22.120
and we'll use that for
the color guard today.

01:16:22.120 --> 01:16:23.869
KAISER: I'll be looking
forward to hearing

01:16:23.869 --> 01:16:26.470
that, because, as you know, I
play a brass instrument myself,

01:16:26.470 --> 01:16:29.620
and I don't think there's a
lovelier sound than brass.

01:16:29.620 --> 01:16:31.580
Tell me, what are
you going to do next?

01:16:31.580 --> 01:16:33.121
ISAACSON: Well, I'm
going to head off

01:16:33.121 --> 01:16:35.890
to Aspen in about a week and
do their July 4th concert.

01:16:35.890 --> 01:16:38.650
It's only-- I gather there's
10,000 people here today-- it's

01:16:38.650 --> 01:16:40.410
only 5,000 in that audience.

01:16:40.410 --> 01:16:41.755
It's a small audience.

01:16:41.755 --> 01:16:43.900
KAISER: A comedown,
but not much of one.

01:16:43.900 --> 01:16:47.380
Larry, it's always a pleasure
to hear you and the Mass Brass

01:16:47.380 --> 01:16:49.060
and to hear your
wonderful music.

01:16:49.060 --> 01:16:51.557
What would commencement
be without it?

01:16:51.557 --> 01:16:52.390
Thank you very much.

01:16:52.390 --> 01:16:52.870
ISAACSON: Thank you, Jay.

01:16:52.870 --> 01:16:54.370
KAISER: And the
stage is now yours.

01:17:13.831 --> 01:17:17.324
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS]

01:27:05.530 --> 01:27:10.240
ANNOUNCER: Ladies and gentlemen,
the academic procession

01:27:10.240 --> 01:27:14.840
will enter Killian Court
in about 10 minutes.

01:27:14.840 --> 01:27:21.887
At this time, please welcome the
MIT Campus Police Color Guard.

01:27:32.366 --> 01:27:35.859
[MUSIC - JOHN WILLIAMS, "OLYMPIC
 FANFARE"]

01:29:43.104 --> 01:29:46.597
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS]

01:38:40.382 --> 01:38:45.160
Ladies and gentlemen,
the academic procession,

01:38:45.160 --> 01:38:49.654
led by the Chief Marshal,
will now enter Killian Court.

01:38:56.128 --> 01:38:59.614
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS]

01:39:53.400 --> 01:39:54.750
SEAMANS: Welcome to the--

01:39:54.750 --> 01:39:56.720
can you hear me, Larry?

01:39:56.720 --> 01:40:00.150
OK, OK.

01:40:00.150 --> 01:40:07.050
Welcome to our 136th
MIT Commencement.

01:40:07.050 --> 01:40:11.340
The first one in 1868
had 13 graduates.

01:40:11.340 --> 01:40:13.040
We have a few more
than that today.

01:40:13.040 --> 01:40:16.435
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS]

01:40:26.620 --> 01:40:29.110
The president of the
MIT Alumni Association

01:40:29.110 --> 01:40:33.310
carries the mace, which
is the symbolic instrument

01:40:33.310 --> 01:40:36.491
for the Institute.

01:40:36.491 --> 01:40:41.014
There's Chuck Vest,
current president.

01:40:41.014 --> 01:40:44.558
KAISER: There's Rafael Bras
who's the chair of the faculty.

01:40:48.941 --> 01:40:52.490
And this is what-- did you
mention it's the 138th?

01:40:52.490 --> 01:40:53.867
SEAMANS: 136th.

01:40:53.867 --> 01:40:54.450
KAISER: 136th.

01:40:58.860 --> 01:41:01.065
SEAMANS: It's hard to tell
because in 1944, there

01:41:01.065 --> 01:41:03.160
were two graduations
during World War II.

01:41:03.160 --> 01:41:03.910
KAISER: Ah, right.

01:41:03.910 --> 01:41:07.784
SEAMANS: So it's
either 136 or 137.

01:41:07.784 --> 01:41:13.650
KAISER: President Vest is
wearing the recently minted MIT

01:41:13.650 --> 01:41:16.310
gown, the crimson and gray.

01:41:28.990 --> 01:41:34.430
This year, there are going
to be 2,205 undergraduates

01:41:34.430 --> 01:41:40.230
and graduate students scheduled
to receive 1,114 bachelor's

01:41:40.230 --> 01:41:49.927
degrees, 1,161 master's, 211
doctorates, and 10 engineering

01:41:49.927 --> 01:41:50.427
degrees.

01:42:00.407 --> 01:42:03.860
SEAMANS: This is the procession
of the faculty coming in

01:42:03.860 --> 01:42:06.034
to be seated on the stage.

01:42:06.034 --> 01:42:14.160
The stage is covered by a huge
sail made by the MIT ocean

01:42:14.160 --> 01:42:16.532
engineering faculty.

01:42:16.532 --> 01:42:18.490
KAISER: There's a glimpse
of all of the degrees

01:42:18.490 --> 01:42:20.220
that are going to be handed out.

01:42:20.220 --> 01:42:23.040
And you can see what a logistic
problem it is to get them

01:42:23.040 --> 01:42:24.900
all stacked up in
the right order

01:42:24.900 --> 01:42:27.570
so that when their
student comes up

01:42:27.570 --> 01:42:31.430
onto the platform as her
name is pronounced, that you

01:42:31.430 --> 01:42:34.780
gets the right diploma.

01:42:34.780 --> 01:42:36.700
SEAMANS: That close-up
of the mace you saw

01:42:36.700 --> 01:42:41.320
has the beaver, MIT's mascot,
on its top, and the bottom of it

01:42:41.320 --> 01:42:42.625
is an acorn.

01:42:42.625 --> 01:42:44.500
KAISER: You're looking
at Steve Lerman there.

01:42:44.500 --> 01:42:49.630
He is the master
of the new graduate

01:42:49.630 --> 01:42:55.120
dorm on Albany Street, very
active member of the MIT

01:42:55.120 --> 01:42:57.364
community.

01:42:57.364 --> 01:43:01.450
SEAMANS: And there's a
partial view of the audience

01:43:01.450 --> 01:43:04.240
for the commencement ceremony.

01:43:04.240 --> 01:43:07.510
Most of those people started
gathering here about 7:30

01:43:07.510 --> 01:43:08.470
this morning.

01:43:08.470 --> 01:43:11.440
So they've been out there
for, most of them, for two

01:43:11.440 --> 01:43:15.272
to two and a half hours already.

01:43:15.272 --> 01:43:18.146
KAISER: The sail
works as, of course,

01:43:18.146 --> 01:43:21.990
a protection from the sun
when you're sitting there.

01:43:21.990 --> 01:43:25.460
But it's also, in
the past it's acted

01:43:25.460 --> 01:43:28.520
as an umbrella when
there have been

01:43:28.520 --> 01:43:32.487
really inclement commencements.

01:43:32.487 --> 01:43:34.070
SEAMANS: We should
point out that this

01:43:34.070 --> 01:43:36.790
is sort of a miracle day.

01:43:36.790 --> 01:43:39.250
The last two weeks have
been almost continuous rain

01:43:39.250 --> 01:43:43.090
here in the Boston area, and
yesterday it rained off and on

01:43:43.090 --> 01:43:44.420
most of the day.

01:43:44.420 --> 01:43:50.290
Yet today came forth just this
beautiful, cool, wonderful day.

01:43:50.290 --> 01:43:51.970
Rain is predicted
again for tomorrow.

01:43:51.970 --> 01:43:59.041
So it's this sun is shine
brightly on MIT today.

01:43:59.041 --> 01:43:59.540
KAISER: Yes.

01:43:59.540 --> 01:44:03.532
I mean, if the weather were
traffic, MIT would be--

01:44:03.532 --> 01:44:05.496
let's say if the weather
were New York traffic,

01:44:05.496 --> 01:44:12.370
MIT would be a New Yorker
trying to get across the street.

01:44:12.370 --> 01:44:16.530
We've managed to dodge
so many different storm

01:44:16.530 --> 01:44:18.175
systems yesterday and today.

01:44:18.175 --> 01:44:20.000
We had yesterday the
Hooding Ceremony,

01:44:20.000 --> 01:44:22.010
and it rained before it.

01:44:22.010 --> 01:44:25.070
Then when the ceremony
started, the sun shone.

01:44:25.070 --> 01:44:28.173
And when the ceremony was over,
the clouds came back in again,

01:44:28.173 --> 01:44:29.480
and it rained some more.

01:44:29.480 --> 01:44:33.660
And just look at the
sky above the dome now.

01:44:33.660 --> 01:44:35.640
There isn't a cloud in the sky.

01:44:35.640 --> 01:44:38.520
I can't remember in recent
years a commencement which has

01:44:38.520 --> 01:44:40.945
had a cloudless sky like this.

01:44:40.945 --> 01:44:43.320
SEAMANS: For those of you who
may be watching who are not

01:44:43.320 --> 01:44:48.290
familiar with MIT, this is MIT's
Great Court, Killian Court,

01:44:48.290 --> 01:44:53.310
which was dedicated
in 1916, 88 years ago.

01:44:53.310 --> 01:44:57.030
And it has traditionally
served as MIT's open living

01:44:57.030 --> 01:45:01.470
room, where everything from
graduations, commencements,

01:45:01.470 --> 01:45:03.150
to inauguration
of new presidents,

01:45:03.150 --> 01:45:06.420
to scientific happenings,
to celebrations

01:45:06.420 --> 01:45:14.580
of the end of World War II,
just whatever has been happening

01:45:14.580 --> 01:45:17.940
is sort of reflected by
the events in the Great

01:45:17.940 --> 01:45:19.380
Court, Killian Court.

01:45:19.380 --> 01:45:20.280
KAISER: Yes.

01:45:20.280 --> 01:45:24.360
Now, there is the president
of MIT, Chuck Vest.

01:45:24.360 --> 01:45:31.680
As I said, this is going to be
probably his last commencement

01:45:31.680 --> 01:45:32.820
as president.

01:45:32.820 --> 01:45:37.380
Of course, everything depends
on who the new president is

01:45:37.380 --> 01:45:39.540
and when the new
president can come.

01:45:39.540 --> 01:45:42.180
But Chuck announced
several months ago

01:45:42.180 --> 01:45:43.390
that he was stepping down.

01:45:43.390 --> 01:45:49.535
Right now, he's talking
to Elias Zerhouni,

01:45:49.535 --> 01:45:52.763
who is director of the
National Institutes of Health.

01:45:59.665 --> 01:46:08.170
And Dr. Zerhouni will provide
the principal address.

01:46:08.170 --> 01:46:11.430
SEAMANS: Now you see
entering the 50-year class

01:46:11.430 --> 01:46:12.550
with their red jackets.

01:46:12.550 --> 01:46:15.560
This is a symbol
of MIT's longevity.

01:46:15.560 --> 01:46:19.700
But since 1966,
the 50-year class

01:46:19.700 --> 01:46:23.144
has been allowed to wear
the cardinal red jacket.

01:46:23.144 --> 01:46:26.096
So these are all members
of the class of '50--

01:46:26.096 --> 01:46:28.556
'54 coming in.

01:46:28.556 --> 01:46:31.759
KAISER: In that
previous shot, kind

01:46:31.759 --> 01:46:35.780
of a poignant
picture there, that

01:46:35.780 --> 01:46:40.100
was of Paul Gray,
who is now taking

01:46:40.100 --> 01:46:44.780
his seat with the other
50-year [? corps. ?]

01:46:44.780 --> 01:46:49.330
Paul has, of course,
spent, I think,

01:46:49.330 --> 01:46:56.150
41 years attending commencements
at MIT sitting on this stage.

01:46:56.150 --> 01:46:59.735
And now this will
be the first one

01:46:59.735 --> 01:47:03.354
that he'll spend sitting
with his comrades.

01:47:03.354 --> 01:47:04.770
SEAMANS: There's
[? Phil ?] Curry,

01:47:04.770 --> 01:47:10.180
who is the dean of the School of
Humanities and Social Studies.

01:47:10.180 --> 01:47:11.200
KAISER: Social Sciences.

01:47:11.200 --> 01:47:12.241
SEAMANS: Social Sciences.

01:47:12.241 --> 01:47:15.910
KAISER: And there
is Tom Magnanti,

01:47:15.910 --> 01:47:18.760
who's dean of the
School of Engineering,

01:47:18.760 --> 01:47:21.810
with the orange tie.

01:47:21.810 --> 01:47:26.130
And who's that?

01:47:26.130 --> 01:47:29.460
That's the dean of
the Sloan School.

01:47:29.460 --> 01:47:34.810
I believe that's Dick
Schmalensee, Dick Schmalensee.

01:47:34.810 --> 01:47:37.470
So there are three deans,
three of the five deans,

01:47:37.470 --> 01:47:39.630
and the chair of the
faculty in the lower left.

01:47:42.200 --> 01:47:46.890
There's Eleanor Westney,
the woman in the orange cap.

01:47:46.890 --> 01:47:48.540
She is also Sloan School.

01:47:48.540 --> 01:47:51.060
Her specialty is Japan.

01:47:51.060 --> 01:47:51.567
And--

01:47:51.567 --> 01:47:52.900
ANNOUNCER: Ladies and gentlemen,

01:47:52.900 --> 01:47:54.450
KAISER: --she's
going to go back to--

01:47:54.450 --> 01:47:54.750
ANNOUNCER: --the guests--

01:47:54.750 --> 01:47:55.700
KAISER: --Japan next year--

01:47:55.700 --> 01:47:55.890
ANNOUNCER: --of honor--

01:47:55.890 --> 01:47:57.098
KAISER: --for her sabbatical.

01:47:57.098 --> 01:48:00.120
ANNOUNCER: --the Class of 2004.

01:48:00.120 --> 01:48:02.590
SEAMANS: We're just about
to have the procession

01:48:02.590 --> 01:48:05.450
of the Class of 2004.

01:48:05.450 --> 01:48:10.895
They come in from Memorial
Drive on to the stage.

01:48:10.895 --> 01:48:14.360
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS]

01:48:20.795 --> 01:48:22.760
KAISER: Now, I know
on the right there

01:48:22.760 --> 01:48:30.288
is Larry Benedict, who
is the dean for Student

01:48:30.288 --> 01:48:33.282
Affairs at MIT.

01:48:33.282 --> 01:48:36.276
And although he's
shaded, I can't tell yet,

01:48:36.276 --> 01:48:37.773
that should be--

01:48:37.773 --> 01:48:42.264
to Larry's right that
should be Bob Redwine.

01:48:42.264 --> 01:48:47.254
But it doesn't look
like Bob, who is--

01:48:47.254 --> 01:48:54.240
Bob Redwine is the dean of
Undergraduate Education.

01:48:54.240 --> 01:48:55.238
It's getting closer.

01:49:01.226 --> 01:49:06.880
Those are the two deans,
the two most important deans

01:49:06.880 --> 01:49:11.310
in the undergraduate
life of these students--

01:49:11.310 --> 01:49:13.927
Bob Redwine on the screen
left and Larry Benedict,

01:49:13.927 --> 01:49:14.652
screen right.

01:49:17.550 --> 01:49:20.560
A terrific duo.

01:49:20.560 --> 01:49:23.580
They've been in their offices
now, I guess, for three years,

01:49:23.580 --> 01:49:27.785
and they've just done an
absolutely splendid job.

01:49:27.785 --> 01:49:32.630
MIT is incredibly lucky
to have two gifted--

01:49:32.630 --> 01:49:35.030
SEAMANS: The group
standing on either side,

01:49:35.030 --> 01:49:40.310
but you can see to the
right now, are the families.

01:49:40.310 --> 01:49:42.361
This morning we found
that there were people

01:49:42.361 --> 01:49:47.275
from as far away as Perth,
Australia and Argentina,

01:49:47.275 --> 01:49:48.760
India, of course.

01:49:48.760 --> 01:49:50.492
People from around
the world have

01:49:50.492 --> 01:49:52.225
come for this glorious event.

01:50:15.500 --> 01:50:18.930
KAISER: Other
speakers at the event

01:50:18.930 --> 01:50:24.390
today, beside President
Vest and Elias Zerhouni,

01:50:24.390 --> 01:50:28.110
will include the president of
the Graduate Student Council,

01:50:28.110 --> 01:50:30.750
Erich Caulfield,
and Maria Hidalgo,

01:50:30.750 --> 01:50:33.004
who's president of
the Class of 2004, who

01:50:33.004 --> 01:50:36.053
will present the class gift.

01:50:36.053 --> 01:50:44.950
The Invocation will be delivered
by another MIT long-timer,

01:50:44.950 --> 01:50:47.980
Reverend Robert M. Randolph,
who is Senior Associate

01:50:47.980 --> 01:50:52.270
Dean for Students at MIT and an
affiliate minister at Harvard

01:50:52.270 --> 01:50:53.660
University's Memorial Church.

01:51:00.569 --> 01:51:02.110
SEAMANS: We should
indicate, perhaps,

01:51:02.110 --> 01:51:04.290
that the commentary
as we are doing it,

01:51:04.290 --> 01:51:09.050
in case you've joined recently,
is being given by Jay Kaiser

01:51:09.050 --> 01:51:12.566
and by Warren Seamans, myself.

01:51:12.566 --> 01:51:14.087
KAISER: Yes, I'm Jay Kaiser.

01:51:14.087 --> 01:51:16.522
And to my right is--

01:51:16.522 --> 01:51:19.444
SEAMANS: Warren Seamans.

01:51:19.444 --> 01:51:20.418
KAISER: Warren was--

01:51:29.680 --> 01:51:33.230
Warren was director of the MIT
Museum for many, many years

01:51:33.230 --> 01:51:37.060
before he retired in 1996.

01:51:37.060 --> 01:51:43.398
Warren retired two years
before I did, and both of us

01:51:43.398 --> 01:51:47.390
can attest to the
glories of retirement.

01:51:47.390 --> 01:51:50.883
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS, "POMP AND
 CIRCUMSTANCE"]

01:51:53.378 --> 01:51:56.372
And there's the
mace, the MIT mace,

01:51:56.372 --> 01:51:59.865
standing up above
the crowd there.

01:51:59.865 --> 01:52:03.358
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS, "POMP AND
 CIRCUMSTANCE"]

01:52:31.801 --> 01:52:37.838
Dr. Zerhouni is the 15th
director of the NIH.

01:52:40.820 --> 01:52:50.240
He took office in May of 2002,
and his major focus at the NIH

01:52:50.240 --> 01:52:58.620
has been in laying out
new pathways of discovery

01:52:58.620 --> 01:53:02.960
in clinical research
enterprise and research teams

01:53:02.960 --> 01:53:09.360
for re-engineering clinical
work, primarily concerned

01:53:09.360 --> 01:53:10.460
with biomedical research.

01:53:15.690 --> 01:53:19.950
SEAMANS: As the graduates
file into the Killian Court,

01:53:19.950 --> 01:53:22.080
they must get into
a certain order,

01:53:22.080 --> 01:53:28.192
because as their names are
called they go under the awning

01:53:28.192 --> 01:53:30.632
and receive their diploma.

01:53:30.632 --> 01:53:36.440
At the time they do this,
they are read simultaneously.

01:53:36.440 --> 01:53:41.330
The grad students and
the undergrads are read.

01:53:41.330 --> 01:53:42.395
So they are divided.

01:53:42.395 --> 01:53:47.097
So they go up, and you'll see
two diplomas being given out

01:53:47.097 --> 01:53:48.345
almost simultaneously.

01:53:55.840 --> 01:54:00.420
Earlier you saw the stacks of
diplomas, which must be exactly

01:54:00.420 --> 01:54:04.080
in order so that each person
gets exactly the diploma he's

01:54:04.080 --> 01:54:04.580
entitled to.

01:54:04.580 --> 01:54:08.084
There we see the
stacks of diplomas.

01:54:08.084 --> 01:54:11.542
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS, "POMP AND
 CIRCUMSTANCE"]

01:54:18.952 --> 01:54:25.868
KAISER: There's the MIT mace
carried into the procession

01:54:25.868 --> 01:54:28.329
by the--

01:54:28.329 --> 01:54:30.370
SEAMANS: President of the
MIT Alumni Association.

01:54:30.370 --> 01:54:32.560
KAISER: President of the
MIT Alumni Association.

01:54:32.560 --> 01:54:37.858
That mace goes back
how far, Warren?

01:54:41.430 --> 01:54:43.450
The beaver's on top
of the mace, right?

01:54:43.450 --> 01:54:45.440
SEAMANS: Yes, the
beaver's on top,

01:54:45.440 --> 01:54:47.179
and an acorn is on the bottom.

01:54:47.179 --> 01:54:47.804
KAISER: Uh-huh.

01:54:47.804 --> 01:54:49.238
And what's in the middle there?

01:54:49.238 --> 01:54:50.490
It's an urn, right?

01:54:50.490 --> 01:54:52.695
SEAMANS: Yes.

01:54:52.695 --> 01:54:56.460
KAISER: And there are
ashes in that urn, right?

01:54:56.460 --> 01:55:00.055
SEAMANS: I do not really
know what's in there.

01:55:00.055 --> 01:55:02.088
This particular urn only--

01:55:02.088 --> 01:55:07.357
particular mace is only,
perhaps, 20 to 22 years old.

01:55:07.357 --> 01:55:11.949
So a relatively new tradition
in the entire proceedings.

01:55:11.949 --> 01:55:13.740
KAISER: I see, carrying
the mace is a new--

01:55:13.740 --> 01:55:16.000
SEAMANS: Carrying the mace.

01:55:16.000 --> 01:55:17.710
KAISER: Well, we know what the--

01:55:17.710 --> 01:55:20.710
we did a hooding ceremony
yesterday, as I said,

01:55:20.710 --> 01:55:25.820
211 doctorates were
hooded yesterday.

01:55:25.820 --> 01:55:27.840
I might say a word
about what that means.

01:55:27.840 --> 01:55:34.170
When you become a
PhD, you get a hood.

01:55:34.170 --> 01:55:37.670
And you could see the
backs here of the faculty,

01:55:37.670 --> 01:55:39.650
all of those colorful
things there,

01:55:39.650 --> 01:55:40.880
the yellows and the blues.

01:55:40.880 --> 01:55:42.860
Those are hoods.

01:55:42.860 --> 01:55:46.670
Those hoods go back
to medieval times

01:55:46.670 --> 01:55:53.131
when they reflect a time when
scholars were mendicants.

01:55:53.131 --> 01:55:57.800
And they would lecture,
and for their pains,

01:55:57.800 --> 01:56:04.350
they would be given money or
food or things of that sort,

01:56:04.350 --> 01:56:06.090
which were kept in these hoods.

01:56:06.090 --> 01:56:08.460
Now they're strictly
ornamental, and what they do

01:56:08.460 --> 01:56:12.990
is they reflect the school
that the graduate came from.

01:56:12.990 --> 01:56:16.860
For example, my
PhD was from Yale,

01:56:16.860 --> 01:56:19.360
and it's a very deep blue.

01:56:19.360 --> 01:56:24.250
The MIT hood will be
crimson and gray--

01:56:24.250 --> 01:56:25.350
SEAMANS: Cardinal.

01:56:25.350 --> 01:56:28.145
KAISER: Cardinal, of
course, cardinal and gray.

01:56:31.860 --> 01:56:36.510
And yesterday, we gave the
hoods to the PhD candidates,

01:56:36.510 --> 01:56:44.130
which means that this ceremony
can be cut by about an hour.

01:56:44.130 --> 01:56:46.530
It took that long to--

01:56:46.530 --> 01:56:49.080
we used to give the hoods
in the same ceremony.

01:56:49.080 --> 01:56:50.940
But dividing these
ceremonies in two

01:56:50.940 --> 01:56:53.550
was a wonderful idea
because not only

01:56:53.550 --> 01:56:56.880
did it give special
recognition to PhDs,

01:56:56.880 --> 01:56:59.850
but it also enabled
this ceremony

01:56:59.850 --> 01:57:03.780
to move along at a faster clip.

01:57:03.780 --> 01:57:05.925
SEAMANS: The books that
everybody is holding

01:57:05.925 --> 01:57:08.030
is the commencement
program, which

01:57:08.030 --> 01:57:14.012
also lists all of the graduates
in the order by the school,

01:57:14.012 --> 01:57:18.966
and usually it lists
the hometown as well.

01:57:18.966 --> 01:57:21.622
Kathryn Willmore, vice
president and secretary

01:57:21.622 --> 01:57:25.440
of the Corporation,
Allan Bufferd, treasurer.

01:57:25.440 --> 01:57:28.926
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS]

01:57:38.627 --> 01:57:39.710
KAISER: So we've got what?

01:57:39.710 --> 01:57:41.380
What did I say, 2,000?

01:57:41.380 --> 01:57:42.871
SEAMANS: We have 2,200 or--

01:57:42.871 --> 01:57:46.847
KAISER: 2,200.

01:57:46.847 --> 01:57:50.326
People are marching in from
Howard Johnson Athletic Center,

01:57:50.326 --> 01:57:55.296
across Mass Avenue, and
up into Killian Court.

01:57:55.296 --> 01:57:58.278
SEAMANS: Chuck Vest,
outgoing president.

01:57:58.278 --> 01:58:01.260
KAISER: Dr. Zerhouni
next to him.

01:58:09.709 --> 01:58:17.661
SEAMANS: Killian Court
was dedicated 30 years ago

01:58:17.661 --> 01:58:22.134
this year in honor of James
Rhyne Killian, former president

01:58:22.134 --> 01:58:25.116
and chairman of the Corporation
at the time of his retirement

01:58:25.116 --> 01:58:27.150
from the latter.

01:58:27.150 --> 01:58:30.960
Prior to that time, it had
been known as the Great Court.

01:58:30.960 --> 01:58:32.880
And it always, as I
indicated earlier,

01:58:32.880 --> 01:58:36.390
had always been the
center of MIT activity.

01:58:36.390 --> 01:58:38.520
However, graduations
traditionally

01:58:38.520 --> 01:58:40.860
were not held in
the Great Court.

01:58:40.860 --> 01:58:45.760
Starting in early
1920s, they decided

01:58:45.760 --> 01:58:50.310
to try to hold the graduation
ceremonies in the court.

01:58:50.310 --> 01:58:54.940
But in '23, the tent fell
down that they were under,

01:58:54.940 --> 01:58:58.500
and they moved back to
Symphony Hall for most

01:58:58.500 --> 01:59:01.350
of the next 20 to 25 years.

01:59:01.350 --> 01:59:07.230
Then after the Rockwell
Cage was built post-war,

01:59:07.230 --> 01:59:10.860
they moved graduation
to that facility,

01:59:10.860 --> 01:59:15.020
where it remained until about
20 years we moved back outside.

01:59:15.020 --> 01:59:17.140
And weather has
usually cooperated,

01:59:17.140 --> 01:59:22.480
although there have been times
when raincoats were furnished

01:59:22.480 --> 01:59:27.110
to everybody attending,
and umbrellas were usually,

01:59:27.110 --> 01:59:29.100
in those two or
three years, were

01:59:29.100 --> 01:59:31.375
the primary thing
you saw looking out

01:59:31.375 --> 01:59:32.992
from this direction.

01:59:35.770 --> 01:59:40.110
For those of you familiar
with this view of the court,

01:59:40.110 --> 01:59:43.790
you turn around and
see the view going back

01:59:43.790 --> 01:59:45.700
toward the Boston skyline.

01:59:45.700 --> 01:59:48.084
There's now a large
tent across there,

01:59:48.084 --> 01:59:53.660
which blocks off the view of
the river and Memorial Drive

01:59:53.660 --> 02:00:00.850
and pretty well hides the view,
which is always spectacular.

02:00:00.850 --> 02:00:06.440
KAISER: Those flowers that you
see there are not always there.

02:00:06.440 --> 02:00:11.730
They are brought in especially
for the commencement exercises.

02:00:11.730 --> 02:00:15.000
And what will happen after
the commencement exercises

02:00:15.000 --> 02:00:19.500
is that they'll all be
taken in to the lobby

02:00:19.500 --> 02:00:21.530
beneath the dome,
which you now see,

02:00:21.530 --> 02:00:24.750
and they're sold to
the general public,

02:00:24.750 --> 02:00:26.909
and the proceeds go
to some charitable--

02:00:26.909 --> 02:00:28.700
SEAMANS: Community
Service Fund, I believe.

02:00:28.700 --> 02:00:30.320
KAISER: The Community
Service Fund.

02:00:30.320 --> 02:00:36.180
So that's always a great
event to go down and buy some

02:00:36.180 --> 02:00:37.910
of those beautiful flowers.

02:00:37.910 --> 02:00:42.284
They don't go to waste,
go to a very good cause.

02:00:42.284 --> 02:00:44.390
SEAMANS: The dome
you currently see

02:00:44.390 --> 02:00:51.330
has been the scene of some
of MIT's most famous hacks--

02:00:51.330 --> 02:00:54.920
the police cruiser on
the dome, the helicopter

02:00:54.920 --> 02:00:56.580
earlier this year.

02:00:56.580 --> 02:01:01.960
It's always been a
student-central thing to have

02:01:01.960 --> 02:01:03.200
been--

02:01:03.200 --> 02:01:08.710
to have done a hack
on the main dome.

02:01:08.710 --> 02:01:12.099
KAISER: Yes, the students
know how to get up there.

02:01:12.099 --> 02:01:13.890
They're very clever
about getting up there.

02:01:13.890 --> 02:01:15.940
SEAMANS: Even though
it's illegal, of course.

02:01:15.940 --> 02:01:17.660
KAISER: The most
recent hack there

02:01:17.660 --> 02:01:22.960
was a beautiful, beautiful
reproduction of the Kitty Hawk.

02:01:22.960 --> 02:01:24.460
SEAMANS: The Wright
brothers' plane.

02:01:24.460 --> 02:01:26.470
KAISER: The Wright
brothers' plane.

02:01:26.470 --> 02:01:31.310
And the hawks, the
red-tailed hawks

02:01:31.310 --> 02:01:36.260
that have taken up residence
at MIT, one of them

02:01:36.260 --> 02:01:37.575
is named Kitty Hawk.

02:01:37.575 --> 02:01:40.540
SEAMANS: [LAUGHS]

02:01:40.540 --> 02:01:44.200
KAISER: And I think that
he was named Kitty Hawk not

02:01:44.200 --> 02:01:50.310
after the Wright Brothers
plane, but after the MIT hack.

02:01:50.310 --> 02:01:57.890
The hack is-- it does
cause some consternation

02:01:57.890 --> 02:02:02.550
on the part of officialdom at
MIT because on the one hand,

02:02:02.550 --> 02:02:07.140
it is a remarkable expression
of student ingenuity.

02:02:07.140 --> 02:02:09.910
On the other hand,
it's dangerous to go up

02:02:09.910 --> 02:02:12.190
on to the dome.

02:02:12.190 --> 02:02:15.300
But these are MIT
students, and if you

02:02:15.300 --> 02:02:20.940
block all of the known
entrances to the dome,

02:02:20.940 --> 02:02:22.110
they'll find another way.

02:02:22.110 --> 02:02:23.526
SEAMANS: They'll
find another one.

02:02:23.526 --> 02:02:25.140
KAISER: And even
if it means scaling

02:02:25.140 --> 02:02:26.767
the columns from outside.

02:02:33.620 --> 02:02:37.380
There is Dr. Vest now, and
a good shot of Dr. Vest

02:02:37.380 --> 02:02:39.960
and Dr. Zerhouni, who
came to the United States

02:02:39.960 --> 02:02:44.610
from Algeria in 1975.

02:02:44.610 --> 02:02:47.820
He earned a medical degree
from the University of Algiers.

02:02:47.820 --> 02:02:51.210
And he was accepted as
a radiology resident

02:02:51.210 --> 02:02:54.870
at John Hopkins University
School of Medicine,

02:02:54.870 --> 02:02:58.770
where he became chairman of
the Department of Radiology

02:02:58.770 --> 02:03:01.740
and Radiological Science,
radiologist-in-chief,

02:03:01.740 --> 02:03:03.570
and president of the
Clinical Practice

02:03:03.570 --> 02:03:06.480
Association, executive
vice dean, and professor

02:03:06.480 --> 02:03:09.810
of biomedical engineering.

02:03:09.810 --> 02:03:14.370
He singly or jointly
holds eight patents

02:03:14.370 --> 02:03:16.650
for various
computerized tomography

02:03:16.650 --> 02:03:20.420
and magnetic resonance
imaging inventions.

02:03:20.420 --> 02:03:23.660
And it's significant that
our speaker today would come

02:03:23.660 --> 02:03:27.260
from the biological sciences.

02:03:27.260 --> 02:03:30.734
That is one of the
burgeoning areas

02:03:30.734 --> 02:03:33.668
in most institutions
of higher learning

02:03:33.668 --> 02:03:37.091
and particularly in MIT.

02:03:37.091 --> 02:03:40.410
It will be interesting to see
what Dr. Zerhouni has to say.

02:03:40.410 --> 02:03:43.380
In the upper left-hand
corner there, you

02:03:43.380 --> 02:03:47.082
saw Bob Silbey, who's dean
of the School of Science.

02:03:52.830 --> 02:03:55.900
SEAMANS: Those who have
received a commission to ROTC

02:03:55.900 --> 02:04:00.120
will be wearing their uniform
for the respective service

02:04:00.120 --> 02:04:06.372
that they did their
ROTC training in.

02:04:06.372 --> 02:04:07.830
KAISER: What I've
been looking for,

02:04:07.830 --> 02:04:12.326
as the graduates have processed
down the center aisle,

02:04:12.326 --> 02:04:20.340
is I've been looking for some
originality in their costume.

02:04:20.340 --> 02:04:21.789
And I don't see any.

02:04:21.789 --> 02:04:23.238
SEAMANS: No, it's
been very staid.

02:04:23.238 --> 02:04:29.517
And only one or two
cell phones as well.

02:04:29.517 --> 02:04:31.449
KAISER: I mean, I don't
see a single adorned--

02:04:31.449 --> 02:04:32.420
SEAMANS: Not one.

02:04:32.420 --> 02:04:33.470
KAISER: --mortarboard.

02:04:33.470 --> 02:04:33.984
Amazing.

02:04:33.984 --> 02:04:35.942
SEAMANS: A few minutes
ago-- a few seconds ago,

02:04:35.942 --> 02:04:39.316
we saw the class
of '04's banner.

02:04:39.316 --> 02:04:44.030
Each class has a banner
that's prepared for them

02:04:44.030 --> 02:04:46.700
by the Institute, and
then this is brought out

02:04:46.700 --> 02:04:51.580
for reunions and other
events that the class has.

02:04:51.580 --> 02:04:55.788
It's something maintained
by the Institute.

02:04:55.788 --> 02:04:57.487
KAISER: There's
[INAUDIBLE] cell phone.

02:04:57.487 --> 02:04:59.528
SEAMANS: This too goes--
that tradition goes back

02:04:59.528 --> 02:05:02.079
to 1868, the class banner.

02:05:02.079 --> 02:05:02.620
KAISER: Yeah.

02:05:06.524 --> 02:05:10.428
Yes, I've noticed that over
the last-- for several years

02:05:10.428 --> 02:05:13.720
the classes has been
less adventurous.

02:05:13.720 --> 02:05:15.470
SEAMANS: Yeah, there's
another cell phone.

02:05:15.470 --> 02:05:16.930
There's two cell phones.

02:05:16.930 --> 02:05:18.664
Last year, almost
everybody who came in

02:05:18.664 --> 02:05:20.839
had a cell phone as they
were talking to somebody.

02:05:20.839 --> 02:05:22.630
KAISER: Yeah, and they
were usually talking

02:05:22.630 --> 02:05:24.550
to the person next to them.

02:05:24.550 --> 02:05:28.574
SEAMANS: Maybe that is
something that has become passe.

02:05:28.574 --> 02:05:32.032
Let's hope so.

02:05:32.032 --> 02:05:34.008
But I think it's
fascinating that there's

02:05:34.008 --> 02:05:34.996
so little adornment.

02:05:34.996 --> 02:05:37.480
KAISER: Yeah, yeah.

02:05:37.480 --> 02:05:40.910
I believe that we're going
into a conservative cycle.

02:05:40.910 --> 02:05:41.410
Oh, boy.

02:05:47.505 --> 02:05:49.370
That's Bob Brown on the right.

02:05:49.370 --> 02:05:53.424
He is provost of MIT.

02:05:53.424 --> 02:05:55.340
SEAMANS: Provost is the
chief academic officer

02:05:55.340 --> 02:05:58.360
of an institute.

02:05:58.360 --> 02:06:01.030
KAISER: And the word "provost"--
and I don't know if you knew

02:06:01.030 --> 02:06:02.770
this, Warren-- but
the word "provost"

02:06:02.770 --> 02:06:05.900
actually comes from the
same root as the word

02:06:05.900 --> 02:06:07.290
for "preposition."

02:06:07.290 --> 02:06:08.290
SEAMANS: Really?

02:06:08.290 --> 02:06:08.830
KAISER: That's right.

02:06:08.830 --> 02:06:09.621
It's the same word.

02:06:09.621 --> 02:06:15.830
It's the Latin [LATIN],, and
it's from the past participle.

02:06:15.830 --> 02:06:18.040
And a provost is someone
that you should never

02:06:18.040 --> 02:06:18.970
end an argument with.

02:06:18.970 --> 02:06:21.920
[LAUGHTER]

02:06:21.920 --> 02:06:24.820
SEAMANS: As I say, he is
the chief academic officer,

02:06:24.820 --> 02:06:30.760
and everybody else reports
through him to the president.

02:06:30.760 --> 02:06:34.690
KAISER: We've been talking
about Dr. Zerhouni giving

02:06:34.690 --> 02:06:36.250
the principal address.

02:06:36.250 --> 02:06:42.880
The president of
MIT, of course, also

02:06:42.880 --> 02:06:46.090
delivers an address each year.

02:06:46.090 --> 02:06:48.010
And when the president
delivers the address,

02:06:48.010 --> 02:06:50.380
it's called the charge.

02:06:50.380 --> 02:06:54.070
And so what the president
actually does is,

02:06:54.070 --> 02:06:56.410
I think the proper
usage is the president

02:06:56.410 --> 02:06:58.840
charges the class,
which seems to be

02:06:58.840 --> 02:07:00.790
a bit unfortunate since
he's been charging

02:07:00.790 --> 02:07:03.820
the class for the
last four years.

02:07:03.820 --> 02:07:07.132
SEAMANS: We won't
say in which way.

02:07:07.132 --> 02:07:08.100
Behind--

02:07:08.100 --> 02:07:09.068
KAISER: Yes?

02:07:09.068 --> 02:07:11.730
SEAMANS: Behind
him-- behind the flag

02:07:11.730 --> 02:07:14.392
is Ike Colbert, who's
dean of the Grad School.

02:07:14.392 --> 02:07:18.826
KAISER: Yeah, dean of
the Graduate School.

02:07:18.826 --> 02:07:24.040
There's a great shot
of the adornment.

02:07:24.040 --> 02:07:25.890
I wish I knew the
names of those flowers.

02:07:25.890 --> 02:07:27.750
I don't, so I'll
just point them out.

02:07:27.750 --> 02:07:30.830
There's some blue ones,
and there's some pink ones.

02:07:30.830 --> 02:07:31.810
SEAMANS: Ageratum and--

02:07:31.810 --> 02:07:32.300
KAISER: Geraniums.

02:07:32.300 --> 02:07:32.760
Are those--

02:07:32.760 --> 02:07:33.950
SEAMANS: Yeah, the
orange ones are--

02:07:33.950 --> 02:07:35.158
KAISER: Are those blue ones--

02:07:35.158 --> 02:07:37.589
SEAMANS: The pinkish
ones are geranium.

02:07:37.589 --> 02:07:39.130
KAISER: Are the blue
ones hydrangeas?

02:07:39.130 --> 02:07:41.419
SEAMANS: No, they're ageratum.

02:07:41.419 --> 02:07:41.960
KAISER: What?

02:07:41.960 --> 02:07:42.810
SEAMANS: Ageratum.

02:07:42.810 --> 02:07:44.560
KAISER: Ageratum.

02:07:44.560 --> 02:07:48.450
This is a family show, Warren.

02:07:48.450 --> 02:07:50.710
So ageratum, ageratum yourself.

02:07:55.321 --> 02:07:57.789
SEAMANS: I could
call it ageratum.

02:07:57.789 --> 02:07:58.330
KAISER: What?

02:07:58.330 --> 02:08:02.694
SEAMANS: Could call it
ageratum, I suppose.

02:08:02.694 --> 02:08:04.170
KAISER: How do you know that?

02:08:04.170 --> 02:08:06.138
SEAMANS: I was a horticulture
major in college.

02:08:09.725 --> 02:08:11.350
KAISER: Well, will
wonders never cease?

02:08:11.350 --> 02:08:12.910
A horticulture major, really?

02:08:12.910 --> 02:08:14.592
SEAMANS: Yeah, I
switched early on--

02:08:14.592 --> 02:08:15.550
KAISER: I'll be darned.

02:08:15.550 --> 02:08:16.340
SEAMANS: --to something else.

02:08:16.340 --> 02:08:17.298
KAISER: I'll be darned.

02:08:20.150 --> 02:08:23.460
Well, I mean, here's
some department heads.

02:08:23.460 --> 02:08:24.770
There's--

02:08:24.770 --> 02:08:25.910
SEAMANS: Stan Anderson.

02:08:25.910 --> 02:08:27.410
KAISER: Stan Anderson,
architecture.

02:08:27.410 --> 02:08:29.240
SEAMANS: Head of architecture.

02:08:29.240 --> 02:08:33.270
KAISER: And Ian Hutchinson.

02:08:33.270 --> 02:08:35.090
What is-- I'm not sure
of his department.

02:08:35.090 --> 02:08:38.030
That's Ian Hutchinson
in the blue.

02:08:38.030 --> 02:08:41.460
And I don't recognize the man
with the yellow next to him.

02:08:44.910 --> 02:08:48.140
That's Steve Lippard
in the MIT gown there.

02:08:48.140 --> 02:08:49.952
Well, it's not an
MIT gown, is it?

02:08:49.952 --> 02:08:50.910
Looks a little like it.

02:08:54.790 --> 02:08:59.260
And Steve Lippard is
head of chemistry,

02:08:59.260 --> 02:09:02.710
and he's talking to Lester
Thurow, the man who used

02:09:02.710 --> 02:09:06.367
to be dean of the School of--

02:09:06.367 --> 02:09:07.200
SEAMANS: Management.

02:09:07.200 --> 02:09:07.520
KAISER: The--

02:09:07.520 --> 02:09:08.436
SEAMANS: Sloan School.

02:09:08.436 --> 02:09:09.540
KAISER: The Sloan School.

02:09:09.540 --> 02:09:11.300
And next to him, I
don't know who he is,

02:09:11.300 --> 02:09:12.740
but he looks
awfully comfortable.

02:09:12.740 --> 02:09:14.557
He's got his feet up
on the railing there.

02:09:14.557 --> 02:09:15.640
SEAMANS: The dark glasses.

02:09:23.135 --> 02:09:23.885
KAISER: All right.

02:09:29.250 --> 02:09:31.980
Yes, John Belcher is talking--
that's John Belcher with

02:09:31.980 --> 02:09:35.610
the tassel over his left eye--

02:09:35.610 --> 02:09:39.067
and he's talking to Steve
Lerman, who is on his right

02:09:39.067 --> 02:09:41.295
and directly in front of him.

02:09:49.215 --> 02:09:52.770
Now, the next person
we're going to see

02:09:52.770 --> 02:09:54.545
is the mayor of the
city of Cambridge.

02:09:54.545 --> 02:09:56.220
And he's the one
nearest us, and he's

02:09:56.220 --> 02:09:58.910
talking to Phil Clay,
who is the chancellor.

02:09:58.910 --> 02:10:01.980
He was from the Department
of Urban Studies.

02:10:01.980 --> 02:10:08.010
And the mayor, of course,
presided last a few weeks ago

02:10:08.010 --> 02:10:12.000
over that historic evening
when same-sex marriages

02:10:12.000 --> 02:10:15.186
were allowed in Cambridge.

02:10:20.430 --> 02:10:24.390
And Phil Clay is sandwiched
in between the mayor

02:10:24.390 --> 02:10:30.270
on the left and Mr. Mead,
who is the new chairman

02:10:30.270 --> 02:10:33.480
of the Corporation.

02:10:33.480 --> 02:10:35.920
He's now adjusting his cuff.

02:10:35.920 --> 02:10:37.050
That's him.

02:10:37.050 --> 02:10:39.638
And right above him-- that's it.

02:10:44.358 --> 02:10:47.064
SEAMANS: We're reaching
just about the end

02:10:47.064 --> 02:10:48.780
of the procession, I believe.

02:10:51.730 --> 02:10:55.962
Takes about a half an hour
for all the 2,200 people

02:10:55.962 --> 02:10:59.180
to get into the
great Killian Court.

02:10:59.180 --> 02:11:02.645
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS]

02:11:12.050 --> 02:11:13.050
KAISER: All right.

02:11:13.050 --> 02:11:16.200
Now you can see
the PhD candidates.

02:11:16.200 --> 02:11:19.194
They already have their hoods.

02:11:19.194 --> 02:11:21.620
There was one PhD
candidate yesterday

02:11:21.620 --> 02:11:24.560
who actually came
up on the platform,

02:11:24.560 --> 02:11:26.120
and she already had a hood.

02:11:26.120 --> 02:11:28.412
This was her second PhD.

02:11:28.412 --> 02:11:30.674
And you might catch
a glimpse of her.

02:11:30.674 --> 02:11:33.340
I don't if she's allowed
to wear two hoods,

02:11:33.340 --> 02:11:35.738
but it'd be interesting
to see if you do that.

02:11:35.738 --> 02:11:36.821
Well, I mean, not allowed.

02:11:36.821 --> 02:11:37.231
I'm sure you could allow.

02:11:37.231 --> 02:11:39.306
There isn't any hood
policemen around.

02:11:39.306 --> 02:11:42.360
But I wonder what the protocol
is if you have two PhDs.

02:11:42.360 --> 02:11:45.090
And she's now enrolled in
a program to get a third

02:11:45.090 --> 02:11:50.560
doctorate, the
MIT-Harvard Health--

02:11:50.560 --> 02:11:54.000
joint medical health program.

02:11:54.000 --> 02:11:59.746
The music in the background,
as I'm sure you can hear,

02:11:59.746 --> 02:12:05.640
is Mass Brass, directed by
Larry Isaacson, the brass

02:12:05.640 --> 02:12:12.550
ensemble that entertains
the commencement every year.

02:12:12.550 --> 02:12:16.380
Larry was actually a member
of MIT teaching staff

02:12:16.380 --> 02:12:19.695
from 1990 to 2000.

02:12:19.695 --> 02:12:26.306
He is now assistant director
of the music division

02:12:26.306 --> 02:12:29.550
of the Boston Conservatory.

02:12:29.550 --> 02:12:32.460
And right after
today's event, well,

02:12:32.460 --> 02:12:33.980
just within a
couple of days, he's

02:12:33.980 --> 02:12:38.860
going to be hopping a
plane to Aspen, Colorado,

02:12:38.860 --> 02:12:44.236
where he will lead the
Aspen Festival Concert.

02:12:54.900 --> 02:13:00.718
SEAMANS: This is a view from
behind the speakers podium,

02:13:00.718 --> 02:13:07.049
where very soon the Commencement
ceremonies will start.

02:13:07.049 --> 02:13:10.542
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS]

02:13:28.506 --> 02:13:32.890
There are Priscilla
Gray and Becky Vest,

02:13:32.890 --> 02:13:38.045
the current and immediate
past presidents' wives.

02:13:42.010 --> 02:13:42.540
OK.

02:13:42.540 --> 02:13:47.910
We are ready to sign off because
the Commencement is coming.

02:13:47.910 --> 02:13:48.715
It's commencing.

02:13:48.715 --> 02:13:51.080
KAISER: This is Warren
Seamans and Jay Kaiser saying

02:13:51.080 --> 02:13:52.666
goodbye for another year.

02:13:59.080 --> 02:14:02.710
PRESENTER: The Corporation
and the faculty

02:14:02.710 --> 02:14:05.260
of the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

02:14:05.260 --> 02:14:08.830
are now declared
convened together

02:14:08.830 --> 02:14:12.340
with this assembly on the
occasion of the Commencement

02:14:12.340 --> 02:14:18.650
Exercises of this Institution
for the conferring of degrees.

02:14:18.650 --> 02:14:21.360
The stage assembly
and the audience

02:14:21.360 --> 02:14:24.740
will please rise
for the Invocation

02:14:24.740 --> 02:14:28.310
by Dean Robert
Randolph and remain

02:14:28.310 --> 02:14:32.660
standing to join
the MIT Chorallaries

02:14:32.660 --> 02:14:36.246
in the singing of one verse
of "The Star-Spangled Banner."

02:14:46.180 --> 02:14:51.170
RANDOLPH: Almighty God,
known by many names,

02:14:51.170 --> 02:14:59.840
heard in many voices, we invoke
your presence with us this day.

02:14:59.840 --> 02:15:03.080
Today we celebrate
the accomplishments

02:15:03.080 --> 02:15:08.020
of those who receive diplomas
and of those who begin

02:15:08.020 --> 02:15:12.080
new chapters in their lives.

02:15:12.080 --> 02:15:13.820
We invoke your
blessings upon them.

02:15:16.550 --> 02:15:19.790
There are those here who
have passed through MIT

02:15:19.790 --> 02:15:24.100
and seldom looked
to right or left.

02:15:24.100 --> 02:15:29.890
There are those here who have
struggled and arrive here today

02:15:29.890 --> 02:15:31.015
battered and bruised.

02:15:33.720 --> 02:15:39.240
Make each mindful, we pray, of
what they have accomplished.

02:15:39.240 --> 02:15:43.930
Let success mend self-esteem
and give perspective

02:15:43.930 --> 02:15:45.385
to achievement.

02:15:48.300 --> 02:15:50.370
Bless as well,
Almighty God, those

02:15:50.370 --> 02:15:54.760
who have supported them in
their work, in the classroom,

02:15:54.760 --> 02:15:57.420
at home, and on the way--

02:15:57.420 --> 02:15:59.490
teachers who have
given of themselves

02:15:59.490 --> 02:16:01.800
in ways that will
only be understood

02:16:01.800 --> 02:16:07.670
with the passage of time,
families who have sacrificed

02:16:07.670 --> 02:16:13.114
much, friends who have learned
from them and taught them,

02:16:13.114 --> 02:16:14.506
as only peers can.

02:16:18.690 --> 02:16:21.920
We ask special blessings
on President Charles Vest

02:16:21.920 --> 02:16:24.910
and his good wife Rebecca.

02:16:24.910 --> 02:16:28.150
They have given much
to this Institution,

02:16:28.150 --> 02:16:30.550
and we ask that the next
chapter in their lives

02:16:30.550 --> 02:16:35.799
be as successful as
their time with us.

02:16:35.799 --> 02:16:40.290
We pray for the
security of our nation

02:16:40.290 --> 02:16:43.040
and for the safety of
those who defend freedom,

02:16:43.040 --> 02:16:49.010
whoever and wherever they are.

02:16:49.010 --> 02:16:53.150
Make us ever mindful of those
on whose shoulders we stand,

02:16:53.150 --> 02:16:56.600
as well as those who
follow in our path.

02:16:56.600 --> 02:17:00.809
And as we celebrate
accomplishment and transition,

02:17:00.809 --> 02:17:02.760
may we continue to seek wisdom.

02:17:02.760 --> 02:17:04.683
Hear our prayer.

02:17:04.683 --> 02:17:05.183
Amen.

02:17:11.566 --> 02:17:15.003
[MUSIC - MASS BRASS, "THE STAR
 SPANGLED BANNER"]

02:17:24.332 --> 02:17:35.500
(SINGING) O, say can you see
by the dawn's early light

02:17:35.500 --> 02:17:45.479
what so proudly we hailed at
the twilight's last gleaming?

02:17:45.479 --> 02:17:50.248
Whose broad stripes
and bright stars

02:17:50.248 --> 02:18:00.420
through the perilous fight
o'er the ramparts we watched

02:18:00.420 --> 02:18:05.160
were so gallantly streaming?

02:18:05.160 --> 02:18:14.470
And the rocket's red glare,
the bombs bursting in air

02:18:14.470 --> 02:18:24.760
gave proof through the night
that our flag was still there.

02:18:24.760 --> 02:18:37.540
O, say does that star-spangled
banner yet wave o'er

02:18:37.540 --> 02:18:49.915
the land of the free and
the home of the brave?

02:18:53.875 --> 02:18:57.340
[APPLAUSE]

02:18:59.639 --> 02:19:00.805
PRESENTER: Please be seated.

02:19:17.170 --> 02:19:20.350
I'm pleased to welcome
to the platform

02:19:20.350 --> 02:19:23.010
the honorable
Michael A. Sullivan,

02:19:23.010 --> 02:19:25.710
mayor of the city of Cambridge.

02:19:25.710 --> 02:19:26.210
Mayor?

02:19:29.114 --> 02:19:32.018
[APPLAUSE]

02:19:36.379 --> 02:19:41.990
I'm also pleased to welcome
Dr. Elias A. Zerhouni, director

02:19:41.990 --> 02:19:45.059
of the National
Institutes of Health,

02:19:45.059 --> 02:19:48.378
who will now give the
graduation address.

02:19:48.378 --> 02:19:50.290
Dr. Zerhouni.

02:19:50.290 --> 02:19:53.636
[APPLAUSE]

02:19:56.990 --> 02:19:58.790
ZERHOUNI: Thank you very much.

02:19:58.790 --> 02:20:02.720
It's really a privilege for
me to be here and celebrate

02:20:02.720 --> 02:20:05.370
with you on this beautiful day.

02:20:05.370 --> 02:20:07.310
I also wish my
mother-in-law was here

02:20:07.310 --> 02:20:10.490
to see what I was doing
today, because I still

02:20:10.490 --> 02:20:13.070
have to convince
her on a daily basis

02:20:13.070 --> 02:20:17.210
that her daughter made the
right choice many years ago.

02:20:17.210 --> 02:20:20.960
I'm honored to be here because
I also, as a parent myself,

02:20:20.960 --> 02:20:25.580
can feel the joy of your parents
and friends who are here.

02:20:25.580 --> 02:20:28.970
As a parent, I remember
the birth of my first son

02:20:28.970 --> 02:20:30.790
as if it was yesterday.

02:20:30.790 --> 02:20:32.690
And I can tell you,
your parents also

02:20:32.690 --> 02:20:37.550
remember those 22 years ago
and days when you were born.

02:20:37.550 --> 02:20:43.040
For you, those 22 years may have
seemed very long and arduous.

02:20:43.040 --> 02:20:46.530
But I can tell you, for
parents they are very short.

02:20:46.530 --> 02:20:49.010
They all remember you
as a baby, and they

02:20:49.010 --> 02:20:52.375
can't believe how you've become
such a formidable graduate

02:20:52.375 --> 02:20:55.664
of one of the most prestigious
institutions in the world.

02:20:55.664 --> 02:20:57.080
And that's an
illustration of what

02:20:57.080 --> 02:21:01.500
I call the relativity
principle of time and aging.

02:21:01.500 --> 02:21:07.910
The older you are, the
faster time seems to go by

02:21:07.910 --> 02:21:12.800
and the faster tuition bills
seem to come through as well.

02:21:12.800 --> 02:21:17.120
But you know, you are the legacy
of your parents to this world,

02:21:17.120 --> 02:21:20.540
and they all deserve our
heartfelt recognition

02:21:20.540 --> 02:21:21.830
for doing such a good job.

02:21:21.830 --> 02:21:24.140
And I'd like all the
graduates, if you don't mind,

02:21:24.140 --> 02:21:26.370
to give a round of
applause to your parents.

02:21:26.370 --> 02:21:29.751
[APPLAUSE]

02:21:37.010 --> 02:21:39.680
It's also a great
honor for me to give

02:21:39.680 --> 02:21:44.300
this address in the last year
of President Charles Vest's

02:21:44.300 --> 02:21:47.780
extraordinary tenure at MIT.

02:21:47.780 --> 02:21:49.460
There's no question
that Dr. Vest,

02:21:49.460 --> 02:21:52.700
from my point of view as
a federal agency official,

02:21:52.700 --> 02:21:54.980
today is one of the
most influential thought

02:21:54.980 --> 02:21:57.590
leaders in higher education.

02:21:57.590 --> 02:21:59.570
He has this rare
combination that you

02:21:59.570 --> 02:22:02.450
don't find a lot in
life that combines

02:22:02.450 --> 02:22:05.570
vision and flawless execution.

02:22:05.570 --> 02:22:09.350
Well, last night he
conducted the Boston Pops

02:22:09.350 --> 02:22:12.140
with a flawless
execution, I hear.

02:22:12.140 --> 02:22:15.620
President Vest, we're impressed.

02:22:15.620 --> 02:22:18.170
Recently, my younger
son Adam actually

02:22:18.170 --> 02:22:22.300
was initiated in a fraternity
called Phi Kappa Psi.

02:22:22.300 --> 02:22:24.170
And as I was
checking to make sure

02:22:24.170 --> 02:22:28.790
that this was a good
fraternity, a decent one,

02:22:28.790 --> 02:22:32.750
I found out that Dr. Vest was
a member of that fraternity.

02:22:32.750 --> 02:22:36.460
So when people ask me
now about my son Adam,

02:22:36.460 --> 02:22:37.700
I say, oh, don't worry.

02:22:37.700 --> 02:22:41.840
He belongs to the exact same
fraternity Dr. Vest belongs to.

02:22:41.840 --> 02:22:46.130
So I'm very proud to do
this in his last year.

02:22:46.130 --> 02:22:48.170
But when I was preparing
my speech, what can

02:22:48.170 --> 02:22:51.770
you say to 2,200
very bright graduates

02:22:51.770 --> 02:22:56.240
that will make a
difference in telling them

02:22:56.240 --> 02:22:58.730
about where you
see life and where

02:22:58.730 --> 02:23:02.226
you see your field of science,
where you see yourself?

02:23:02.226 --> 02:23:03.600
And as I was
preparing my speech,

02:23:03.600 --> 02:23:07.080
I was looking at, what was
the best strategy to do that?

02:23:07.080 --> 02:23:10.100
And I came across the story
of a commencement speaker

02:23:10.100 --> 02:23:12.830
at Yale University
who had the great idea

02:23:12.830 --> 02:23:15.800
of using every letter
of the Yale name

02:23:15.800 --> 02:23:16.940
as a starting concept.

02:23:16.940 --> 02:23:21.320
So he used Y for youth, and
he went on and on about youth.

02:23:21.320 --> 02:23:25.070
And then he used A for
ability, which you all have,

02:23:25.070 --> 02:23:26.477
and he went on and on.

02:23:26.477 --> 02:23:28.310
And from the back of
the room somebody said,

02:23:28.310 --> 02:23:30.350
thank god we're not
the Massachusetts

02:23:30.350 --> 02:23:31.490
Institute of Technology.

02:23:31.490 --> 02:23:33.740
[LAUGHTER]

02:23:33.740 --> 02:23:35.580
So I give up on that strategy.

02:23:35.580 --> 02:23:38.510
I'm not going to use
that strategy today.

02:23:38.510 --> 02:23:40.070
Instead, I'll tell
you about what

02:23:40.070 --> 02:23:43.670
I think are the very critical
both scientific challenges

02:23:43.670 --> 02:23:48.470
of the 21st century, but
also, what is your role in it?

02:23:48.470 --> 02:23:52.340
The challenge, frankly,
for us in the 21st century

02:23:52.340 --> 02:23:55.040
is one that we have
brought upon ourselves.

02:23:55.040 --> 02:23:57.170
And I'll just go back
in history a little bit

02:23:57.170 --> 02:24:00.200
to give you the perspective
of how I see it.

02:24:00.200 --> 02:24:03.140
When you think
about the universe,

02:24:03.140 --> 02:24:08.060
you hear that about 13.7 billion
years ago there was a big bang,

02:24:08.060 --> 02:24:10.400
and off of that big
bang came the universe.

02:24:10.400 --> 02:24:13.610
And then planets and
solar systems and galaxies

02:24:13.610 --> 02:24:14.951
organized themselves.

02:24:14.951 --> 02:24:17.450
That was the first big bang,
but there were other big bangs.

02:24:17.450 --> 02:24:20.900
The Earth came about
5 billion years ago.

02:24:20.900 --> 02:24:27.430
And about 4 billion years
ago, with a mysterious event,

02:24:27.430 --> 02:24:29.960
life appeared.

02:24:29.960 --> 02:24:32.600
And what happened is
that through replication

02:24:32.600 --> 02:24:37.220
of very special molecules, DNA
and RNA, something very unique

02:24:37.220 --> 02:24:40.460
happened, where by
natural evolution

02:24:40.460 --> 02:24:43.100
allowed, through multiple
variations and survival

02:24:43.100 --> 02:24:48.680
of the fittest, the emergence
of a very diverse life on Earth.

02:24:48.680 --> 02:24:50.000
But there is a third big bang.

02:24:50.000 --> 02:24:51.374
And this is the
one you're living

02:24:51.374 --> 02:24:54.140
in today, which you
have to take account of,

02:24:54.140 --> 02:24:56.780
and that is the big
bang of knowledge.

02:24:56.780 --> 02:24:59.510
It occurred about
100,000 years ago,

02:24:59.510 --> 02:25:02.690
when about 10,000
individuals, at most--

02:25:02.690 --> 02:25:04.700
as we look at the
genome and we look

02:25:04.700 --> 02:25:08.210
at the variation of the genome
across the human population,

02:25:08.210 --> 02:25:10.580
it is very clear
that all of us have

02:25:10.580 --> 02:25:13.220
come from the same
founding population

02:25:13.220 --> 02:25:17.810
of 10,000 individuals in
Africa about 100,000 years ago.

02:25:17.810 --> 02:25:21.680
So we're not that different from
each other in historical terms.

02:25:21.680 --> 02:25:24.500
And yet that changed
the game of life,

02:25:24.500 --> 02:25:27.100
because through knowledge,
through our ability,

02:25:27.100 --> 02:25:31.580
through our intelligence we're
able to transfer information

02:25:31.580 --> 02:25:34.040
from one child-- from
one parent to a child,

02:25:34.040 --> 02:25:36.890
from a parent to another one.

02:25:36.890 --> 02:25:39.650
And through generations,
we're able to develop

02:25:39.650 --> 02:25:43.490
tools of adaptation the
world has never seen.

02:25:43.490 --> 02:25:46.850
We've been able to change
our environment at a speed,

02:25:46.850 --> 02:25:50.930
at a velocity, that is much
faster than what we can adapt

02:25:50.930 --> 02:25:53.450
to ourselves through
our natural mechanisms

02:25:53.450 --> 02:25:55.490
of natural evolution.

02:25:55.490 --> 02:25:57.120
Let me give you an example.

02:25:57.120 --> 02:26:02.260
Obesity is an emerging
public health threat.

02:26:02.260 --> 02:26:04.750
This year, the Center
for Disease Control

02:26:04.750 --> 02:26:06.610
said that obesity
will be the second--

02:26:06.610 --> 02:26:11.140
is the second cause of premature
mortality and morbidity

02:26:11.140 --> 02:26:12.490
in this country.

02:26:12.490 --> 02:26:14.170
Now, why is that?

02:26:14.170 --> 02:26:17.110
Because for millions
of years, our genes

02:26:17.110 --> 02:26:20.720
evolve in the context
of food scarcity.

02:26:20.720 --> 02:26:22.690
There was not a lot
of food around us

02:26:22.690 --> 02:26:24.460
for millions of years.

02:26:24.460 --> 02:26:26.740
And all of a sudden,
because of our intelligence,

02:26:26.740 --> 02:26:28.600
because of our
knowledge, we changed

02:26:28.600 --> 02:26:31.630
that in less than 50 years.

02:26:31.630 --> 02:26:34.780
Most of our genes
are, in fact, designed

02:26:34.780 --> 02:26:41.650
to allow you to accumulate
energy and keep that energy,

02:26:41.650 --> 02:26:46.700
which then translates itself
into overweight and obesity.

02:26:46.700 --> 02:26:49.640
Well, what are we
going to do about it?

02:26:49.640 --> 02:26:51.880
What I will dare
to say to you is

02:26:51.880 --> 02:26:54.820
that life sciences
and their applications

02:26:54.820 --> 02:26:59.150
will be the defining challenge
of the 21st century, bar none.

02:26:59.150 --> 02:27:03.340
And the reason is that we
are changing our environment

02:27:03.340 --> 02:27:07.000
at a speed which will
require us to understand

02:27:07.000 --> 02:27:10.900
life sciences to a degree
we do not understand today.

02:27:10.900 --> 02:27:14.470
And let me tell you, it will
require the intelligence

02:27:14.470 --> 02:27:19.370
and commitment of many classes
of graduates like yours.

02:27:19.370 --> 02:27:21.670
It is not coming from biology.

02:27:21.670 --> 02:27:23.890
The solution will not
come from biology alone.

02:27:23.890 --> 02:27:26.350
It will come from the
integration of biology

02:27:26.350 --> 02:27:29.290
and computer sciences and
mathematics and physics

02:27:29.290 --> 02:27:30.430
and chemistry.

02:27:30.430 --> 02:27:34.160
And we want to encourage
that to happen.

02:27:34.160 --> 02:27:35.970
Why is this a great
opportunity for you?

02:27:35.970 --> 02:27:37.580
Let me tell you a little story.

02:27:37.580 --> 02:27:40.520
About a few weeks ago,
I was at a meeting

02:27:40.520 --> 02:27:46.400
of the annual convention of all
the biotechnology executives.

02:27:46.400 --> 02:27:49.430
All the CEOs of the many
biotechnology companies

02:27:49.430 --> 02:27:52.220
in this country were there
assembled in New York

02:27:52.220 --> 02:27:54.200
with their investment bankers.

02:27:54.200 --> 02:27:56.600
Now, you know this must be
a very important meeting

02:27:56.600 --> 02:28:00.950
for someone who is trying
to get funds for their idea.

02:28:00.950 --> 02:28:05.090
And I just conducted a
poll, and I said to them,

02:28:05.090 --> 02:28:07.250
I am the director of the NIH.

02:28:07.250 --> 02:28:10.370
I'm supposed to-- as the
nation's medical research

02:28:10.370 --> 02:28:13.190
agency, I want to
ask you a question.

02:28:13.190 --> 02:28:15.950
How much do you think
you know of what you

02:28:15.950 --> 02:28:20.810
need to know to be effective in
combating obesity or diabetes

02:28:20.810 --> 02:28:24.222
and any of the health challenges
that we have in front of us?

02:28:24.222 --> 02:28:26.180
And I asked the question,
do you think you know

02:28:26.180 --> 02:28:28.490
90% of what you need to know?

02:28:28.490 --> 02:28:30.740
And no one answered that.

02:28:30.740 --> 02:28:31.610
50%?

02:28:31.610 --> 02:28:32.980
No one raised their hand.

02:28:32.980 --> 02:28:34.490
20%?

02:28:34.490 --> 02:28:35.780
No one raised their hand.

02:28:35.780 --> 02:28:37.880
So I say, what
about less than 10%?

02:28:37.880 --> 02:28:40.460
Everybody raised their hand.

02:28:40.460 --> 02:28:44.400
This is from the leaders
of life sciences today.

02:28:44.400 --> 02:28:45.830
So think about it.

02:28:45.830 --> 02:28:48.290
Think how much opportunity
there is in front of you.

02:28:48.290 --> 02:28:52.430
90% of what there is to
discover is still ahead of us.

02:28:52.430 --> 02:28:54.990
Now I turned to the
investment bankers,

02:28:54.990 --> 02:28:57.200
and I said, well, I
don't understand this.

02:28:57.200 --> 02:29:01.130
Now, you are
investing good dollars

02:29:01.130 --> 02:29:03.200
on people who just
admitted in front of you

02:29:03.200 --> 02:29:05.725
that they know less than 10%
than what they need to know.

02:29:05.725 --> 02:29:09.530
Can you imagine?

02:29:09.530 --> 02:29:11.210
And the reason is simple.

02:29:11.210 --> 02:29:14.800
It is such a great challenge,
and the risk-reward

02:29:14.800 --> 02:29:15.730
is so great.

02:29:15.730 --> 02:29:17.680
If you can find just
the cure for one

02:29:17.680 --> 02:29:19.960
of the major
diseases of mankind,

02:29:19.960 --> 02:29:24.220
you will affect the relationship
between our environment

02:29:24.220 --> 02:29:25.700
and ourselves.

02:29:25.700 --> 02:29:29.230
So it's important, I think,
to keep that in perspective

02:29:29.230 --> 02:29:31.690
and to understand that
there is a real race going

02:29:31.690 --> 02:29:35.020
on between our ability to
understand how we respond

02:29:35.020 --> 02:29:38.900
to our environment
biologically and our ability

02:29:38.900 --> 02:29:43.370
to change that environment in
ways and consequences that we--

02:29:43.370 --> 02:29:47.090
with consequences that we
may not always predict.

02:29:47.090 --> 02:29:51.500
So as the NIH director,
I have to give you

02:29:51.500 --> 02:29:53.720
some advice about how to
conduct yourself, then,

02:29:53.720 --> 02:29:56.690
for the next challenge, if
that's the challenge that we

02:29:56.690 --> 02:29:57.380
think is there.

02:29:57.380 --> 02:30:00.040
And now I can only do
this with no certainty,

02:30:00.040 --> 02:30:01.790
obviously, about what
the right answer is.

02:30:01.790 --> 02:30:04.910
I can only talk to you
about myself and the rules

02:30:04.910 --> 02:30:09.290
I've used in life to
go around and do this.

02:30:09.290 --> 02:30:13.700
First and foremost,
I learned one thing

02:30:13.700 --> 02:30:16.030
because I came from
another country actually.

02:30:16.030 --> 02:30:19.550
I came from Algeria when I
was 24 years old to America.

02:30:19.550 --> 02:30:25.910
And I immigrated, and I had
$300 in my pocket, a new wife,

02:30:25.910 --> 02:30:29.750
and no friends, and no family.

02:30:29.750 --> 02:30:32.150
And basically, this
is where I learned

02:30:32.150 --> 02:30:35.960
that you can't make a
contribution unless you're

02:30:35.960 --> 02:30:39.600
connected to others and you're
able to connect to others.

02:30:39.600 --> 02:30:43.350
So I developed these rules
called my 50-50 rules.

02:30:43.350 --> 02:30:44.810
You have to have
a balance in life

02:30:44.810 --> 02:30:47.860
because you never know
when you are going to need

02:30:47.860 --> 02:30:50.010
the interactions of others.

02:30:50.010 --> 02:30:51.620
So what are these 50-50 rules?

02:30:51.620 --> 02:30:56.600
Well, the first rule that I'd
like to share with you is this.

02:30:56.600 --> 02:31:00.330
Today you're going
to receive a diploma.

02:31:00.330 --> 02:31:02.810
What you know today,
I can assure you,

02:31:02.810 --> 02:31:06.800
is 50% wrong and 50% right.

02:31:06.800 --> 02:31:10.190
The challenge for you now is to
figure out what part is right

02:31:10.190 --> 02:31:12.170
and what part is wrong.

02:31:12.170 --> 02:31:14.210
Now, don't take my
speech as an excuse

02:31:14.210 --> 02:31:17.460
to go and ask for a
reimbursement on your tuition.

02:31:17.460 --> 02:31:19.257
I don't think they will do that.

02:31:19.257 --> 02:31:21.590
But on the other hand, I think
it's a very important way

02:31:21.590 --> 02:31:24.020
to look at the
knowledge fund that you

02:31:24.020 --> 02:31:28.250
have as new scientists,
new graduates of MIT.

02:31:28.250 --> 02:31:31.460
I think it is important
to also realize

02:31:31.460 --> 02:31:33.650
that in life many of
your contributions

02:31:33.650 --> 02:31:36.110
will not come from
your core discipline.

02:31:36.110 --> 02:31:38.330
They will come from
disciplines that you probably

02:31:38.330 --> 02:31:41.420
have no contact with typically.

02:31:41.420 --> 02:31:43.550
And this is the other
50-50 rule that I

02:31:43.550 --> 02:31:46.220
will like to leave you with.

02:31:46.220 --> 02:31:50.310
Read 50% of what you read in the
area that you're interested in,

02:31:50.310 --> 02:31:53.180
but make sure that
50% of what you read

02:31:53.180 --> 02:31:55.770
is unrelated to
what you have to do.

02:31:55.770 --> 02:31:57.470
And I did this
consistently because I

02:31:57.470 --> 02:31:58.670
had to learn a new language.

02:31:58.670 --> 02:32:03.080
I had to connect with new
friends and new disciplines.

02:32:03.080 --> 02:32:05.300
50% of what I read
was in radiology.

02:32:05.300 --> 02:32:07.250
I loved medical
imaging because it

02:32:07.250 --> 02:32:10.370
combined mathematics and
physics, which I love,

02:32:10.370 --> 02:32:13.910
and medicine, which I think
gave me the human contact,

02:32:13.910 --> 02:32:17.000
and that's why I worked and
made these contributions.

02:32:17.000 --> 02:32:21.770
But 50% of the time I would read
things outside of radiology.

02:32:21.770 --> 02:32:24.240
It's really fun to see
the world that way.

02:32:24.240 --> 02:32:26.390
But it's also more
fun to understand

02:32:26.390 --> 02:32:31.780
that you are smarter when
you're in the company

02:32:31.780 --> 02:32:34.060
of smarter people than you.

02:32:34.060 --> 02:32:37.300
It is amazing to see the
enrichment that you get

02:32:37.300 --> 02:32:41.000
from interacting with others.

02:32:41.000 --> 02:32:44.740
So my rule is that
50% of my friends

02:32:44.740 --> 02:32:47.440
have to be from walks of life
that are not directly related

02:32:47.440 --> 02:32:49.000
to my walk of life.

02:32:49.000 --> 02:32:51.760
And more importantly, I try
to make sure that at least 50%

02:32:51.760 --> 02:32:53.650
of my friends are
smarter than I am.

02:32:53.650 --> 02:32:56.830
Because you can be
assured that at least half

02:32:56.830 --> 02:33:01.367
of your life contributions
will be stimulated by others

02:33:01.367 --> 02:33:02.950
that are interacting
with you, and you

02:33:02.950 --> 02:33:05.140
will stimulate others as well.

02:33:05.140 --> 02:33:09.190
Often you hear about the spark
of genius that somebody had,

02:33:09.190 --> 02:33:13.360
this unique individual, and we
all admire these individuals.

02:33:13.360 --> 02:33:15.940
But it's rarely true
that it happens to people

02:33:15.940 --> 02:33:18.010
who are completely isolated.

02:33:18.010 --> 02:33:19.870
Throughout scientific
history, you've

02:33:19.870 --> 02:33:22.600
always had that
interaction of people,

02:33:22.600 --> 02:33:26.890
founder groups that got together
and created new advances.

02:33:26.890 --> 02:33:28.240
Witness Watson and Crick.

02:33:28.240 --> 02:33:31.570
Watson was a zoologist,
and Crick was a physicist.

02:33:31.570 --> 02:33:33.160
And coming together,
they created

02:33:33.160 --> 02:33:35.620
the field of molecular biology.

02:33:35.620 --> 02:33:38.320
Look at laboratories
around the world that

02:33:38.320 --> 02:33:39.910
have been very productive.

02:33:39.910 --> 02:33:43.690
They've been productive because
they have, in fact, encouraged

02:33:43.690 --> 02:33:46.960
the clustering of people from
diverse backgrounds coming

02:33:46.960 --> 02:33:51.040
from diverse horizons
with different ideas.

02:33:51.040 --> 02:33:54.120
Now, this process is
admittedly social.

02:33:54.120 --> 02:33:56.005
It is not an individual process.

02:33:56.005 --> 02:33:58.630
It is a process you
have to participate in.

02:33:58.630 --> 02:34:01.690
But now I'm going to tell you
about some of the exceptions

02:34:01.690 --> 02:34:02.980
that I've learned as well.

02:34:02.980 --> 02:34:05.740
People will tell you that if
you go and talk about things

02:34:05.740 --> 02:34:10.270
you do not understand to
people who do not know you,

02:34:10.270 --> 02:34:13.040
you will tend to
look a little stupid.

02:34:13.040 --> 02:34:15.110
And the objection
that I hear a lot

02:34:15.110 --> 02:34:18.110
is, but you can look
foolish asking questions

02:34:18.110 --> 02:34:20.540
about fields you
do not understand

02:34:20.540 --> 02:34:23.300
and those people
who do not know you.

02:34:23.300 --> 02:34:24.930
Well, that's true.

02:34:24.930 --> 02:34:25.730
That's very true.

02:34:25.730 --> 02:34:28.730
I asked a lot of stupid
questions in my life,

02:34:28.730 --> 02:34:29.942
and you will too.

02:34:29.942 --> 02:34:31.400
But the one thing
I can tell you is

02:34:31.400 --> 02:34:33.650
that it's not deadly to
ask a stupid question.

02:34:33.650 --> 02:34:36.350
What's deadly is to not
ask the right question

02:34:36.350 --> 02:34:38.480
at the right time.

02:34:38.480 --> 02:34:41.240
The other is people
will also tell you

02:34:41.240 --> 02:34:43.760
if you talk too much
about your ideas,

02:34:43.760 --> 02:34:46.100
someone will steal
them from you.

02:34:46.100 --> 02:34:47.990
Well, my response to
that is that if you

02:34:47.990 --> 02:34:50.960
have ideas that are
so easy to steal from,

02:34:50.960 --> 02:34:54.030
they must not be that good.

02:34:54.030 --> 02:34:56.760
In fact, my experience
is different.

02:34:56.760 --> 02:35:00.125
With truly original
ideas, the response

02:35:00.125 --> 02:35:03.185
is that most people
don't believe you.

02:35:03.185 --> 02:35:04.560
One of the three
or four things I

02:35:04.560 --> 02:35:09.090
did in my life that were
semi-original were fiercely

02:35:09.090 --> 02:35:13.650
disbelieved and criticized
and initially rejected

02:35:13.650 --> 02:35:18.150
for both publication, and most
importantly, NIH funding, which

02:35:18.150 --> 02:35:20.580
is the agency that
I direct today.

02:35:20.580 --> 02:35:23.130
So don't despair.

02:35:23.130 --> 02:35:26.350
I even carried this
further, this 50-50 rule

02:35:26.350 --> 02:35:28.980
further, because I
spent half of my life

02:35:28.980 --> 02:35:32.280
in our country and half of
my life in another country.

02:35:32.280 --> 02:35:35.100
I don't recommend you
push that to that extent.

02:35:35.100 --> 02:35:39.660
But as any rule that you
make for young colleagues

02:35:39.660 --> 02:35:43.320
that you talk to, there
are big exceptions.

02:35:43.320 --> 02:35:46.680
First, this rule doesn't mean
that you should develop a split

02:35:46.680 --> 02:35:48.360
personality.

02:35:48.360 --> 02:35:51.070
It shouldn't split
your integrity.

02:35:51.070 --> 02:35:54.670
Your integrity has
to be constant, 100%.

02:35:54.670 --> 02:35:58.740
Another one is that
in affairs of love,

02:35:58.740 --> 02:36:02.250
I don't think you should
play the 50-50 rule.

02:36:02.250 --> 02:36:05.800
That would be deadly,
so don't do it.

02:36:05.800 --> 02:36:08.130
And last but not
least, I would say

02:36:08.130 --> 02:36:15.840
you should have big dreams,
full dreams, not half dreams.

02:36:15.840 --> 02:36:17.350
It's very simple.

02:36:17.350 --> 02:36:19.580
You can't put a large
box in a small box.

02:36:22.180 --> 02:36:28.660
Well, you cannot put a full
life in a small dream box.

02:36:28.660 --> 02:36:33.430
What you need is to have a box,
a dream box, and a life that

02:36:33.430 --> 02:36:36.880
is as full as the
potential you have today.

02:36:36.880 --> 02:36:39.340
For universities and
teachers, there's

02:36:39.340 --> 02:36:41.890
just no greater
satisfaction than seeing

02:36:41.890 --> 02:36:46.000
you graduate and enter
your professional calling.

02:36:46.000 --> 02:36:48.970
I think you have the
potential to transform

02:36:48.970 --> 02:36:52.450
our understanding of the
relationship between humanity

02:36:52.450 --> 02:36:55.150
and environment this century.

02:36:55.150 --> 02:36:57.280
I think you need to do it.

02:36:57.280 --> 02:36:59.680
And you know something?

02:36:59.680 --> 02:37:02.920
There's nothing greater than
coming from a university

02:37:02.920 --> 02:37:06.560
like MIT to be able to do that.

02:37:06.560 --> 02:37:10.660
I actually read that
if you asked yourself

02:37:10.660 --> 02:37:14.890
about the 100 governments
that existed in 1900, how

02:37:14.890 --> 02:37:17.950
many of the 100 governments
that were active in 1900

02:37:17.950 --> 02:37:21.950
are still unchanged today?

02:37:21.950 --> 02:37:23.640
You know what the answer is?

02:37:23.640 --> 02:37:25.260
Two.

02:37:25.260 --> 02:37:28.930
There are only two governments
in the world that stayed stable

02:37:28.930 --> 02:37:31.180
for the past 104 years--

02:37:31.180 --> 02:37:34.300
the United States
and Great Britain.

02:37:34.300 --> 02:37:36.620
If you asked yourself
the question,

02:37:36.620 --> 02:37:38.740
what about universities?

02:37:38.740 --> 02:37:43.720
Well, let me ask you, if
you took the year 1500

02:37:43.720 --> 02:37:45.490
and you took the 100
universities that

02:37:45.490 --> 02:37:48.880
were active in 1500, how
many of them do you think

02:37:48.880 --> 02:37:52.820
have survived intact in 2004?

02:37:52.820 --> 02:37:57.070
The number is 75 out of 100.

02:37:57.070 --> 02:38:00.640
So what I can tell you
is that universities

02:38:00.640 --> 02:38:03.870
beat governments hands down.

02:38:03.870 --> 02:38:07.100
There is no institution that can
survive as long as a university

02:38:07.100 --> 02:38:10.450
if it's cared for by its
graduates and alumni.

02:38:10.450 --> 02:38:12.210
The only institutions
that last longer

02:38:12.210 --> 02:38:14.290
are the institutions
of the church.

02:38:14.290 --> 02:38:16.210
So I'm sure that
MIT will certainly

02:38:16.210 --> 02:38:19.360
be here at the end of the
century and many more centuries

02:38:19.360 --> 02:38:23.800
to come, thanks to you,
as newly minted graduates

02:38:23.800 --> 02:38:24.980
and future alumni.

02:38:24.980 --> 02:38:28.150
I understand the
Class of 1954 is here,

02:38:28.150 --> 02:38:30.170
and I want to salute
them for coming back

02:38:30.170 --> 02:38:31.296
to their institution.

02:38:31.296 --> 02:38:32.262
This is--

02:38:32.262 --> 02:38:35.643
[APPLAUSE]

02:38:39.520 --> 02:38:41.050
Because you know?

02:38:41.050 --> 02:38:45.130
We're all engaged throughout
the world on the global basis

02:38:45.130 --> 02:38:47.740
with a game that has no
frontier, a game that

02:38:47.740 --> 02:38:51.640
has no nationality,
and that is to build

02:38:51.640 --> 02:38:53.860
the fund of
knowledge of humanity

02:38:53.860 --> 02:38:55.930
to the service of humanity.

02:38:55.930 --> 02:38:57.370
This was my message.

02:38:57.370 --> 02:38:59.350
Believe in yourself.

02:38:59.350 --> 02:39:01.870
Life sciences are
a great challenge.

02:39:01.870 --> 02:39:05.230
We have a lot to do, and I hope
you'll join us in this fight.

02:39:05.230 --> 02:39:07.835
Good luck to you, and
God may bless you all.

02:39:07.835 --> 02:39:11.272
[APPLAUSE]

02:39:20.522 --> 02:39:21.980
PRESENTER: Thank
you, Dr. Zerhouni,

02:39:21.980 --> 02:39:26.170
for your wisdom
and your insights.

02:39:26.170 --> 02:39:29.470
And also thank you
for the great service

02:39:29.470 --> 02:39:31.900
that you're rendering
to our country

02:39:31.900 --> 02:39:36.880
with your leadership of the
National Institutes of Health.

02:39:36.880 --> 02:39:40.630
Now Mr. Erich Caulfield,
president of the Graduate

02:39:40.630 --> 02:39:45.220
Student Council, will
present a salute to MIT

02:39:45.220 --> 02:39:48.240
from the graduate student body.

02:39:48.240 --> 02:39:52.350
Following Mr. Caulfield,
Miss Maria Hidalgo,

02:39:52.350 --> 02:39:56.490
president of the senior class,
will present the class gift

02:39:56.490 --> 02:40:00.630
to President Vest, after which
the president will deliver

02:40:00.630 --> 02:40:03.000
his charge to the graduates.

02:40:21.980 --> 02:40:27.560
CAULFIELD: Good morning,
family and friends of MIT.

02:40:27.560 --> 02:40:30.980
From the dawn of the
human experiment,

02:40:30.980 --> 02:40:32.810
we have grappled
with the challenge

02:40:32.810 --> 02:40:36.710
and the potential
inherent in our existence.

02:40:36.710 --> 02:40:41.540
Today, my graduate student
brethren and my sistren,

02:40:41.540 --> 02:40:44.300
you join a grand procession that
has marched across the sands

02:40:44.300 --> 02:40:48.260
of time, marshaled by the
first human being who gazed up

02:40:48.260 --> 02:40:51.140
at the magnificent majesty
of a midnight sky and asked

02:40:51.140 --> 02:40:58.070
the question, what more can I
know, culminating at this time,

02:40:58.070 --> 02:41:02.960
at this place, at this junction
in the space-time continuum

02:41:02.960 --> 02:41:06.980
with you and this august
and awe-inspiring assembly

02:41:06.980 --> 02:41:09.720
of the academically
accomplished.

02:41:09.720 --> 02:41:12.800
For whether you are the first
in your family to attend college

02:41:12.800 --> 02:41:15.470
or of the fifth generation
to finish graduate school,

02:41:15.470 --> 02:41:17.300
you represent the
collective dreams

02:41:17.300 --> 02:41:19.970
of countless of your
comrades and ancestors,

02:41:19.970 --> 02:41:22.310
some no longer with
us, who have aspired

02:41:22.310 --> 02:41:26.270
for centuries to join with
you in this moment here today.

02:41:26.270 --> 02:41:29.630
For you have become the
embodiment of accomplishment,

02:41:29.630 --> 02:41:32.570
of drive, of passion, of
perseverance, of ambition,

02:41:32.570 --> 02:41:35.270
of strength, and hope.

02:41:35.270 --> 02:41:38.540
For in graduating today from
one of history's great wombs

02:41:38.540 --> 02:41:42.110
of wondrous ideas,
cradles of creativity,

02:41:42.110 --> 02:41:44.490
nursery for Nobel
Laureate, you prepare

02:41:44.490 --> 02:41:46.580
to take your place
amongst the great thinkers

02:41:46.580 --> 02:41:49.130
and movers of our time.

02:41:49.130 --> 02:41:54.110
For indeed, you have ascended
to the top of the Mount Olympus

02:41:54.110 --> 02:41:57.790
of the modern academic world.

02:41:57.790 --> 02:42:02.170
However, ascending to
the top and becoming

02:42:02.170 --> 02:42:04.510
the scholastic and
pedagogical gods and goddesses

02:42:04.510 --> 02:42:08.500
of your department
does not signify

02:42:08.500 --> 02:42:10.600
that your journey is over.

02:42:10.600 --> 02:42:13.060
But to the contrary,
it merely means

02:42:13.060 --> 02:42:14.830
that you have a
more excellent view

02:42:14.830 --> 02:42:16.890
of a future that is
pregnant with a plenitude

02:42:16.890 --> 02:42:18.360
of possibilities.

02:42:22.290 --> 02:42:24.710
And so now that
you've come to MIT

02:42:24.710 --> 02:42:27.050
and flexed your
magnificent mental muscles,

02:42:27.050 --> 02:42:29.410
made mighty by the methodical
mastery of mathematically

02:42:29.410 --> 02:42:31.190
menacing, scientifically
sophisticated,

02:42:31.190 --> 02:42:35.954
and econometrically intimidating
systems of equations and such--

02:42:35.954 --> 02:42:39.866
[LAUGHTER]

02:42:39.866 --> 02:42:43.289
[APPLAUSE]

02:42:45.740 --> 02:42:48.680
--you have proven that you
are now ready to leave,

02:42:48.680 --> 02:42:52.520
for you have
outgrown this place.

02:42:52.520 --> 02:42:56.570
When I take time to consider
the significance of this moment,

02:42:56.570 --> 02:42:59.300
I am compelled to ponder the
proclamations of the poet who

02:42:59.300 --> 02:43:03.290
confessed, "I'm tired of
sailing my little boat far

02:43:03.290 --> 02:43:04.910
inside the harbor bar.

02:43:04.910 --> 02:43:07.510
I want to go out where the
big ships float, on the deep

02:43:07.510 --> 02:43:09.080
where the great ones are.

02:43:09.080 --> 02:43:11.840
And should my frail
craft prove too slight

02:43:11.840 --> 02:43:14.182
for the waves that sweep
those billows o'er,

02:43:14.182 --> 02:43:16.640
I'd rather go down in a stirring
fight than drowse to death

02:43:16.640 --> 02:43:17.960
by the sheltered shore."

02:43:17.960 --> 02:43:19.820
For in the purest
sense, the poet

02:43:19.820 --> 02:43:24.440
captures the essence of
this experience exactly.

02:43:24.440 --> 02:43:30.110
For whether you've been here
for one year, or two years,

02:43:30.110 --> 02:43:39.530
or three years, or n
years, or n plus 1 years,

02:43:39.530 --> 02:43:41.750
in a career that will last
nearly a half-century,

02:43:41.750 --> 02:43:44.660
your greatest deeds
have yet to be done.

02:43:44.660 --> 02:43:46.640
And you can scarcely
imagine the impact

02:43:46.640 --> 02:43:51.380
that you'll have on society
and, indeed, on human history.

02:43:51.380 --> 02:43:54.290
And so I encourage you not only
to reflect on and appreciate

02:43:54.290 --> 02:43:57.140
the awesomeness of
your contributions thus

02:43:57.140 --> 02:44:01.280
far, but also to marvel at the
magnificence of future feats

02:44:01.280 --> 02:44:02.950
yet to come.

02:44:02.950 --> 02:44:05.540
And so now as you prepare to
set sail for the great world

02:44:05.540 --> 02:44:08.570
outside of MIT, it is my
hope that triumph will

02:44:08.570 --> 02:44:10.130
be your ever present companion.

02:44:10.130 --> 02:44:11.980
May passion fuel
your every endeavor.

02:44:11.980 --> 02:44:14.410
May innovation infect
your every invention.

02:44:14.410 --> 02:44:16.490
And may a preternatural
propensity for progress

02:44:16.490 --> 02:44:19.310
pepper your every project.

02:44:19.310 --> 02:44:22.230
And may a blazing torch of
morality light your path.

02:44:22.230 --> 02:44:23.810
And as you go
forth, I would urge

02:44:23.810 --> 02:44:26.570
you to stand boldly at
the great helm of history

02:44:26.570 --> 02:44:29.540
and steer a course such that
future generations will say,

02:44:29.540 --> 02:44:37.520
with passionate regard,
that you graduated from MIT,

02:44:37.520 --> 02:44:42.850
and the world is a better
place because of it.

02:44:42.850 --> 02:44:45.585
And so, you daughters
and sons of MIT,

02:44:45.585 --> 02:44:47.460
it is with the greatest
of pride and elation,

02:44:47.460 --> 02:44:49.560
heartfelt joy and
awestruck admiration,

02:44:49.560 --> 02:44:52.920
and the deepest respect that, on
behalf of the Graduate Student

02:44:52.920 --> 02:44:57.920
Council, I bid thee
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

02:45:24.710 --> 02:45:28.767
In other words, congratulations
on an outstanding display

02:45:28.767 --> 02:45:31.506
of academic magnificence.

02:45:31.506 --> 02:45:34.992
[APPLAUSE]

02:45:47.450 --> 02:45:52.730
Go ye forth, gentle geniuses--

02:45:52.730 --> 02:45:54.630
[LAUGHTER]

02:45:54.630 --> 02:46:00.430
--history is waiting
to hear from you.

02:46:00.430 --> 02:46:01.420
Thank you.

02:46:01.420 --> 02:46:04.390
[APPLAUSE]

02:46:28.150 --> 02:46:30.250
HIDALGO: Good morning.

02:46:30.250 --> 02:46:32.711
Everyone here expects
a lot from us.

02:46:32.711 --> 02:46:34.960
This is because they think
we have a lot to contribute

02:46:34.960 --> 02:46:37.210
to the world, and we do.

02:46:37.210 --> 02:46:38.620
But you know what?

02:46:38.620 --> 02:46:40.870
I'll bet we expect a
lot more from ourselves,

02:46:40.870 --> 02:46:43.750
because we know just
how much we can give.

02:46:43.750 --> 02:46:45.250
Just look at what
we've accomplished

02:46:45.250 --> 02:46:47.530
in only these past few years.

02:46:47.530 --> 02:46:49.750
It's exhausting
to think about it.

02:46:49.750 --> 02:46:52.240
We're tired, and the
overall sentiment

02:46:52.240 --> 02:46:54.940
is that it feels
so good to be done.

02:46:54.940 --> 02:46:56.890
But we aren't finished.

02:46:56.890 --> 02:47:01.270
We may be done here at MIT,
but we're only getting started.

02:47:01.270 --> 02:47:03.010
We have so much to contribute.

02:47:03.010 --> 02:47:04.870
Today can't be the end.

02:47:04.870 --> 02:47:08.740
Today is merely a pause,
an opportunity for us

02:47:08.740 --> 02:47:11.050
to catch our breath
and reflect on our most

02:47:11.050 --> 02:47:13.100
recent accomplishments.

02:47:13.100 --> 02:47:16.180
Tomorrow, we'll continue
down our individual paths

02:47:16.180 --> 02:47:19.990
and join thousands of others
whose achievements have changed

02:47:19.990 --> 02:47:22.880
and will change society forever.

02:47:22.880 --> 02:47:27.000
We will become MIT alumni.

02:47:27.000 --> 02:47:29.690
And now, as a symbol
of our graduation,

02:47:29.690 --> 02:47:33.770
we remove our brass
rats, and we turn

02:47:33.770 --> 02:47:37.490
them to face outward at the
world we are about to join.

02:47:40.350 --> 02:47:45.540
And as we leave MIT,
we, the Class of 2004,

02:47:45.540 --> 02:47:49.120
leave behind our gift
to the Institute.

02:47:49.120 --> 02:47:51.550
Dr. Vest, it is a
particular honor

02:47:51.550 --> 02:47:54.160
to present this
year's class gift.

02:47:54.160 --> 02:47:57.220
Those of us in the Class of
2004 are aware that you, too,

02:47:57.220 --> 02:48:00.520
will soon be graduating, moving
on from your current role

02:48:00.520 --> 02:48:02.890
as president of MIT.

02:48:02.890 --> 02:48:05.620
We salute you for all you've
done for the Institute,

02:48:05.620 --> 02:48:07.450
and we wish you
all the best as you

02:48:07.450 --> 02:48:10.870
move into the next stage
of your life and career.

02:48:10.870 --> 02:48:14.470
At this time, it is my
privilege to present this gift

02:48:14.470 --> 02:48:18.160
on behalf of the
graduating Class of 2004.

02:48:18.160 --> 02:48:20.230
The HUGE Fund--

02:48:20.230 --> 02:48:23.470
HUGE stands for Helping
Undergraduates Gain

02:48:23.470 --> 02:48:24.730
Excellence--

02:48:24.730 --> 02:48:26.470
will provide
budgetary assistance

02:48:26.470 --> 02:48:29.650
to undergraduate students who
require additional funding

02:48:29.650 --> 02:48:31.580
for major projects.

02:48:31.580 --> 02:48:33.940
This includes additional
research funding

02:48:33.940 --> 02:48:38.410
for UROPs, theses, and
other class projects.

02:48:38.410 --> 02:48:49.892
So far, 29.3% of the Class of
2004 has raised $31,736.67.

02:48:49.892 --> 02:48:53.259
[APPLAUSE]

02:48:58.965 --> 02:49:03.360
283 members of our class
have donated thus far,

02:49:03.360 --> 02:49:07.000
and we only need seven
more gifts to get to 30%.

02:49:07.000 --> 02:49:10.113
So if anyone out there has $10
bills, wave them up in the air,

02:49:10.113 --> 02:49:11.488
and we'll be by
with a collection

02:49:11.488 --> 02:49:12.916
basket in a few moments.

02:49:16.248 --> 02:49:19.420
And last, I would just like
to say, for lack of a better

02:49:19.420 --> 02:49:21.820
word, congratulations.

02:49:21.820 --> 02:49:25.532
Enjoy today and
enjoy the weather.

02:49:25.532 --> 02:49:29.004
[APPLAUSE]

02:49:36.470 --> 02:49:40.550
VEST: Thank you, Miss
Hidalgo and Mr. Caulfield.

02:49:40.550 --> 02:49:44.410
I am most grateful for
your words and your gifts,

02:49:44.410 --> 02:49:48.140
but especially for all that
you and your fellow students

02:49:48.140 --> 02:49:52.390
have meant to this
great institution.

02:49:52.390 --> 02:49:57.090
I thought long and hard about
what my parting message to you

02:49:57.090 --> 02:50:01.780
should be, and it is this.

02:50:01.780 --> 02:50:03.640
Thank goodness I
will never again

02:50:03.640 --> 02:50:06.340
have to speak after
Erich Caulfield.

02:50:06.340 --> 02:50:09.742
[LAUGHTER]

02:50:09.742 --> 02:50:13.144
[APPLAUSE]

02:50:14.620 --> 02:50:19.960
Be that as it may,
here we are again,

02:50:19.960 --> 02:50:23.650
gathered in Killian Court
to celebrate accomplishment,

02:50:23.650 --> 02:50:26.250
heritage, and passage.

02:50:26.250 --> 02:50:29.800
It is a major passage
in life for you,

02:50:29.800 --> 02:50:33.430
and it is a major
life passage for me.

02:50:33.430 --> 02:50:35.470
Together we end
an important phase

02:50:35.470 --> 02:50:40.520
of our lives and education
and commence a new chapter.

02:50:40.520 --> 02:50:43.110
It may perhaps seem
odd that a community

02:50:43.110 --> 02:50:46.850
so dedicated to the future
gathers here wearing

02:50:46.850 --> 02:50:51.320
strange and colorful
medieval regalia.

02:50:51.320 --> 02:50:55.780
But it is fitting and seemingly
fulfilling of deep human needs

02:50:55.780 --> 02:50:59.060
that such rituals take place.

02:50:59.060 --> 02:51:01.940
This ritual reminds
us of the continuity

02:51:01.940 --> 02:51:07.520
through the ages of our role
in an unbroken, centuries-old

02:51:07.520 --> 02:51:11.480
chain of human discovery
and accomplishment.

02:51:11.480 --> 02:51:15.440
But above all, it celebrates
your accomplishments

02:51:15.440 --> 02:51:18.630
during your student years.

02:51:18.630 --> 02:51:21.120
This is not to say that
you have accomplished

02:51:21.120 --> 02:51:23.240
the remarkable
feat of graduating

02:51:23.240 --> 02:51:26.780
from MIT all on your own.

02:51:26.780 --> 02:51:32.600
We are indeed surrounded by
parents, family, friends,

02:51:32.600 --> 02:51:36.740
spouses, partners,
and children who

02:51:36.740 --> 02:51:40.780
have supported and sustained
you through the years.

02:51:40.780 --> 02:51:43.940
You will recognize them
today by the smiles brought

02:51:43.940 --> 02:51:47.510
about by their great pride
in your accomplishments

02:51:47.510 --> 02:51:50.210
and, no doubt, by a
great sense of relief

02:51:50.210 --> 02:51:52.660
to their bank accounts.

02:51:52.660 --> 02:51:57.730
Let us then, yet again,
express our deep appreciation

02:51:57.730 --> 02:52:00.550
to all who have come
to Cambridge today

02:52:00.550 --> 02:52:03.398
to join you in your
Commencement ceremony.

02:52:03.398 --> 02:52:04.370
Thank your parents.

02:52:04.370 --> 02:52:07.772
[APPLAUSE]

02:52:14.110 --> 02:52:18.070
It is also especially
wonderful to see

02:52:18.070 --> 02:52:20.440
the babies and
small children who

02:52:20.440 --> 02:52:24.670
have come to see their
mothers and fathers graduate.

02:52:24.670 --> 02:52:26.920
They, too, are welcome.

02:52:26.920 --> 02:52:29.770
And as this ceremony
stretches onward,

02:52:29.770 --> 02:52:32.950
I give them special
presidential approval

02:52:32.950 --> 02:52:37.500
to comment on the proceedings
at any time and in any manner

02:52:37.500 --> 02:52:38.450
they see fit.

02:52:42.260 --> 02:52:45.770
Each of the last
13 years, I have

02:52:45.770 --> 02:52:47.960
had the honor of
briefly addressing

02:52:47.960 --> 02:52:51.730
those gathered for MIT's
Commencement ceremony.

02:52:51.730 --> 02:52:55.520
And each year, I have
concluded with the same charge,

02:52:55.520 --> 02:52:58.400
the same brief statement
of advice and challenge

02:52:58.400 --> 02:53:00.530
to our graduates.

02:53:00.530 --> 02:53:03.410
Before I do so yet
again, I want to explain

02:53:03.410 --> 02:53:06.350
what the elements of
that charge mean to me

02:53:06.350 --> 02:53:09.860
and why I think they
are important for you,

02:53:09.860 --> 02:53:11.780
as graduates of
the Massachusetts

02:53:11.780 --> 02:53:15.110
Institute of Technology.

02:53:15.110 --> 02:53:19.310
First, you should
ponder the unthinkable.

02:53:19.310 --> 02:53:22.850
You each have completed an
intense, rigorous education

02:53:22.850 --> 02:53:27.200
with a strong scientific
or quantitative bent.

02:53:27.200 --> 02:53:30.470
This is true regardless of the
intellectual field in which you

02:53:30.470 --> 02:53:33.725
concentrated and regardless
of the level of degree

02:53:33.725 --> 02:53:36.390
you are receiving today.

02:53:36.390 --> 02:53:39.450
Armed with this
education and experience,

02:53:39.450 --> 02:53:42.720
you have an opportunity
and a capability

02:53:42.720 --> 02:53:47.820
to ponder ancient questions
in new ways and also to think,

02:53:47.820 --> 02:53:52.010
of course, about new
challenges in new ways.

02:53:52.010 --> 02:53:54.010
The world in all
of its dimensions

02:53:54.010 --> 02:53:57.610
is evolving at an
ever increasing rate.

02:53:57.610 --> 02:54:02.285
You have an unprecedented
opportunity to expand knowledge

02:54:02.285 --> 02:54:05.780
and to create wealth and jobs.

02:54:05.780 --> 02:54:09.410
At the same time, there
is a tragic expansion

02:54:09.410 --> 02:54:15.390
of misery, inhumanity,
and illness in our world.

02:54:15.390 --> 02:54:17.520
And it will take new
vision, new thinking,

02:54:17.520 --> 02:54:21.570
and new levels of determination
to alleviate these problems

02:54:21.570 --> 02:54:25.170
and create a more civil
and humane society.

02:54:25.170 --> 02:54:27.060
In other words, to
make a difference

02:54:27.060 --> 02:54:29.160
in the condition
of the world, you

02:54:29.160 --> 02:54:33.750
must start by pondering
the seemingly unthinkable.

02:54:33.750 --> 02:54:36.660
Is it unthinkable that we
might truly be dedicated

02:54:36.660 --> 02:54:39.000
to sustainable development?

02:54:39.000 --> 02:54:42.660
That is, to using our energy
and material resources

02:54:42.660 --> 02:54:46.660
in a way that not only respects
the natural environment,

02:54:46.660 --> 02:54:49.050
but offers the
developing world a chance

02:54:49.050 --> 02:54:52.470
to reach the same levels
of health and well-being

02:54:52.470 --> 02:54:54.540
that we enjoy?

02:54:54.540 --> 02:54:57.210
Is it unthinkable
that we should find

02:54:57.210 --> 02:54:59.310
ways of cutting
through the increasing

02:54:59.310 --> 02:55:04.160
clash of multiple, unmovable
fundamentalist views

02:55:04.160 --> 02:55:08.070
and get to the core of
humanity beneath them?

02:55:08.070 --> 02:55:09.630
Is it unthinkable
that we could at

02:55:09.630 --> 02:55:12.150
last truly bridge,
once and for all,

02:55:12.150 --> 02:55:14.660
the racial divides
in this country

02:55:14.660 --> 02:55:17.730
and see that our best
hope for the future

02:55:17.730 --> 02:55:22.130
rests in pulling together
rather than pulling apart?

02:55:22.130 --> 02:55:24.260
That's the first challenge--

02:55:24.260 --> 02:55:26.510
think the unthinkable.

02:55:26.510 --> 02:55:30.530
A corollary to that is to
question the status quo.

02:55:30.530 --> 02:55:33.620
If we are to advance
the condition of society

02:55:33.620 --> 02:55:39.440
to create what could be, we
must first question what is.

02:55:39.440 --> 02:55:42.650
By definition, advancing
means changing and moving

02:55:42.650 --> 02:55:46.220
in more positive, more
productive directions.

02:55:46.220 --> 02:55:48.230
The status quo,
therefore, will not

02:55:48.230 --> 02:55:51.230
correct the inequities
and ineffectiveness

02:55:51.230 --> 02:55:54.980
that are rampant in so many of
America's primary and secondary

02:55:54.980 --> 02:55:56.360
schools.

02:55:56.360 --> 02:56:00.620
The status quo will not stem
the tide of AIDS in Africa

02:56:00.620 --> 02:56:03.770
or vanquish the now
constant threat of terrorism

02:56:03.770 --> 02:56:07.810
in every corner of the
world, including our corner.

02:56:07.810 --> 02:56:11.470
But as MIT graduates, as
educated men and women,

02:56:11.470 --> 02:56:13.660
you have a responsibility
to question

02:56:13.660 --> 02:56:19.330
the status quo in an
informed, fact-based manner.

02:56:19.330 --> 02:56:22.900
It is your passion and
compassion that will drive you.

02:56:22.900 --> 02:56:24.790
But to make a
difference, you also

02:56:24.790 --> 02:56:27.760
need clear eyes and
heads and the ability

02:56:27.760 --> 02:56:31.750
to objectively analyze the
problems you care about.

02:56:31.750 --> 02:56:34.300
That is what can set you apart.

02:56:34.300 --> 02:56:36.490
That is what gives
you the opportunity

02:56:36.490 --> 02:56:40.840
not only to ponder, but also
to act, to dedicate your lives

02:56:40.840 --> 02:56:45.880
and work to important,
worthwhile things.

02:56:45.880 --> 02:56:48.880
And when you think about
the things you want to do,

02:56:48.880 --> 02:56:52.750
consider the entire world as
your field of opportunity.

02:56:52.750 --> 02:56:54.400
In other words,
live in the world

02:56:54.400 --> 02:56:57.130
as well as in your own nation.

02:56:57.130 --> 02:57:00.880
You are citizens of the world as
well as citizens of the United

02:57:00.880 --> 02:57:06.400
States or China or Iran or
Italy or wherever you were born

02:57:06.400 --> 02:57:08.410
or gained citizenship.

02:57:08.410 --> 02:57:12.610
Each of you shares the Earth
with every other human being.

02:57:12.610 --> 02:57:16.630
In this troubled time,
there is a continual tension

02:57:16.630 --> 02:57:20.080
between fragmentation
and integration.

02:57:20.080 --> 02:57:22.360
The very electronic
communications

02:57:22.360 --> 02:57:25.600
that we developed here
at MIT connects us

02:57:25.600 --> 02:57:29.900
to each other as never
before in human history.

02:57:29.900 --> 02:57:32.140
But simultaneously,
we seem to be

02:57:32.140 --> 02:57:37.270
fragmenting into increasingly
more isolated geographical,

02:57:37.270 --> 02:57:42.130
economic, political, religious,
or cultural enclaves.

02:57:42.130 --> 02:57:44.470
We stare at each
other with suspicion

02:57:44.470 --> 02:57:46.600
rather than with welcoming.

02:57:46.600 --> 02:57:49.660
We accuse rather
than enlightening.

02:57:49.660 --> 02:57:54.370
We raise walls rather than
open doors and open minds.

02:57:54.370 --> 02:57:57.700
Integration, mutual respect,
and mutual understanding

02:57:57.700 --> 02:58:03.830
must, in the end, win out over
fragmentation and isolation.

02:58:03.830 --> 02:58:06.620
But that will not
happen by default.

02:58:06.620 --> 02:58:10.550
That will happen only if we
use our minds and our actions

02:58:10.550 --> 02:58:14.810
to create a spirit and
a reality of openness.

02:58:14.810 --> 02:58:18.890
The cornerstone on which great
American research universities

02:58:18.890 --> 02:58:21.450
are built is openness--

02:58:21.450 --> 02:58:24.710
openness of our national
boundaries and openness

02:58:24.710 --> 02:58:28.710
of our campuses to immigrants,
visitors, students, faculty,

02:58:28.710 --> 02:58:32.690
and scholarly colleagues
from all over the world.

02:58:32.690 --> 02:58:37.280
MIT, I'm proud to say,
stands as a prime example.

02:58:37.280 --> 02:58:40.460
Our MIT Nobel
Laureates were born

02:58:40.460 --> 02:58:44.460
in the United States,
Mexico, Germany, Italy,

02:58:44.460 --> 02:58:46.850
Japan, and India.

02:58:46.850 --> 02:58:49.340
Even our responsibility
to protect

02:58:49.340 --> 02:58:53.090
those who live and learn
and work on our campus

02:58:53.090 --> 02:58:55.790
and in these United
States, protect them

02:58:55.790 --> 02:58:59.210
from the reality of
terrorism, must not

02:58:59.210 --> 02:59:03.620
undermine this enriching
and empowering openness.

02:59:03.620 --> 02:59:07.910
MIT will continue to work
aggressively and effectively

02:59:07.910 --> 02:59:11.000
with our federal government
to develop and implement

02:59:11.000 --> 02:59:14.150
sound policies that
will keep our colleges

02:59:14.150 --> 02:59:17.630
and our universities open
to international students

02:59:17.630 --> 02:59:21.920
and scholars and that will
allow open and productive

02:59:21.920 --> 02:59:25.520
scientific communication
and collaboration

02:59:25.520 --> 02:59:28.220
among international colleagues.

02:59:28.220 --> 02:59:33.500
I ask that each of you join
us in that important mission.

02:59:33.500 --> 02:59:36.230
But world citizenship
extends beyond

02:59:36.230 --> 02:59:39.290
these important concerns.

02:59:39.290 --> 02:59:42.500
It extends to a deep
understanding of the fact

02:59:42.500 --> 02:59:47.000
that we all inhabit the same
Earth, that our every action

02:59:47.000 --> 02:59:50.640
affects people in far-flung
parts of the world,

02:59:50.640 --> 02:59:54.330
and that their actions,
in turn, affect us.

02:59:54.330 --> 02:59:58.490
We must not be blind to
evil or to real threats,

02:59:58.490 --> 03:00:00.550
and they must be countered.

03:00:00.550 --> 03:00:05.120
But they are best countered
by making common cause.

03:00:05.120 --> 03:00:10.520
Force has its place, but so
do humility and the forging

03:00:10.520 --> 03:00:13.190
of collective efforts
based on shared

03:00:13.190 --> 03:00:16.730
values and cultural reality.

03:00:16.730 --> 03:00:19.520
Share your talents.

03:00:19.520 --> 03:00:22.280
The best way to forge
collective efforts

03:00:22.280 --> 03:00:25.790
is to share your talents,
and they are legion.

03:00:25.790 --> 03:00:28.490
And you have honed many
of them in important ways

03:00:28.490 --> 03:00:30.950
during your days at MIT.

03:00:30.950 --> 03:00:33.830
But they are not yours alone.

03:00:33.830 --> 03:00:38.930
Society is entrusted to
talented people such as you.

03:00:38.930 --> 03:00:44.330
There is a world to feed,
energy to be provided,

03:00:44.330 --> 03:00:47.150
natural resources to
be used efficiently

03:00:47.150 --> 03:00:53.090
and wisely, human communication
and learning to be improved.

03:00:53.090 --> 03:00:58.500
Good health and security need
to be spread across the lands.

03:00:58.500 --> 03:01:04.200
And this can only happen by
your sharing your talents.

03:01:04.200 --> 03:01:07.230
Commune with all people.

03:01:07.230 --> 03:01:10.290
Now, sharing is both
easier and more effective

03:01:10.290 --> 03:01:13.020
if you can see yourself as
part of the greater world

03:01:13.020 --> 03:01:17.040
community, embracing all
the variety and richness

03:01:17.040 --> 03:01:20.700
that the different
cultures have to offer.

03:01:20.700 --> 03:01:25.650
Every survey we make of
MIT students and graduates

03:01:25.650 --> 03:01:29.790
shows that you greatly
value the diversity

03:01:29.790 --> 03:01:33.630
of friends and colleagues
you got to know here.

03:01:33.630 --> 03:01:36.590
In all that you do, you
should be steady friends

03:01:36.590 --> 03:01:38.490
and bold companions.

03:01:38.490 --> 03:01:42.180
Friendship, in both
personal and global sense,

03:01:42.180 --> 03:01:46.110
cannot come or go at
the wisp of a moment.

03:01:46.110 --> 03:01:49.880
There is both beauty
and practicality

03:01:49.880 --> 03:01:53.920
in enduring companionship
and dialogue.

03:01:53.920 --> 03:01:56.340
It is on such
enduring friendships

03:01:56.340 --> 03:01:59.430
that productive
societies are built.

03:01:59.430 --> 03:02:01.710
And it is from
such relationships

03:02:01.710 --> 03:02:04.740
as they continue through
the changes of time

03:02:04.740 --> 03:02:08.880
and changes of events
that wisdom comes.

03:02:08.880 --> 03:02:12.150
But be bold companions as well.

03:02:12.150 --> 03:02:14.290
Progress comes
from such boldness

03:02:14.290 --> 03:02:17.460
in the context of common
values and aspirations.

03:02:20.080 --> 03:02:24.740
And so as you embark on the
next stage of your journey,

03:02:24.740 --> 03:02:27.860
I offer this now
traditional charge

03:02:27.860 --> 03:02:31.730
to you, the graduates of MIT.

03:02:31.730 --> 03:02:34.700
Ponder the unthinkable.

03:02:34.700 --> 03:02:37.550
Question the status quo.

03:02:37.550 --> 03:02:41.840
Live in the world as well
as in your own nation.

03:02:41.840 --> 03:02:46.430
Dream of a better future, but
contribute to the present.

03:02:46.430 --> 03:02:49.100
Share your talents.

03:02:49.100 --> 03:02:51.740
Commune with all people.

03:02:51.740 --> 03:02:55.490
Be steady friends
and bold companions.

03:02:55.490 --> 03:02:59.750
Address the truly important
issues of your time.

03:02:59.750 --> 03:03:03.080
Be honest in all that you do.

03:03:03.080 --> 03:03:07.550
Take your education, your
talent, and your energy

03:03:07.550 --> 03:03:10.550
and build a nation and
a world community that

03:03:10.550 --> 03:03:14.870
consider knowledge
a gift to be shared,

03:03:14.870 --> 03:03:18.430
a healthy planet a
place to be cherished,

03:03:18.430 --> 03:03:22.040
and human dignity
and opportunity

03:03:22.040 --> 03:03:27.200
fundamental conditions to
be enjoyed by all people.

03:03:27.200 --> 03:03:32.209
Men and women of MIT, I wish
you godspeed and the very

03:03:32.209 --> 03:03:34.432
best of good fortune.

03:03:34.432 --> 03:03:37.890
[APPLAUSE]

03:03:46.790 --> 03:03:49.340
PRESENTER: Thank
you, President Vest.

03:03:49.340 --> 03:03:54.050
And thank you and Becky for
your dedicated and inspiring

03:03:54.050 --> 03:03:58.645
leadership of MIT during
the past 14 years.

03:03:58.645 --> 03:04:02.110
[APPLAUSE]

03:04:42.280 --> 03:04:44.680
By virtue of the authority
delegated to them

03:04:44.680 --> 03:04:46.750
by the Corporation
of the Massachusetts

03:04:46.750 --> 03:04:50.810
Institute of Technology
and on the recommendation

03:04:50.810 --> 03:04:54.200
of the faculty,
President Vest will now

03:04:54.200 --> 03:04:57.890
present the following degrees--

03:04:57.890 --> 03:05:04.790
Bachelor of Science, Bachelor
of Science/Master of Science,

03:05:04.790 --> 03:05:11.230
Bachelor of Science/Master
of Engineering,

03:05:11.230 --> 03:05:13.960
and the advanced
degrees for the School

03:05:13.960 --> 03:05:18.670
of Science and Whitaker
College of Health Sciences

03:05:18.670 --> 03:05:21.340
and Technology.

03:05:21.340 --> 03:05:24.340
Provost Brown will present
the advanced degrees

03:05:24.340 --> 03:05:27.910
for the School of
Architecture and Planning,

03:05:27.910 --> 03:05:32.140
the School of Engineering, the
School of Humanities, Arts,

03:05:32.140 --> 03:05:37.954
and Social Sciences, and for
the Sloan School of Management.

03:05:37.954 --> 03:05:41.906
[CHEERS]

03:05:41.906 --> 03:05:42.820
Thank you.

03:05:45.650 --> 03:05:49.400
As they approach the ramp,
undergraduate degree recipients

03:05:49.400 --> 03:05:52.560
will be greeted
by the Chancellor,

03:05:52.560 --> 03:05:56.180
the Dean for Student Life,
and the Dean for Graduate

03:05:56.180 --> 03:05:57.050
Education--

03:05:57.050 --> 03:05:59.710
Undergraduate Education.

03:05:59.710 --> 03:06:05.820
Graduate degree recipients will
be greeted by the school deans.

03:06:05.820 --> 03:06:08.370
The first graduates
to be recognized

03:06:08.370 --> 03:06:12.015
are the class marshals who
are seated on the stage.

03:06:15.859 --> 03:06:17.400
[? PRESENTER: ?]
Recognition will now

03:06:17.400 --> 03:06:21.210
be given to the officers
of the Class of 2004

03:06:21.210 --> 03:06:24.020
and the officers of the
Graduate Student Council who

03:06:24.020 --> 03:06:27.100
